export const rawHelpDocs: Record<string, string> = {
  "administration.md": "---\ntitle: 'Administration'\ngroup: 'Administration'\norder: 170\nroutes: ['/configurations', '/catalogs', '/tenants', '/facilities', '/users', '/staff', '/spaces', '/rbac', '/plugin-management']\ndescription: 'Tenant and facility context, configurations and feature switches, ABDM settings, clinical catalogs, tenants, facilities, users, staff, spaces, roles and plugins.'\n---\n\nAdministration is where you set up the organisation the rest of the app runs on: which features are\nswitched on, the master data clinicians pick from, who can sign in, which facilities they work in,\nand what their roles allow.\n\nYou reach it two ways:\n\n- The sidebar's **Administration** group, which is always shown: **Configurations**, **ABDM Settings**,\n  **Clinical Catalogs**, **RCM Setup** and **PE Setup**.\n- The **Administration** menu behind the gear icon in the topbar: **Tenants**, **Facilities**, **Users**,\n  **Staff**, **Spaces**, **RBAC** and, below a divider, **Plugin Management**. These screens are not in\n  the sidebar, so the gear menu is the way in.\n\n**RCM Setup** is explained in [Billing setup](billing-setup.md) and **PE Setup** in\n[Patient engagement](patient-engagement.md#pe-setup).\n\n## Tenant, facility and what they change\n\nA **tenant** is your organisation as a whole: a clinic group or hospital network. A **facility** is\none site within it. Everything you do is done as one user, in one tenant, in one facility at a time.\nYour current facility is shown under your name at the foot of the sidebar.\n\nEvery request the app makes to the server carries your tenant and current facility. So the facility\nyou are in decides:\n\n- which records you see and which you create: patients, encounters, orders and bills are written\n  against the current facility;\n- which **Spaces** are listed (the [Spaces](#spaces) screen shows only the current facility's);\n- which configuration values apply, because a facility can override a tenant setting (see\n  [Configurations](#configurations));\n- and so which sidebar sections you see, because those are switched on by configuration.\n\n### Switching facility\n\n1. Open the user menu (the person icon at the right of the topbar). It shows your email and your first\n   role, as **Role: _name_**, or **No roles assigned**.\n2. Under **Switch Facility**, pick a facility. Each lists its type; your home facility is marked\n   **(Home)** and the current one has a tick and cannot be picked.\n3. The app asks the server for a new sign-in token for that facility, says **Facility switched**\n   (\"You are now working in the selected facility.\"), and reloads the page.\n\n**Check:** the facility name at the foot of the sidebar has changed.\n\n**If instead:** the **Switch Facility** section is missing, you have access to only one facility. Ask\nan administrator to grant more on your user record ([Facility access](#facility-access)). If the server\nrefuses, the screen says **Unable to switch facility** with its reason.\n\n### What controls the sidebar\n\nSidebar sections are switched on by **feature switches**, not by roles. A section such as Wellness,\nMembership, Pharmacy, Inpatient Care, Revenue Cycle, Results & Reporting, Patient Engagement or\nAnalytics appears only when its `feature.nav.*` setting is on for your tenant (or facility). A\nspecialty plugin such as Oncology adds its section when it is activated for the tenant. The core\nsections (Patient Access, Clinical, Operating Theatre, Billing and Administration) are always shown.\n\n**Roles do not hide sidebar items.** Your roles and their permissions are checked by the server when\nyou load or save something. If a role does not allow an action, the screen still opens but the server\nrefuses, and you see the screen's own failure message (for example, saving an ABDM credential\nwithout the `secret.manage` permission). Ask an administrator to change your roles on\n[Users](#users).\n\n## Configurations\n\n**System Configurations** (`/configurations`) lists every setting the platform has, grouped into\ncards by category, with a search box (**Search configurations...**) that matches the key, the\ndescription or the category.\n\nThe rule for which value wins is shown at the top of the first tab: **facility overrides take\nprecedence over tenant overrides, which take precedence over instance defaults.** The instance default\nis the platform-wide value; a tenant override is your organisation's own value.\n\nFour tabs:\n\n- **Effective Configuration**: the value actually in use for you, with a **Tenant Override** or\n  **Facility Override** badge where one applies and the **Instance Default** shown underneath for\n  comparison.\n- **Instance Level**: the platform defaults.\n- **Tenant Overrides**: only the settings your tenant has changed, each with **Reset to default**.\n  Says \"No tenant-specific overrides configured\" when there are none.\n- **Facility Overrides**: the current facility's overrides. Read-only here.\n\nEach setting shows its key, its type (boolean, string, number, json), and badges:\n\n| Badge | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Overridable** | Your tenant may set its own value. Only these can be edited. |\n| **Sensitive** (red) | The value is hidden as dots and cannot be edited here. |\n| **Tenant Override** | Your tenant has its own value. |\n| **Facility Override** | The current facility has its own value, which wins. |\n\nUnder each value: when it was last **Updated** and **By** whom (a shortened user id).\n\n### Changing a setting\n\nAll changes made here are saved as a **tenant override**: they apply to every facility in your tenant\nthat has no facility override of its own.\n\n- **On/off settings**: flip the switch. It says **Saving...**, then **Enabled** or **Disabled**. The\n  switch is greyed out when a facility override is in force, because the facility value would win\n  anyway.\n- **Text and number settings**: press **Edit**, type the new value, then press the tick (or Enter).\n  The cross (or Escape) cancels.\n- **JSON settings** show their value but have no **Edit** button.\n- **Reset** (or **Reset to default** on the Tenant Overrides tab) deletes your tenant override, so the\n  instance default applies again.\n\nThe footer counts the total settings, tenant overrides and facility overrides.\n\n### Switching a module on\n\nThe sidebar's optional sections are settings in the **features** category, all off by default:\n\n| Setting | Sidebar section |\n| --- | --- |\n| `feature.nav.resultsReporting` | Lab, and Results & Reporting |\n| `feature.nav.inpatientCare` | Inpatient Care |\n| `feature.nav.revenueCycle` | Revenue Cycle |\n| `feature.nav.pharmacy` | Pharmacy |\n| `feature.nav.prm` | Patient Engagement |\n| `feature.nav.analytics` | Analytics |\n| `feature.nav.wellness` | Wellness |\n| `feature.nav.membership` | Membership |\n\n1. On **Effective Configuration**, search for `feature.nav`.\n2. Flip the switch for the section.\n\n**Check:** the sidebar shows (or drops) the section. Other users see the change the next time their\napp reloads its settings, within about five minutes, or straight away if they refresh.\n\nPlugin sections such as Oncology are switched from [Plugin Management](#plugin-management) instead.\n\n## ABDM Settings\n\n**ABDM Settings** (`/configurations/abdm`) sets up the Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission connection, which\nlets you create and link patients' ABHA numbers. It has three cards.\n\n### Activation\n\nA switch reading **ABDM is enabled for this tenant** or **ABDM is disabled for this tenant**. The card\nalso shows the **Environment** the connection points at (for example `sandbox`). Offering ABHA to\npatients needs this switch on **and** working credentials below.\n\n### Gateway credentials\n\nThe credentials are **write-only**: they are encrypted when saved and can never be shown again, only\nreplaced.\n\n1. Choose the **Credential scope**. In ABDM each facility is its own health information provider, so\n   credentials are usually stored per facility. Choose **Tenant-wide (all facilities)** for a single\n   registration covering the whole tenant.\n2. For **ABDM Client ID** and **ABDM Client Secret**, type the value and press **Save**.\n\nEach credential shows **Not configured**, or **Configured · updated _date_** once saved. A **Saved** badge\nflashes for a few seconds after a save. When one is configured the button reads **Rotate**: type a new\nvalue and press it to replace the old one.\n\n**If instead:** the screen says \"Could not save the credential — check your permissions (secret.manage)\nand try again.\" Your role lacks the permission to manage secrets.\n\n### Gateway health\n\nPress **Check now** to run a real handshake with the ABDM gateway using the stored credentials. The\nresult badge:\n\n| Badge | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| **OK** | The gateway accepted the credentials. |\n| **MOCK** | No credentials are stored; ABHA flows run against an offline mock. |\n| **ERROR** (red) | The handshake failed; the detail says why. |\n\n\"Health check failed — is the clinical service reachable?\" means the app could not reach its own\nclinical service at all.\n\n## Clinical Catalogs\n\n**Clinical Catalogs** (`/catalogs`) is the master data the rest of the app picks from: the drugs\nprescribers choose, the tests labs run, the diagnoses coders search, and so on. It opens on a grid\nof cards, one per catalog:\n\n| Card | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Value Sets** | Coded pick-lists used across forms, with English and Arabic display text. |\n| **Packages** | Bundled services such as health checks and surgical packages. |\n| **Administrative Services** | Registration, consultation and other non-clinical services. |\n| **Vital Signs Templates** | Which vitals are captured, by care setting and age group. |\n| **Medications** | The formulary: form, strength, route, NDC, drug class, controlled status. |\n| **Diagnoses** | ICD releases and their codes. |\n| **Lab Tests** | Lab tests with LOINC codes, specimens and reference ranges. |\n| **Observation Codes** | The coded analytes that results are recorded against. |\n| **Imaging Studies** | Imaging studies with modality, body part and CPT code. |\n| **Procedures** | Procedures with CPT and ICD-10-PCS codes and anaesthesia type. |\n| **Note Templates** | Clinical note templates and their versions. |\n| **Checklist Templates** | Checklists for inpatient and outpatient workflows. |\n| **Form Master** | OpenMedForm forms clinicians fill against an encounter. |\n\nEvery catalog list has a **Back** button to this grid, a search box, and a **Status** filter (**All**,\n**Active**, **Inactive**) where the catalog has one. Rows open the item's detail page, or press **View**.\nActive items carry an **Active** badge; inactive ones a grey **Inactive** badge.\n\nMost catalogs are **browse-only** in the app: their contents come from the setup wizard or from data\nloads. The ones you can change here are Vital Signs Templates, Lab Tests, Note Templates, Checklist\nTemplates and Form Master, plus billing mappings on several detail pages.\n\n### Setting up catalogs for the first time\n\nWhile no catalog has ever been populated, the grid shows a blue **Setup Your Catalogs** banner. While\na population job is running it shows an amber **Catalog Population In Progress** banner with\n**View Progress** instead.\n\n**Setup Catalogs** (`/catalogs/setup`) is a four-step wizard: **Country**, **Catalogs**, **Confirm**,\n**Progress**.\n\n1. **Select Country**: choose from the list. A country marked **Curated** has prepared template data;\n   the screen lists which catalogs come from templates and which will be **AI-generated**. A country\n   without curated data warns that \"All catalog data will be generated by AI\", and recommends\n   reviewing it before clinical use. Press **Next**.\n2. **Select Catalogs**: every catalog is ticked to start. Each is badged **Template** or **AI**. Use\n   **Select All** or **Clear**, and tick the ones you want. **Next** stays greyed out until at least\n   one is ticked.\n3. **Review & Confirm**: shows the country and how many catalogs, with a reminder that AI-generated\n   items are active immediately but flagged for review. Press **Start Population**.\n4. **Progress**: a bar shows \"_n_ of _m_ catalogs\", and each catalog has a row with its status icon\n   (clock waiting, spinner in progress, green tick done, red cross failed, skip arrow skipped), the\n   items inserted, and where the data came from (**Template**, **AI Enriched**, **AI Generated**).\n   **Cancel** stops the job while it runs.\n\n**Check:** the heading reads **Population Complete** with \"Successfully inserted _n_ items across _m_\ncatalogs\". Then **View Catalogs**, or **Start New** to run again.\n\n**If instead:** **Failed to start** shows the server's reason on the confirm step; **Population Failed**\nor **Population Cancelled** shows on the progress step.\n\n### Value Sets\n\n**Value Sets** (`/catalogs/value-sets`): columns **Value Set** (name and code), **Category**,\n**Version**, **Concepts** and **Status**; filter by category. Open one (`/catalogs/value-sets/{code}`)\nto see its **Concepts**, searchable by code, display or definition, shown in **English (en)** or\n**Arabic (ar)**, with **Include inactive** to show retired concepts.\n\n### Packages and Administrative Services\n\n**Packages** (`/catalogs/packages`) lists each package's **Type**, **Care Setting**, **Items**,\n**Validity** and **Status**. The detail page (`/catalogs/packages/{id}`) shows the overview (care\nsetting, validity, age range, gender restriction) and **Package items**, each with its quantity and\nwhether it is **Mandatory** or **Clinical only**.\n\n**Administrative Services** (`/catalogs/administrative-services`) lists **Service**, **Category**,\n**Type**, **Care Setting**, **Duration** and **Status**, filterable by category and type. The detail\npage (`/catalogs/administrative-services/{id}`) badges a service **Taxable**, **Staff Required** or\n**Room Required**, and shows its department, duration and billing code.\n\n### Medications, Diagnoses, Imaging Studies and Procedures\n\nBrowse-only lists with a detail page each:\n\n- **Medications** (`/catalogs/medications`): **Form**, **Strength**, **Route**, **NDC**, **Drug Class**,\n  and **Controlled**, a red badge naming the controlled class.\n- **Diagnoses** (`/catalogs/diagnoses`): **Code**, **Description**, **Version**, **Chapter / Block**,\n  and **Billable** (green) or **Reference** (grey, not to be billed). Filter by ICD version. The detail\n  page also shows synonyms, search terms and the ICD release it came from.\n- **Imaging Studies** (`/catalogs/imaging-studies`): **Modality**, **Body Part**, **CPT Code**,\n  **Contrast**, **Duration**, **Radiologist**.\n- **Procedures** (`/catalogs/procedures`): **Body System**, **CPT Code**, **ICD-10-PCS**, **Anesthesia**,\n  **Duration**, **Consent**.\n\n**Observation Codes** (`/catalogs/observation-codes`) is a browse-only list (**Observation Catalog**)\nof coded analytes with their **Category**, **Lab Domain**, **Data Type**, **Default Unit** and\n**Default Range**.\n\n### Lab Tests\n\n**Lab Tests** (`/catalogs/lab-tests`) lists **Test Name**, **LOINC Code**, **CPT Code**, **Specimen**,\n**Fasting**, **TAT**, **Report Style**, **Discipline**, **Units** and **Status**.\n\nThe detail page (`/catalogs/lab-tests/{id}`) shows basic information, classification, coding,\nspecimen and collection, normal ranges, processing and metadata. Two parts can be changed.\n\n**Edit** opens **Edit lab master maintenance fields**:\n\n- **Report Style**: **Structured**, **Narrative** or **Hybrid**.\n- **Lab Discipline**: Hematology, Chemistry, Microbiology and so on, or **Not specified**.\n- **Turnaround Time (hours)**, a whole number.\n- **Reference Lab**, for example In-house.\n- **Active lab master**: inactive tests stay in the catalog but should not be offered for new orders.\n\nPress **Save changes**. \"Lab master updated\" confirms it. \"Turnaround time must be a whole number of\nhours.\" refuses a fractional or non-numeric time.\n\n**Result Analyte Template** sets out the rows a result for this test is entered against:\n\n1. **Add root analyte** opens **Add laboratory analyte**. Search the observation catalog (WBC, HGB, a\n   LOINC code) and pick one.\n2. **Add group** adds a heading row such as Differential Count; then use the add button on the group\n   to put analytes under it.\n3. Change a row's **Display label**, toggle it required or optional, or remove it.\n4. **Save template** saves the whole hierarchy (\"Analyte template updated\"); **Reset** discards your\n   unsaved changes.\n\nGroups only organise the panel: result entry still records one row per analyte.\n\n### Vital Signs Templates\n\n**Vital Signs Templates** (`/catalogs/vital-signs-templates`) lists **Template**, **Care Settings**,\n**Age Groups**, **Specialties**, **Version** (with a **Default** badge) and **Status**. Filter by care\nsetting, age group, default or not, and status. Detail pages (`/catalogs/vital-signs-templates/{id}`)\nare read-only.\n\nTo create one, press **New** to open **New Vital Signs Template**\n(`/catalogs/vital-signs-templates/new`):\n\n1. **Template details**: **Template code**, **Version**, **Name (EN)** and **Name (AR)**, descriptions,\n   **Specialties (comma separated)**, and switches for **Active** and **Default for context**.\n2. **Context**: tick the **Care settings** (OPD, ER, IPD, ICU, Daycare, Any) and **Age groups**\n   (Newborn, Infant, Child, Adolescent, Adult, Elderly, All) it applies to.\n3. **Groups & Items**: **Add group**, then **Add item** within it. Each item has an ID, code, type\n   (Number, Text, Select, Multiselect, Boolean, Calculated), English and Arabic labels, default unit,\n   options for select types, sort order and **Required**.\n4. Press **Create template**.\n\n**If instead:** **Cannot create template** with one of:\n\n- \"Template code is required.\"\n- \"Template name (EN) is required.\"\n- \"Select at least one care setting.\"\n- \"Select at least one age group.\"\n- \"Add at least one group.\"\n- \"Each group must have at least one item.\"\n\n### Note Templates\n\n**Note Templates** (`/catalogs/note-templates`) has tiles for **Total templates**, **Active\ntemplates**, **Tenant-owned** and **Global defaults**, and lists **Template**, **Type**,\n**Specialty**, **Status**, **Version**, **Scope** and **Last updated**. Filter by status (**Active**,\n**Inactive**, **Archived**) and type (SOAP Note, Discharge Summary, Progress Note and so on).\n\n**New template** (`/catalogs/note-templates/new`) opens **Create note template**: **Template name**,\n**Template Type**, **Specialty (optional)**, **Status**, **Change log**, **Description**, and\n**Schema JSON**, the template's structure (sections, fields, defaults). Press **Create template**.\n\"Name required\" or \"Schema must be valid JSON.\" refuse it.\n\nThe detail page (`/catalogs/note-templates/{id}`) shows the overview, the latest version and the\nversion history, badged **Tenant template** or **Global default**. A tenant template can be archived:\npress **Archive template** and confirm. Archived templates are hidden from charting but kept in\nhistory. Global defaults cannot be archived.\n\n### Checklist Templates\n\n**Checklist Templates** (`/catalogs/checklists`) lists **Template**, **Category**, **Status**,\n**Version** and **Last updated**, filterable by status, category and **Inpatient** / **Outpatient**.\n\n1. Press **New Template**. In **Create Checklist Template** give a **Code** (for example\n   SURGERY_PRE_OP_V1), a **Name**, a **Category** (Discharge, Surgery, Pre-operative, Admission and so\n   on) and an optional **Description**.\n2. Save. The template is created as a draft and its page opens (`/catalogs/checklists/{id}`).\n3. Press **Add Item** for each line: **Label**, **Item Key**, **Type**, an optional **Section** (items\n   are grouped under section headings) and **Help Text**, and tick **Required** if the item must be\n   completed before the checklist can be submitted.\n\n**If instead:** \"Code must be at least 3 characters\", \"Name must be at least 3 characters\", \"Label must\nbe at least 3 characters\", or \"Key must be lowercase alphanumeric with underscores\".\n\n### Form Master\n\n**Form Master** (`/catalogs/form-master`) holds forms designed in OpenMedForm that clinicians fill\nagainst a patient's encounter. The list shows **Name**, **Form Code**, **Version**, **Frequency**,\n**Status** and **Uploaded**.\n\n1. Press **Upload Form** (`/catalogs/form-master/new`).\n2. Download the form from OpenMedForm, then paste its JSON into the box or choose the file.\n3. Choose the **Frequency type**: how often it should be filled (Every N hours, Every N days, Once\n   daily, Weekly, Once per shift, Once per admission, Once per episode, Fill whenever needed, Trigger\n   after an event). For the \"every N\" types set **Every** and the **Unit**.\n4. Press **Upload Form**.\n\n**Check:** \"Form uploaded\": the form is now available for clinicians to fill.\n\n**If instead:** \"Invalid JSON.\", or \"This does not look like an OpenMedForm export bundle — expected\nformCode, version, dataSchema, and uiSchema.\"\n\nTo replace an active form, press **New Version** on its row or detail page and upload the new export.\nThe previous version is archived automatically; charting always uses the latest active version. The\ndetail page (`/catalogs/form-master/{id}`) shows a read-only **Form Preview**, exactly as clinicians\nwill see it.\n\n### Billing mappings\n\nThe detail pages for packages, administrative services, medications, lab tests, imaging studies and\nprocedures end with **Billing Mappings**: which billing items are charged when the catalog item is\nused. **Add mapping** lets you search for a billing item (code-matched suggestions are highlighted),\nset a quantity, and tick **Primary**, **Automatic** and **Requires approval**. Mappings show as badges:\n**Primary**, **Auto**, **Needs approval**. The bin icon removes a mapping at once, with no\nconfirmation. See [Billing setup](billing-setup.md) for billing items themselves.\n\n## Tenants\n\n**Tenants** (`/tenants`, from the gear menu) lists each organisation with **Tenant Name**, **Domain**,\n**Status**, **Settings** (language and time zone, or **Default**) and **Created**.\n\nTo add one, press **Create tenant**, give a **Tenant name** and a **Domain**, and save. \"Tenant\ncreated\" confirms it; \"Unable to create tenant\" gives the server's reason. **View** opens the tenant\n(`/tenants/{tenantId}`), which shows each of its facilities as a tree.\n\n## Facilities\n\n**Facilities** (`/facilities`, from the gear menu) lists your tenant's facilities with **Facility**,\n**Type**, **City** and **Status**.\n\n1. Press **Create Facility** (`/facilities/new`).\n2. Give the **Facility Name** (required), and optionally its type, code, licence number, address,\n   city, emirate, postal code, phone, email and website.\n3. Press **Create Facility**.\n\n**Check:** \"Facility created\", and the new facility's page opens.\n\n**If instead:** \"Facility name is required\" or \"Invalid email\" under the field, or an error message\nfrom the server.\n\nA facility's page (`/facilities/{facilityId}`) shows its structure as an expandable tree:\ndepartments, then their wards (with patient-room spaces and beds) and clinics (with exam-room\nspaces), or **No wards or clinics**. It is read-only.\n\nA new facility is not usable by anyone until you grant access to it on each user's record\n([Facility access](#facility-access)).\n\n## Users\n\n**Users** (`/users`, from the gear menu) are the people who sign in. The list shows **Name**,\n**Email**, **Role**, **Status** and **Created**.\n\n### Creating a user\n\n1. Press **New User** (`/users/new`).\n2. Enter **Email**, **Password** (at least 8 characters), **First Name** and **Last Name**.\n3. Optionally **Link Staff Profile** to an existing staff member, or leave **No staff linked**.\n4. Press **Create User**.\n\n**Check:** \"User created\", and the user's page opens. A new user has no roles and no facility access\nyet: set both there.\n\n**If instead:** \"Enter a valid email\", \"Password must be at least 8 characters\", \"First name is\nrequired\", \"Last name is required\", or the server's message.\n\n### The user's page\n\nThe user's page (`/users/{userId}`) shows their details with a status badge: **active** (solid),\n**invited** (grey), **inactive** or **suspended** (red).\n\n- **Edit User** changes the name, email and **Status** (**Active**, **Inactive**, **Invited**,\n  **Suspended**). \"User updated\" confirms it.\n- **Delete** removes the account permanently after you confirm. This cannot be undone.\n- **Staff Member**: shows the linked staff profile with **Unlink**, or **Link Staff** to pick one.\n\n### User roles\n\n**User Roles** lists the user's roles, each with **Remove**. Pick one under **Assign Role** and press\n**Assign Role**. \"All tenant roles are already assigned.\" when there are none left to add. What each\nrole allows is set on [Roles](#roles-and-permissions).\n\n### Facility access\n\n**Facility Access** lists the facilities the user may work in, each with its access level badge\n(**standard**, **admin**, **read_only**) and **Default** on the home facility.\n\n1. Under **Assign Facility**, pick the facility and an access level (**Standard**, **Admin**, **Read\n   only**), and tick **Set as default** if it should be their home facility.\n2. Press **Assign Facility**.\n\n**Set Default** makes another facility their home; **Revoke** removes access. A user with more than one\nfacility gets the **Switch Facility** list in their user menu\n([Switching facility](#switching-facility)).\n\n## Staff\n\n**Clinical Staff** (`/staff`, from the gear menu, labelled **Staff**) is the professional record of\neach clinician or staff member: separate from their sign-in, so a staff member can exist without a\nuser account. The list shows **Employee ID**, **Name**, **Staff Type**, **Specialty** (the primary\none), **Languages**, **License** and **Status**.\n\nSearch by name, specialty, licence, language or type, filter by type from the **All Types** button,\nand **Clear All** resets both. Click a row to open it.\n\n### Adding a staff member\n\n1. Press **New Staff** (`/staff/new`).\n2. **Basic Information**: prefix, **First Name**, **Last Name**, middle name, **Employee ID**, **Date of\n   Birth**, **Gender** and **Staff Type** (Physician, Nurse, Technician, Administrative, Support).\n3. **Contact Information** and **Professional Information**: email, phone, qualification, languages\n   (comma separated), licence number and expiry.\n4. Press **Create Staff Member**.\n\n**If instead:** \"First name is required\", \"Last name is required\", \"Date of birth is required\",\n\"Gender is required\", \"Employee ID is required\", \"Staff type is required\" or \"Invalid email\".\n\nThe staff page (`/staff/{id}`) shows their details, languages, primary specialty and licence. Under\n**User Account**, press **Link User** to connect a sign-in account, or **Unlink**. Only users not\nalready linked to someone else are offered.\n\n## Spaces\n\n**Spaces** (`/spaces`, from the gear menu) lists the rooms of your **current facility**: **Space** (name\nand number), **Facility**, **Capacity**, and **Status** (**Available** or **Inactive**). It is\nread-only; switch facility to see another site's spaces.\n\n## Roles and permissions\n\n**Roles** (`/rbac/roles`, from the gear menu as **RBAC**) lists each role's **Code**, **Role name**,\n**Description** and whether it is a **System role**.\n\n### Creating a role\n\n1. Press **New Role**.\n2. Give a **Role Code** (for example billing_manager), a **Role Name** and an optional **Description**.\n3. Press **Create Role**. \"Role created\" confirms it, and the role's page opens for you to add\n   permissions.\n\n**If instead:** \"Code is required\" or \"Name is required\".\n\n### Giving a role its permissions\n\nThe role's page (`/rbac/roles/{id}`) shows **Role Information**, **Role Status** (created, updated and\nthe permissions count) and **Permissions**, grouped by the resource they act on.\n\n1. Search by name, code or resource to narrow the list.\n2. Tick or untick permissions. **Select Visible** and **Clear Visible** act on everything the search is\n   showing.\n3. Press **Save Permissions**. It stays greyed out until something has changed.\n\n**Check:** \"Permissions updated\", and the count under **Permissions** changes.\n\n**Edit Role** changes the name and description. **Delete Role** removes it from every user who has it,\nafter you confirm.\n\n**System roles** are badged **System Role** and are managed by the platform: \"System roles are managed\nby the platform and cannot be edited.\" Their buttons and boxes are greyed out. Make a custom role\ninstead.\n\nPermission changes take effect on the server: a user whose role lost a permission is refused that\naction from then on. They do not change what the sidebar shows (see\n[What controls the sidebar](#what-controls-the-sidebar)).\n\n## Plugin Management\n\n**Plugin Management** (`/plugin-management`, from the gear menu) lists the specialty modules installed\non the platform, such as Oncology, with a search box and a count of plugins installed.\n\nEach plugin card shows its name, version and specialty, and two badges:\n\n| Badge | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Active** / **Inactive** | Green / red | Whether the plugin is on for your tenant. The whole card is tinted to match. |\n| **Backend Online** / **Backend Offline** / **Checking…** | Blue / orange / grey | Whether the service the plugin runs on is answering. Re-checked every 30 seconds. |\n\nUnder that: the service it runs on, how many permissions it adds, and when it was installed.\n\n### Activating a plugin for your tenant\n\n1. Flip the plugin's switch. It says **Updating...**.\n2. **Plugin activated** (\"_name_ has been activated for this tenant.\") confirms it, and the switch reads\n   **Active for this tenant**.\n\nActivating also switches on the plugin's sidebar section for the tenant. Deactivating does the reverse\n(**Plugin deactivated**). **Activation failed** or **Deactivation failed** gives the server's reason.\n\n**Details** opens the plugin's manifest: registry status, backend health, target service, author,\nlicence, the tenants it is active for, and the navigation it adds.\n\n### Connectors & Capabilities\n\nBelow the plugins, **Connectors & Capabilities** lists standalone connector services, which are not\ninstalled through the plugin registry. The **ABDM Connector** shows **Enabled** or **Disabled** (from\nthe ABDM activation switch) and the capabilities bound to it. **Configure** opens\n[ABDM Settings](#abdm-settings).\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- The **Clinical Catalogs** cards always read \"— entries\"; no counts are shown.\n- On the Medications, Diagnoses, Imaging Studies and Procedures detail pages, and on Lab Tests, the\n  **Deactivate** button does nothing. **Edit** does nothing on the first four; on Lab Tests it works.\n- A new checklist template is created as a draft and there is no control to activate it or change its\n  status.\n- The note template archive dialog says you can reactivate an archived template later, but there is no\n  control for that.\n- Catalog Value Sets, Packages, Administrative Services, Medications, Diagnoses, Observation Codes,\n  Imaging Studies and Procedures cannot be added to or edited in the app.\n- Tenants, facilities and staff cannot be edited after creation, and a facility's departments, wards,\n  clinics and spaces cannot be added here. **Spaces** is a read-only list.\n- A new tenant is always created with the Asia/Dubai time zone.\n- **Facility Overrides** on **Configurations** are read-only: facility values cannot be set or reset\n  here.\n- A plugin whose backend is not the clinical service always shows **Backend Offline**, because only the\n  clinical service is checked.\n- `/rbac/permissions` shows three fixed example rows, not the real permission list, and nothing links\n  to it. Use a role's page to see real permissions.\n",
  "analytics.md": "---\ntitle: 'Reports and search'\ngroup: 'Insights'\norder: 160\nroutes: ['/reports', '/search']\ndescription: 'The Analytics section: asking questions of your data in Report Builder, charting and exporting the answer, and finding clinical documents by meaning in Clinical Search.'\n---\n\nShown when your organisation has Analytics switched on.\n\nThe **Analytics** section of the sidebar holds two screens, both marked **AI-Powered**:\n\n- **Report Builder** (`/reports`): type a question about your data in plain English and get a table or\n  chart back, which you can export.\n- **Clinical Search** (`/search`): type what you are looking for and find clinical notes, discharge\n  summaries and reports that mean it, even when they use different words.\n\n**Clinical Search is not the patient search box.** The box in the topbar, **Search patients by name, MRN,\nphone**, finds a patient by name, MRN or phone and opens their record; see\n[Finding a patient](getting-started.md#finding-a-patient). **Clinical Search** looks inside the text of\nclinical documents across all patients. Use the topbar box when you know who you want; use **Clinical\nSearch** when you know what was written.\n\n## Report Builder\n\n**Report Builder** (`/reports`) is headed \"Ask questions about your data in plain English\". Across the top\nright are **Catalog**, **History** and the **AI-Powered** badge.\n\nThe question is turned into a report by an AI service, which picks the figures and groupings it thinks you\nmean and runs them against your organisation's data. Always read the **Interpretation** it gives back\nbefore you rely on the numbers.\n\n### Ask a question\n\n**Go to:** **Analytics** > **Report Builder**.\n\n1. In **Ask a Question**, type your question into the box, for example \"What is the total revenue by\n   department for this month?\".\n2. Press Enter, or the send button to the right of the box. Shift+Enter starts a new line instead. The send\n   button is greyed out while the box is empty, and spins while the report runs.\n\nTwo small buttons sit inside the box:\n\n- the sparkle button (**Example queries**) lists ready-made questions, such as \"What's today's total\n  revenue?\", \"Show me patient count by department\" and \"List all unpaid invoices from last month\". Pick one\n  to put it in the box, then send it;\n- the clock button (**Recent queries**) lists your last five questions that worked. It appears only once you\n  have one.\n\n**Check:** the **Results** panel fills with a table and says how many rows were found, for example \"42 rows\nfound\". Above it you may see:\n\n- **Interpretation**: how the question was understood. If it is not what you meant, rephrase and ask again.\n- **Related Questions**: follow-up questions as chips. Press one to run it straight away.\n\n**If instead:** a red **Error** panel appears above the results with the reason the report could not be\nbuilt, or \"Failed to generate report\" when no reason was given. If the request itself fails (for example the\nservice is unreachable), a red toast also says \"Query failed\" with the reason. Rephrase the question, or\nuse **Catalog** to see what can be asked about.\n\nA report returns at most 1,000 rows. Money is shown in your organisation's billing currency (INR when none\nis set).\n\n### Read the table\n\n- Press a column heading to sort by it; press again to reverse. An arrow shows the direction.\n- The table shows 25 rows a page. Use **Previous** and **Next**; the footer says \"Showing 1 to 25 of 42 rows\"\n  and \"Page 1 of 2\".\n- Columns of money or counts get a **Total** row at the bottom of the page, adding up every row returned.\n- Under the table, \"Query executed in ...ms\" says how long it took.\n\nBefore you ask anything the panel says \"Enter a query to see results\". A question that matches nothing says\n\"No results found for this query\".\n\n### See it as a chart\n\nAbove the results, switch between **Table** and **Chart**.\n\n**Chart** is greyed out unless the answer suits a chart: at least one row and no more than 50, with at least\none column of numbers and at least one column of names or dates to plot them against.\n\nIn chart view, four buttons choose the chart: bar, line, pie and area. The screen picks one for you each time\na new answer arrives:\n\n| Answer looks like | Chart picked |\n| --- | --- |\n| Has a date column | Line |\n| Eight rows or fewer, one column of numbers | Pie |\n| Anything else | Bar |\n\nA pie chart uses only the first column of numbers. If the chart cannot be drawn it says \"No data available\nfor chart visualization\".\n\n### Export\n\n**Export** (beside the view buttons) offers **Excel (.xlsx)**, **CSV (.csv)** and **PDF (.pdf)**. It is\ngreyed out until a question has returned at least one row. The file downloads to your computer, named from\nthe start of your question and today's date.\n\n**Check:** a toast says \"Export successful\" with, for example, \"Report exported as CSV.\".\n\n**If instead:** a red toast says \"Export failed\" with \"There was an error exporting the report. Please try\nagain.\". Read the warning about exports under [Not live yet](#not-live-yet).\n\n### History\n\n**History** opens **Recent Queries**: your last 20 questions, newest first, each with when it ran and either\na row count or a red **Failed** badge. The badge on the **History** button counts them.\n\n- Press a question to run it again.\n- **Clear** empties the list; a toast says \"History cleared\" with \"Your report history has been cleared.\".\n\nHistory is kept in this browser only. It does not follow you to another computer, and clearing your\nbrowser data clears it. When it is empty it says \"No recent queries\".\n\n### Catalog\n\n**Catalog** opens **Available Metrics & Dimensions**: the figures (**Metrics**, such as revenue or patient\ncounts) and the groupings (**Dimensions**, such as department or date) that questions can use. Each list\nshows a count and is grouped by category.\n\n- Type in **Search metrics & dimensions...** to narrow both lists.\n- Hover over an entry to see its description and its field name.\n- Use the names you see here in your question; for example a metric \"Total Revenue\" and a dimension\n  \"Department\" make \"Show total revenue by department\".\n\nIf nothing matches it says \"No metrics found\" or \"No dimensions found\". If the catalog cannot be fetched it\nsays \"Failed to load catalog\".\n\n## Clinical Search\n\n**Clinical Search** (`/search`) is headed \"Search clinical documents using natural language\". Across the\ntop right are **Filters**, **Index Status** and the **AI-Powered** badge.\n\nIt compares the meaning of what you type with the meaning of each indexed document, so \"uncontrolled blood\nsugar\" can find a note that says \"poor glycaemic control\". Until you search, a **How it works** panel\nexplains this.\n\n### Search\n\n**Go to:** **Analytics** > **Clinical Search**.\n\n1. In **Search Clinical Documents**, describe what you are looking for, for example \"Find notes mentioning\n   diabetes with uncontrolled blood sugar\".\n2. Press Enter, or the search button to the right. The button is greyed out while the box is empty.\n\nThe sparkle button inside the box (**Example searches**) offers ready-made searches. They change to suit the\ndocument types you have ticked under **Filters**.\n\n**Check:** **Results** says how many documents were found, for example \"12 documents found\", and lists them\nbest match first. Up to 20 are shown, and only documents that are at least a 30% match.\n\n**If instead:** a red **Search Error** panel shows the reason (or \"Search failed\"), and a red toast says\n\"Search failed\". A search that finds nothing says \"No results found\" and \"Try adjusting your search query\nor filters\".\n\n### Reading a result\n\nEach result is a card showing the passage that matched, not the whole document:\n\n- a coloured badge for the document type (for example **Discharge Summaries** or **Lab Reports**);\n- the match as a percentage, with a bar under it;\n- the matching passage, with key words highlighted in yellow;\n- the patient's name and age, **MRN**, the encounter type, the author, the facility and department, and the\n  document's date, where they are known;\n- a shortened **Document ID**.\n\nThe match percentage is coloured:\n\n| Match | Colour |\n| --- | --- |\n| 90% and above | Green |\n| 80% to 89% | Blue |\n| 70% to 79% | Amber |\n| Below 70% | Grey |\n\nAbove the cards, \"Found 12 results\" and \"Search took ...ms\".\n\n### Open a document\n\nPress the open button at the top right of a card (**View document**). A **Document Preview** panel slides in\nfrom the right with:\n\n- the encounter number (or the shortened document ID);\n- **Patient Information**: name, age, MRN and gender;\n- **Document Details**: author, encounter type, facility and department;\n- **Matched Content**: the passage that matched.\n\nThen:\n\n- **View Full Document** opens where the document lives: the encounter's charting screen\n  (`/encounters/{id}/charting`) for encounter, clinical, progress, consultation, procedure and operative\n  notes; the encounter (`/encounters/{id}`) for discharge summaries and radiology, lab and pathology\n  reports; or the patient's record when the document has no encounter.\n- **Patient Profile** opens the patient's record (`/patients/{id}`).\n\nSee [Patient access](patient-access.md) for those screens.\n\n### Find similar documents\n\nPress **Find similar documents** at the foot of a card to replace the results with up to ten documents most\nlike that one.\n\n**Check:** a toast says \"Similar documents found\" with \"Found 8 similar documents.\".\n\n**If instead:** a red toast says \"Failed to find similar documents\" with the reason, and the results you had\nstay on screen.\n\nTo go back to your own search, type it again.\n\n### Filters\n\n**Filters** opens **Search Filters**. Narrow the search by:\n\n- **Document Types**: tick any of **Encounter Notes**, **Discharge Summaries**, **Clinical Notes**,\n  **Progress Notes**, **Consultation Notes**, **Procedure Notes**, **Operative Notes**, **Radiology Reports**,\n  **Lab Reports** and **Pathology Reports**. Tick none to search them all.\n- **Date Range**: **From** and **To**.\n- **Patient**: **Patient Name**, **MRN**, **Gender** (**Male**, **Female**, **Other**) and **Age Range**\n  (**Min** and **Max**).\n- **Encounter Type**: **Outpatient**, **Inpatient**, **Emergency**, **Day Case** or **Virtual**.\n- **Author/Physician**: the clinician's name.\n\nA count beside **Filters** inside the panel shows how many filters are set; **Clear** removes them all.\nChanging a filter re-runs your current search, but see [Not live yet](#not-live-yet): press search again\nafter you finish setting filters.\n\n### Index Status\n\n**Index Status** opens **Embedding Index Status**. Clinical documents have to be indexed before Clinical\nSearch can find them. The panel shows:\n\n- **Documents** and **Chunks** indexed (long documents are split into chunks);\n- **_n_ pending** in amber and **_n_ failed** in red, or **All synced** in green when neither;\n- **Last sync** and its time.\n\n**Reindex** starts a fresh indexing run. Choose one of:\n\n- **Full Re-index (all documents)**: \"Re-generates embeddings for all documents. Use after model upgrades.\"\n- **New Documents Only**: \"Only indexes documents that do not have embeddings yet.\"\n- **Update Metadata Only**: \"Updates patient/author metadata for existing embeddings without regenerating\n  vectors.\"\n\nA toast says \"Reindex started\" with the server's message, or \"Failed to start reindex\" with \"There was an\nerror starting the reindex job.\". While it runs the panel shows **Reindexing...**, how many documents are\ndone out of the total, a progress bar and an ETA. **Cancel Reindex** stops it (\"Reindex cancelled\"; or\n\"Failed to cancel reindex\" if it could not).\n\nA full re-index works through every document and can take a long time. Leave it to whoever looks after the\nsystem unless you have been asked to run it.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Report Builder, Catalog**: pressing a metric or dimension closes the catalog but does not put anything in\n  the question box. Read the names in the catalog and type them yourself.\n- **Report Builder, Export**: the export runs the question again rather than saving the table on screen, and\n  it uses only the first 30 characters of your question. A longer question can export a different answer\n  from the one you see, or fail. Check the file before you share it, or keep questions short when you mean\n  to export them.\n- **Report Builder**: there is no way to save a report, name it, mark it as a favourite or schedule it. Use\n  **History** to rerun recent questions.\n- **Clinical Search, Filters**: when you change a filter, the automatic re-run uses the filters as they were\n  just before your change, so the results are one step behind. Press search again once you have finished.\n  Typing in a text filter re-runs the search on every keystroke.\n- **Clinical Search, Filters**: the count on the **Filters** button counts only document types and dates;\n  patient, encounter type and author filters are set but not counted there. There is no facility or\n  department filter on this screen.\n- **Clinical Search**: the match percentage in **Document Preview** is always green, whatever the score.\n- **Index Status**: anyone who can open Clinical Search can start or cancel a re-index from here.\n\nFor the patient search box in the topbar, see [Getting started](getting-started.md#finding-a-patient).\nTerms are explained in the [Glossary](glossary.md).\n",
  "billing-setup.md": "---\ntitle: 'Billing setup'\ngroup: 'Billing and revenue'\norder: 120\nroutes: ['/rcm-setup']\ndescription: 'RCM Setup: payers, billing items (the charge master), charge posting rules, fee schedules, payer contracts, and catalog mappings that turn clinical orders into charges.'\n---\n\n**RCM Setup** (`/rcm-setup`, under **Administration** in the sidebar) is where the billing team sets\nup what everything costs and who pays. It opens on six tiles:\n\n- **Payers**: insurers, government schemes and other payers.\n- **Billing items**: the charge master, every billable code and its list price.\n- **Charge posting rules**: how clinical events become charges automatically.\n- **Fee schedules**: base rates and payer price lists.\n- **Payer contracts**: what each payer has agreed to pay.\n- **Catalog mappings**: which billing item each clinical catalog item charges.\n\nSet them up in roughly this order: billing items first, then catalog mappings so orders produce\ncharges, then payers, fee schedules and contracts for insured patients. The screens that use them\nare described in [Billing](billing.md) and [Revenue cycle and claims](revenue-cycle.md).\n\nPrices show in your organisation's **finance.currency** (AED when none is set).\n\n## Payers\n\n**Payers** (`/rcm-setup/payers`) lists every payer: **Payer** (name and electronic ID), **Type**,\n**Status**, **Active policies** (how many patient policies are held with it) and **Last updated**.\nTiles count **Total payers**, **Active**, **Inactive** and **Suspended**. Search with **Search\npayers...** or filter by status. Click a row or **View** to open it.\n\n### Adding a payer\n\n**New payer** (`/rcm-setup/payers/new`):\n\n1. **Name** (required), such as \"Gulf Cooperative Insurance\".\n2. **Payer ID**: the payer's electronic ID.\n3. **Type**: free text, such as insurance, TPA, government, corporate or self. These values get\n   matching coloured badges on the claims list.\n4. **Status**: **Active**, **Inactive** or **Suspended**.\n5. **Contact info (JSON)** and **Configuration (JSON)**, both optional and written as JSON.\n6. Press **Create payer**. \"Payer created\".\n\nThe **Configuration** decides how the payer is reached. Two keys matter to other screens:\n\n- `eligibilityConnector`: the connector used for eligibility checks. Without it a built-in test\n  connector answers.\n- `nhcxParticipantCode`: the payer's participant code on the national claims exchange (NHCX).\n  When set, claims and pre-authorisations for this payer are submitted through the exchange.\n\n**If instead:** \"Payer name is required\"; \"Invalid JSON\" with \"Check contact info/config fields.\"\nwhen either JSON box is not valid JSON; \"Unable to create payer\" with the server's reason.\n\n### A payer's record\n\nOpen a payer (`/rcm-setup/payers/{id}`) to see **Identifiers** (**Payer ID**, **Type**,\n**Created**, **Updated**), **Contact info** and **Configuration**. **Deactivate** moves the payer to\ninactive (\"Payer deactivated\") and returns you to the list; it is hidden once the payer is\ninactive. A refusal shows \"Unable to deactivate payer\".\n\n## Billing items\n\n**Billing items** (`/rcm-setup/billing-items`) is the charge master. The columns are **Code** (with\nits code system), **Description**, **Type**, **Charge**, **List price**, **Status** and **Updated**.\nTiles count **Total items**, **Active** and **Inactive**.\n\nFilter by status, **Item type** (lab, imaging, procedure, consult, registration, pharmacy, package,\nmisc), **Charge type** (registration, consultation, lab, radiology, procedure, pharmacy, package,\nmisc) and **Code system** (INTERNAL, CPT, DHA, DOH, HAAD, MOHAP, LOINC, CUSTOM). **Include global\ncatalog items** (on by default) also lists the shared global items alongside your own.\n\n### Adding or editing a billing item\n\n**New billing item** (`/rcm-setup/billing-items/new`):\n\n1. **Item type**, **Charge type**, **Billing code** and **Code system** (all required).\n2. **Description** (required), such as \"Consultation 30 minutes\".\n3. **Default unit** (such as \"each\") and **List price**. The list price is what fills in when the\n   item is picked on an invoice line.\n4. **Clinical reference ID** (optional).\n5. **Is active?**: inactive codes are hidden from ordering workflows.\n6. Press **Create billing item**. \"Billing item created\".\n\nOpen an item (`/rcm-setup/billing-items/{id}`) to see **Scope & metadata** (whether it is\n**Tenant-specific** or a **Global catalog item**, its code and list price), edit it under **Edit\nbilling item** with **Update billing item**, and manage its **Mapped Catalog Items** (see\n[Catalog mappings](#catalog-mappings)).\n\nUnder **Danger zone**:\n\n- **Deactivate**: confirm \"Deactivate this billing item?\". Deactivated codes stay in history but are\n  hidden from ordering.\n- **Delete permanently**: confirm \"This will permanently delete the billing item. Continue?\". This\n  removes the item and its history; prefer **Deactivate**.\n\n## Charge posting rules\n\n**Charge posting rules** (`/rcm-setup/charge-posting-rules`) decide what charge is created when a\nclinical event happens, such as a lab test ordered or a medication dispensed. The list shows\n**Rule** (with its priority), **Event**, **Billing item**, **Calculation** and **Status**. Filter\nby event type, event source, billing type, or **Active only**.\n\n### Writing a rule\n\n**New rule** (`/rcm-setup/charge-posting-rules/new`), headed **New charge posting rule**:\n\n1. **Rule name** (required).\n2. **Event type**: lab test ordered, medication dispensed, procedure performed, imaging study\n   ordered, daily bed charge, consultation completed, or custom.\n3. **Event source**: encounter, order, pharmacy, scheduler or custom.\n4. **Billing item type** and, optionally, a **Billing item ID** to charge a specific item.\n5. **Calculation method** (fixed, catalog price, custom formula, tiered pricing), **Price source**\n   and **Quantity source**.\n6. **Base price**, **Discount %**, **Tax %** and **Priority**.\n7. **Conditions (JSON)** to limit when the rule fires, and **Configuration (JSON)**, such as tiered\n   prices.\n8. **Description**, **Active** (inactive rules are skipped) and **Auto approve charges** (otherwise\n   charges land in draft).\n9. Optionally, under **Fee schedule price lookup**, pick a code type, enter a billing code and press\n   **Use price** to copy a fee schedule price into **Base price**. The line below says \"Found ...\"\n   with the price, or why none was found.\n10. Press **Save rule**. \"Rule created\".\n\nIf either JSON box is not valid JSON the rule is not saved, and the screen gives no message.\n\nOpen a rule (`/rcm-setup/charge-posting-rules/{id}`) to see its **Rule summary**, edit it (**Update\nrule**), read the **Raw configuration**, **Activate** or **Deactivate** it for new events, or\n**Delete** it (confirm \"Delete this rule? This action cannot be undone.\").\n\n## Fee schedules\n\n**Fee schedules** (`/rcm-setup/fee-schedules`) are price lists. The list shows **Schedule** (with\nversion), **Type**, **Status** and **Effective** dates. Filter by type, status and authority (DHA,\nDOH, MOHAP, HAAD).\n\n| Type | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Authority** | A regulator's published price list. |\n| **Tenant** | Your organisation's own prices. |\n| **Contract** | Prices agreed with a payer. |\n\nStatuses are **Active** (dark badge), **Draft** (outlined), **Inactive** and **Expired**.\n\n### Creating a schedule\n\n**New schedule** (`/rcm-setup/fee-schedules/new`):\n\n1. **Name** and **Effective from** (both required); **Effective to** if it ends.\n2. **Type**; for **Authority**, also the **Authority**.\n3. **Version** (such as \"v1.0\"), **Status**, **Base schedule ID** if it derives from another\n   schedule, and **Metadata (JSON)**.\n4. Press **Save schedule**. \"Fee schedule created\".\n\nThe server refuses an end date that is not after the start date, a base schedule that does not\nexist, and an **Authority** schedule without an authority. Authority schedules are shared across\norganisations, so the server also refuses one created from within your organisation.\n\n### Prices in a schedule\n\nOpen a schedule (`/rcm-setup/fee-schedules/{id}`). Edit its details under **Edit schedule**.\n\n- **Items** lists each price: **Code** (with service group), **Type**, **Amount** and **Priority**.\n  **Edit** opens it below; **Delete** removes it (confirm \"Delete this fee schedule item?\").\n- **Add new item**: **Code** and **Base amount** (required), **Code type**, **Currency**, **Unit**,\n  **Multiplier**, **Discount %**, **Max allowed**, **Service group** and **Priority**, then **Save\n  item**.\n- **Lookup price**: choose a **Code type**, enter a **Code** and press **Lookup** to see the price\n  this schedule gives, or \"No price found\". A blank code says \"Enter a code to lookup.\"\n\n## Payer contracts\n\n**Payer contracts** (`/rcm-setup/payer-contracts`) record what each payer has agreed to pay. The list\nshows **Contract** (with number), **Type**, **Status** (**Active**, **Draft**, **Expired**,\n**Suspended**) and **Effective** dates. Filter by status, type and authority.\n\n### Creating a contract\n\n**New contract** (`/rcm-setup/payer-contracts/new`):\n\n1. **Payer** (required, picked from your payers), **Contract name** (required) and **Contract\n   number**.\n2. **Authority**, **Base fee schedule ID**, **Plan code**, **Network type** and **Line of\n   business**.\n3. **Contract type**: fee for service, capitation, bundled or value based.\n4. **Reimbursement method**: percentage of tariff, fixed rate, percentage of charges, per diem or\n   case rate.\n5. **Effective from** (required), **Effective to** and **Status**.\n6. **Multiplier**, **Discount %** and **Max allowed** for the contract as a whole.\n7. **Terms (JSON)** and **Metadata (JSON)**.\n8. Press **Save contract**.\n\nNothing is saved without a payer, contract name and start date.\n\n### Adjustments and price checks\n\nOpen a contract (`/rcm-setup/payer-contracts/{id}`): **Contract summary**, then **Edit contract**.\n\n- **Adjustments** vary the price for part of the contract. Each row shows its **Scope** (a service\n  group, or \"All services\", marked **Adjustment** or **Exclusion**), **Multiplier**, **Discount %**,\n  **Priority** and **Effective** dates. Under **Add adjustment** set a **Service group**, **Billing\n  item ID** or **Fee schedule item ID**, the **Multiplier**, **Discount %**, **Max allowed**, **Min\n  allowed**, **Priority**, dates and **Notes**, and switch on **Exclusion** to exclude matching\n  services from payment. Then **Save adjustment**.\n- **Calculate contract price**: enter a billing **Code**, **Code type** and **Quantity** and press\n  **Calculate price**. The result reads \"Final: ... (base ...)\". A blank code says \"Enter billing\n  code to calculate price.\"\n\n## Catalog mappings\n\nA catalog mapping links a clinical catalog item (a medication, lab test, imaging study, procedure,\npackage or administrative service, set up under [Administration](administration.md)) to the\nbilling item it charges. When the item is ordered or dispensed, the mapping decides which billing\nitem is charged and how many. Without a mapping, an order produces no automatic charge.\n\n**Catalog Mappings** (`/rcm-setup/catalog-mappings`) lists the mappings: **Catalog item** (type and\nID), **Billing item** (description and code), **Flags**, **Qty**, **Status** and **Effective**\ndates. Tiles count **Total mappings**, **Primary mappings** and **Require approval**. Filter by\n**Catalog type**, **Status**, **Primary** (**Primary only** or **Secondary only**) or a **Billing\nitem ID**.\n\n| Flag | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Primary** | The main billing item for this catalog item. It is chosen first. |\n| **Auto** | The charge is created automatically when the item is ordered. |\n| **Needs approval** | The charge is held until someone approves it. |\n\nOne catalog item can map to several billing items, for example a scan with a reading fee and a\nfacility fee.\n\n### Adding a mapping\n\n**New Mapping** (`/rcm-setup/catalog-mappings/new`):\n\n1. **Catalog type**.\n2. Search for the catalog item (type at least two letters) and pick it.\n3. Search for the **Billing item**. The search is limited to billing items of the matching type, and\n   items whose code matches the catalog item's code are marked **Suggested** and listed first. It\n   stays greyed out until a catalog item is picked.\n4. **Quantity**, **Mapping reason** and **Notes**.\n5. Switch on **Automatic charge**, **Primary mapping** and **Requires approval** as needed.\n6. Press **Create Mapping**. \"Mapping created\".\n\n**If instead:** \"Missing selection\" if either item is not picked; \"Failed to create mapping\" with\nthe server's reason, for example when the same pair is already mapped.\n\nYou can also add and remove mappings from a billing item's page (**Mapped Catalog Items**, then\n**Add mapping**, which opens **Add Billing Mapping**) or from a catalog item's page under Catalogs.\nThe bin button beside a mapping asks \"Remove this mapping?\" and deactivates it.\n\n### Suggested mappings\n\n**Suggested** (`/rcm-setup/catalog-mappings/suggestions`) finds catalog items and billing items that\nshare a code but are not yet mapped, such as a lab test whose LOINC code equals a billing item's\ncode.\n\n1. Pick a **Catalog type**. \"Finding matches…\" shows while it loads.\n2. Review each pair: catalog item and code on the left, billing item and price on the right.\n3. Press **Accept** on a pair, or **Accept all** to take every suggestion.\n\nAccepted mappings are created as **Primary** and **Automatic**, quantity 1. When nothing is left the\npage says \"All code-matched pairs are already mapped!\".\n\n### A mapping's record\n\nOpen a mapping (`/rcm-setup/catalog-mappings/{id}`) to see both sides, the quantity, reason,\nnotes, flags, **Effective date** (\"Any\" if none), **Expiration date** (\"No expiry\"), and any\n**Facility restriction**, **Payer restriction** or **Patient type** limits (\"All\" when none).\n**Deactivate** asks \"Deactivate this mapping? It will no longer be used for charge posting.\" and\nreturns you to the list.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Payers**: a payer cannot be edited after it is created; there is only **Deactivate**.\n- **Catalog mappings**: a mapping cannot be edited. To change one, deactivate it and create a new\n  one. Facility, payer, patient-type and date limits are shown but cannot be set on these screens.\n  The list shows the catalog item's internal ID rather than its name, and rows do not open when\n  clicked; use **View**.\n- **Charge posting rules, fee schedules, payer contracts**: when the server refuses a save, the\n  reason is not shown on screen.\n",
  "billing.md": "---\ntitle: 'Billing'\ngroup: 'Billing and revenue'\norder: 100\nroutes: ['/invoices', '/receipts', '/revenue-cycle/refunds', '/patient-ledger', '/revenue-cycle/credit-notes', '/revenue-cycle/debit-notes', '/billing-workspace', '/medical-coding']\ndescription: 'Raising invoices, taking receipts, refunds, credit and debit notes, the patient ledger, the Billing Workspace for collecting at the desk, and the Medical Coding inbox.'\n---\n\nThe **Billing** section of the sidebar holds the day-to-day money screens:\n\n- **Invoices** (`/invoices`): what the patient has been charged.\n- **Receipts** (`/receipts`): what the patient has paid.\n- **Refunds** (`/revenue-cycle/refunds`): money going back to the patient, with an approval step.\n- **Patient Ledger** (`/patient-ledger`): one patient's running balance, every document in date order.\n- **Credit Notes** (`/revenue-cycle/credit-notes`) and **Debit Notes** (`/revenue-cycle/debit-notes`):\n  corrections that lower or raise what a patient owes.\n- **Billing Workspace** (`/billing-workspace`): a patient's invoices and a payment box side by side,\n  for the cashier's desk.\n- **Medical Coding** (`/medical-coding`): the coder's inbox of diagnosis and procedure coding sessions.\n\nHow the pieces fit:\n\n1. Clinical work posts **charges** against the patient (automatically, through the rules and catalog\n   mappings described in [Billing setup](billing-setup.md), or by hand on an invoice line).\n2. An **invoice** gathers charges into a bill. Its lines lock those charges in as billed.\n3. A **receipt** records money taken. Allocating it to an invoice reduces that invoice's balance.\n4. **Credit notes**, **debit notes**, **refunds** and manual **adjustments** correct the balance.\n5. The **Patient Ledger** shows the net effect of all of it.\n\nEvery money figure is shown with two decimals and the currency code after it, such as\n\"150.00 AED\". The currency is your organisation's **finance.currency** setting; AED is used when\nnone is set.\n\n## Invoices\n\n**Invoices** (`/invoices`) lists invoices raised on the chosen day.\n\nAbove the table:\n\n- **Search** by invoice number, patient name or MRN. The search only looks through the invoices\n  already loaded for the date and status you picked.\n- **Status**: **All statuses**, **Unpaid**, **Partial**, **Paid** or **Cancelled**.\n- **Date**: **Today** (the default), **Yesterday** or **Date Range**. With **Date Range** a button\n  appears showing the range; press it to pick a start and end date on a two-month calendar.\n\nThe columns are **Invoice #**, **Patient** (name, MRN, gender and age), **Date**, **Net amount**,\n**Balance** and **Status**. Click a row, or **View**, to open the invoice.\n\n| Status | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Unpaid** | Orange | Nothing paid yet. |\n| **Partial** | Amber | Some paid, a balance remains. |\n| **Paid** | Green | Balance is zero or less. |\n| **Cancelled** | Rose | Cancelled; its charges are free to bill again. |\n\nIf the list cannot be read, a red box says \"Failed to load invoices:\" followed by the reason.\n\n### Raising an invoice\n\n**Go to:** **Invoices**, then **New invoice** (`/invoices/new`). The page is headed **Create invoice**\nwith the currency beside it.\n\n1. **Patient**: search by name, MRN or mobile and pick the patient.\n2. **Encounter** (optional): the patient's encounters are listed with date, time, doctor and status.\n   Narrow them with **Date** and **Doctor name**, then click one. Press **Change** to pick another.\n   When you pick an encounter, its **unbilled charges** are loaded as invoice lines straight away,\n   replacing whatever lines you had.\n3. **Invoice date** (today by default), **Due date** (optional) and **Amount paid** (anything already\n   collected, 0 by default).\n4. **Invoice lines**: each line needs a **Charge Item**. Type in **Search billing items...** and pick\n   one; its description and list price fill in. Adjust **Qty**, **Unit price** and **Discount**.\n   **Line total** is quantity times price less discount, never below zero. **Add line** adds a row;\n   **Remove** deletes one (the last row cannot be removed).\n5. Check the totals box: **Gross amount**, **Total discounts**, **Net amount**, **Amount paid** and\n   **Balance due**.\n6. Press **Create invoice**.\n\n**Check:** a toast says \"Invoice created\" and you return to the list. The invoice number is made\nfrom your organisation's invoice number format, prefix and start number settings. The status is set\nfrom the figures: **Paid** if the balance is zero or less, **Partial** if something was paid,\notherwise **Unpaid**. Lines you added by hand become new manual charges for the patient.\n\n**If instead:** pressing **Create invoice** does nothing. The screen gives no message; check that:\n\n- a patient is selected;\n- every line has a **Charge Item** picked (a blank line blocks the whole invoice, so remove it);\n- your organisation's invoice numbering settings (**finance.invoice_number_format**,\n  **finance.invoice_prefix**, **finance.invoice_start_number**) are set. Without them no number can\n  be made and nothing is saved. Ask an administrator.\n\n### Working an invoice\n\nOpen an invoice (`/invoices/{id}`). The header shows **Invoice #**, the net amount in large type\nand the status badge. Three cards show the **Patient**, the **Invoice date** (with \"Due\" and the due\ndate if one was set) and the **Balance due**.\n\n- **Invoice summary**: **Gross amount**, **Discounts**, **Net amount**, **Amount paid**,\n  **Balance due**.\n- **Invoice lines**: **#**, **Description** (with the charge it bills), **Qty**, **Unit**,\n  **Discount** and **Amount**.\n\n**Record payment**: enter an **Amount** and an optional **Reference** (receipt or transaction\nnumber), then **Record payment**. The invoice's paid amount and balance update and its status\nmoves to **Partial** or **Paid**. The screen refuses a blank or zero amount with \"Enter a payment\namount\". The server refuses payment on a cancelled invoice.\n\nRecording a payment here changes the invoice's figures only. It does not create a receipt, so it\ndoes not appear on **Receipts** or in the **Patient Ledger**. To take money with a receipt, use\n[Billing Workspace](#billing-workspace) or [Receipts](#receipts) instead.\n\n**Update status**: pick a status and press **Update status**. This sets the badge only; it does not\nchange any amount.\n\n**Cancel invoice** (under **Danger zone**): confirm the prompt \"Cancel this invoice? (Only unpaid\ninvoices can be cancelled)\". The invoice becomes **Cancelled**, its charges go back to unbilled so\nthey can be invoiced again, and the ledger entry is reversed. The button is greyed out once the\ninvoice is cancelled. The server refuses to cancel an invoice that has any amount paid (\"Cannot\ncancel invoice with payments\").\n\nWhen the server refuses a payment, status change or cancellation, the screen does not show the\nreason; the invoice simply stays as it was.\n\n## Receipts\n\n**Receipts** (`/receipts`) lists receipts taken on the chosen day. It filters like Invoices:\n**Search** (receipt number, patient name or MRN), **Payment method** (**All**, **Cash**, **Card**,\n**UPI**, **Bank transfer**, **Wallet**) and the **Today**, **Yesterday** or **Date Range** filter.\n\nThe columns are **Receipt #**, **Patient**, **Date**, **Amount** and **Method**. Click a row or\n**View** to open it.\n\n### Taking a receipt\n\n**Go to:** **Receipts**, then **New receipt** (`/receipts/new`), headed **Create receipt**.\n\n1. **Patient**: search and pick.\n2. **Invoice ID** (optional): the invoice's internal ID. This only records which invoice the\n   receipt is for; it does not reduce the invoice's balance. Use an allocation for that (step 6).\n3. **Receipt date**, today by default.\n4. **Paid amount** and **Paid currency** (both required). The currency starts as your base\n   currency.\n5. **FX rate to** (your base currency): needed only when the patient paid in another currency. It is\n   fixed at 1 and greyed out while the paid currency matches the base currency. The receipt is\n   stored in the base currency at paid amount times rate.\n6. **Payment method**, **Transaction reference** (such as the card machine's transaction ID) and\n   **Notes**.\n7. **Allocations (optional)**: to settle invoices, enter each **Invoice ID** and **Amount**. **Add\n   allocation** adds a row. Rows with no invoice or a zero amount are ignored.\n8. Press **Save receipt**.\n\n**Check:** \"Receipt created\" and you return to the list. Each allocation adds to that invoice's\namount paid and lowers its balance, moving it to **Partial** or **Paid**. The receipt is posted to\nthe patient's ledger.\n\n**If instead:** nothing happens on **Save receipt** when no patient is chosen, the paid amount is\nzero or blank, the rate is zero, or the receipt numbering settings\n(**finance.receipt_number_format**, **finance.receipt_prefix**, **finance.receipt_start_number**)\nare missing. The server refuses allocations that add up to more than the receipt (\"Total allocated\namount (...) exceeds receipt amount (...)\").\n\n### Working a receipt\n\nOpen a receipt (`/receipts/{id}`): **Receipt #**, the amount, the payment method badge, and cards\nfor **Patient**, **Receipt date** and **Reference**.\n\n- **Edit receipt**: the same form as above, with **Update receipt**. A toast says \"Receipt updated\".\n- **Allocations**: the allocations already made (**Invoice**, **Amount**, **Allocated at**), or \"No\n  allocations recorded.\" Below, add more: **Invoice ID** and **Amount** per row, **Add another\n  invoice** for more rows, then **Allocate**. With no usable row the screen says \"Add at least one\n  allocation\". On success, \"Receipt allocated\".\n- **Delete receipt** (under **Danger zone**): confirm \"Delete this receipt?\". The server refuses to\n  delete a receipt that has allocations (\"Cannot delete receipt with allocations. Remove allocations\n  first.\"), and this screen has no way to remove an allocation, so in practice only unallocated\n  receipts can be deleted.\n\n## Refunds\n\n**Refunds** (`/revenue-cycle/refunds`) lists refunds for the chosen day. Filter by **Search** (refund\nnumber, patient name or MRN), **Status**, **Method** and the date filter. The columns are\n**Refund #**, **Patient**, **Date**, **Amount**, **Method** and **Status**.\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Pending** | Raised, waiting for approval. Can be deleted. |\n| **Approved** | Approved, waiting to be paid out. |\n| **Rejected** | Turned down, with a reason. |\n| **Processed** | Paid out. Invoice balances and the ledger are updated. |\n| **Voided** | A processed refund reversed. |\n\nMethods are **Cash**, **Card reversal**, **Bank transfer**, **Cheque**, **Wallet** and **Other**.\n\n### Raising a refund\n\n**Go to:** **Refunds**, then **New refund** (`/revenue-cycle/refunds/new`).\n\n1. **Patient**: search and pick.\n2. **Refund Date** (today by default) and **Receipt ID (optional)** to tie the refund to a receipt.\n3. **Amount** (required, in the base currency) and **Refund Method**.\n4. **Transaction Reference**, **Reason for Refund** and **Notes** as needed.\n5. Press **Create Refund**.\n\n**Check:** \"Refund created\" with the new refund number, and the refund opens as **Pending**.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"Please select a patient\" when no patient is chosen.\n- \"Amount must be greater than 0\".\n- \"Failed to create refund\" with the server's reason, for example when the amount is more than is\n  left unrefunded on the linked receipt (\"Refund amount (...) exceeds unrefunded receipt balance\n  (...)\").\n\n### Approving and paying out a refund\n\nOpen the refund (`/revenue-cycle/refunds/{id}`). The **Actions** card offers only the next step for\nits status:\n\n1. **Pending**: **Approve** (optional notes; \"Refund approved\") or **Reject** (a **Reason** is\n   required; \"Rejection reason is required\" if you leave it blank; \"Refund rejected\").\n2. **Approved**: **Process**, with an optional **Transaction Reference** and **Notes**. \"Refund\n   processed\". Processing reverses the payment on each allocated invoice, raising its balance due,\n   and posts the refund to the ledger.\n3. **Processed**: **Void**, with a required **Reason** (\"Void reason is required\"). The dialog warns\n   that voiding will reverse invoice balance changes. \"Refund voided\".\n\nAny refusal from the server shows as \"Failed to approve\", \"Failed to reject\", \"Failed to process\" or\n\"Failed to void\" with the reason.\n\nThe page also shows the **Patient**, **Refund date**, **Reference**, a **Details** card (reason,\nnotes, and any **Rejection Reason** in red), the **Linked Receipt**, the **Allocations** to invoices\nand an **Audit Trail** of every action with its time. A pending refund can be removed under\n**Danger zone** with **Delete refund** (confirm \"Delete this refund?\").\n\n## Patient Ledger\n\n**Patient Ledger** (`/patient-ledger`) shows one patient's financial history and running balance.\nSearch for the patient first; nothing else shows until you do.\n\nThree cards head the page:\n\n- **Total Debits** (red): charges, refunds, debit adjustments.\n- **Total Credits** (green): payments and credit notes.\n- **Outstanding Balance**: red with \"Patient owes\" when above zero, green with \"Patient credit\" when\n  zero or below.\n\n**Transaction History** lists every entry: **Date**, **Type**, **Doc No**, **Description**,\n**Debit** (red), **Credit** (green), **Balance** after that entry (red when owed, green when not),\nand **Status**. Filter by **Entry Type** (**Invoice**, **Receipt**, **Credit Note**, **Debit Note**,\n**Adjustment**, **Refund**, **Opening Balance**) and **From Date** / **To Date**. The **Doc No** of\nan invoice, receipt or refund links to that document.\n\nEntries reach the ledger by themselves: an invoice when created, a receipt when saved, a credit or\ndebit note when posted, a refund when processed. Cancelling or voiding the document reverses its\nentry.\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Draft** | Not yet counted. |\n| **Posted** | Counted in the balance. Can be reversed. |\n| **Reversed** | Offset by a reversal entry. |\n| **Void** | Cancelled. |\n\n### Making a manual adjustment\n\n1. Press **Create Adjustment**.\n2. Choose **Credit (reduce balance)** or **Debit (increase balance)**.\n3. Enter the **Amount** and a **Reason**; **Notes (optional)**.\n4. Press **Create Adjustment**.\n\n**Check:** \"Adjustment created\" and a posted **Adjustment** row appears.\n\n**If instead:** \"Enter a valid amount.\" or \"Please provide a reason.\"; a server refusal shows as\n\"Failed to create adjustment\" with the reason.\n\n### Reversing an entry\n\nA **Posted** row has a reverse button in **Actions**. The dialog shows the document, type and\namount; enter a **Reason for Reversal** and press **Reverse Entry**. A reversal entry is added to\noffset the original. Without a reason: \"Please provide a reason for reversal.\"\n\n## Credit Notes and Debit Notes\n\nA **credit note** lowers what a patient owes (for example, a service not given). A **debit note**\nraises it (an extra charge after the invoice). The two screens work the same way.\n\n**Credit Notes** (`/revenue-cycle/credit-notes`) and **Debit Notes** (`/revenue-cycle/debit-notes`)\nlist the notes with **Credit note #** / **Debit note #**, **Patient**, **Amount** and **Status**\n(**Draft**, **Posted**, **Voided**). Search with **Search credit notes...** or **Search debit\nnotes...**. Use **View** to open one.\n\n### Raising a note\n\n**Go to:** **New credit note** (`/revenue-cycle/credit-notes/new`) or **New debit note**\n(`/revenue-cycle/debit-notes/new`).\n\n1. **Patient**: search and pick.\n2. **Invoice (optional)**: press it to open **Select Invoice**, listing the patient's invoices with\n   **Date**, **Status**, **Net Amount**, **Paid** and **Balance Due**. It reads \"Select a patient\n   first\" until a patient is chosen.\n3. **Date**, **Reason** and **Notes**.\n4. **Line items**: a **Description** and **Amount** per line. **Add line** for more; **Remove** to\n   delete one. The running **Total** shows above the lines.\n5. Press **Create credit note** or **Create debit note**.\n\n**Check:** the note opens as **Draft**. A draft does not touch the patient's balance yet.\n\nThe server refuses a note whose total is zero (\"Amount must be greater than 0\").\n\n### Posting and voiding\n\nOpen the note (`/revenue-cycle/credit-notes/{id}` or `/revenue-cycle/debit-notes/{id}`). The status\nbadge is yellow for **Draft**, green for **Posted** and red for **Voided**. The page shows the\namount (green for a credit note, red for a debit note), patient, date, a **View Invoice** link if\nan invoice was chosen, reason, notes and the **Line Items** with their **Total**.\n\n- A **Draft** shows a yellow card: \"This credit note is in draft status\" (or debit note). Press\n  **Post Credit Note** / **Post Debit Note** to apply it to the patient's ledger, or **Delete** to\n  remove it (confirm \"Are you sure you want to delete this credit note?\").\n- A **Posted** note shows **Void Credit Note** / **Void Debit Note**. Enter a **Reason for voiding**\n  and press the button; the entry is reversed from the ledger. Without a reason: \"Void reason is\n  required\".\n- A **Voided** note says \"This credit note has been voided\" (or debit note) with the reason.\n\nOnly drafts can be posted or deleted, and only posted notes can be voided.\n\n## Billing Workspace\n\n**Billing Workspace** (`/billing-workspace`) puts a patient's invoices beside a payment box, so the\ncashier can find a bill and take payment without leaving the page.\n\n1. In **Search Patient**, find the patient. Their invoices appear with **Invoice**, **Amount**,\n   **Balance** and **Status** (**Unpaid**, **Partially paid**, **Paid**, **Cancelled**). Narrow them\n   with the status filter. \"No invoices found for this patient.\" if there are none.\n2. Click an invoice. The first one is selected for you. **Invoice detail** shows the net amount,\n   status, **Patient**, **Balance due**, **Invoice date** and up to five **Recently linked\n   receipts** for this patient.\n3. In **Collect payment**, enter the **Amount** (the balance due shows as a hint), the **Payment\n   method**, an optional **Reference** and **Notes**.\n4. Press **Record payment**.\n\n**Check:** \"Payment recorded\" with \"Receipt created and allocated.\" A receipt is created for the\nfull amount and allocated to the selected invoice, so its balance and status update and the\npayment shows on **Receipts** and the **Patient Ledger**.\n\n**If instead:** \"Enter an amount to collect.\" for a blank or zero amount.\n\nReceipts made here are numbered \"RCPT-\" followed by a timestamp, not with your organisation's\nreceipt number format.\n\n## Medical Coding\n\n**Medical coding** (`/medical-coding`) is the coder's inbox, assignments and productivity.\n\n- Four tiles: **Total sessions**, **Pending**, **In review** and **Submitted**.\n- **Coder productivity**: per coder, sessions completed, diagnoses and procedures coded. \"No\n  productivity data.\" when there is none.\n- **Pending inbox**: up to six pending sessions as buttons; click one to open it.\n- **Coding sessions**: the full list with **Patient** (shortened patient and encounter IDs),\n  **Status**, **Priority**, **Coder** (or **Unassigned**) and **Updated**. Search by patient or\n  encounter ID, filter by status, or type a **Queue** name. **Review** opens the session.\n\nStatuses are **New**, **Pending**, **In Review**, **Returned**, **Completed** and **Submitted**.\n\n### Coding a session\n\nOpen a session (`/medical-coding/{id}`). **Session summary** shows the patient ID, encounter ID,\ncoder and last update.\n\n1. For a **Pending** session, press **Start review**. \"Review started\".\n2. **Diagnoses**: under **Add diagnosis**, enter the **Code**, **System** (ICD-10 by default) and\n   **Description**, switch on **Primary diagnosis** if it is, and press **Add diagnosis**. In the\n   list, **Toggle primary** flips the primary flag and **Delete** removes a code.\n3. **Procedures**: under **Add procedure**, enter **Code**, **System** (CPT by default),\n   **Description**, **Modifier** and **Units**, then **Add procedure**. **+1 unit** adds a unit;\n   **Delete** removes it.\n4. **Coder notes**: type and press **Save notes** (\"Session updated\").\n5. Press **Submit session**. \"Session submitted for claim generation\".\n\nA code field left blank means the add button does nothing. The **Audit trail** at the bottom lists\neach action, who did it, notes and when.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Credit and debit notes**: the **Edit** button on a draft note links to an edit page that does\n  not exist yet. To change a draft, delete it and raise it again. The list pages do not open a note\n  when you click the row; use **View**.\n- **Invoices and receipts**: invoice and receipt IDs for allocations and the receipt's **Invoice\n  ID** must be typed in as internal IDs; there is no invoice picker on these forms.\n- **Invoice detail**: when the server refuses a payment, status change or cancellation, no message\n  is shown.\n- **Receipts**: an allocated receipt cannot be deleted, and there is no way to remove an allocation\n  on screen.\n- **Medical coding**: patients, encounters and coders show as internal IDs, not names. There is no\n  button to create or assign a session on these screens.\n",
  "clinical-charting.md": "---\ntitle: 'Charting and triage'\ngroup: 'Clinical care'\norder: 30\nroutes: ['/charting', '/triage']\ndescription: 'Triaging an arrival on the Triage Queue and Triage Assessment, then charting the encounter in Smart Charting: notes, diagnoses, orders, prescriptions, AI coding suggestions and structured forms.'\n---\n\nBoth screens sit under **Clinical** in the sidebar: **Clinical Charting** and **Triage**. They work on\nencounters, so the patient must already be registered and have an encounter open (see\n[Patient access](patient-access.md)).\n\nThe usual order for a visit is:\n\n1. The nurse finds the arrival on the **Triage Queue** and records the triage.\n2. The clinician finds the same encounter on **Clinical Charting** and opens the charting workspace.\n3. The clinician writes the note, adds diagnoses, orders and prescriptions, and fills any forms.\n\nNothing forces that order. You can chart an encounter that was never triaged; the triage summary in the\nchart header simply does not appear.\n\n## Triage Queue\n\n**Triage Queue** (`/triage`) lists encounters so you can pick the one to triage. It opens on **Today**.\n\n**Go to:** **Clinical** > **Triage** in the sidebar.\n\nThe filters along the top:\n\n- **Search by patient or MRN**: type a name or MRN. The list narrows as you type.\n- The status filter: **All statuses**, **Arrived**, **Triaged** or **In progress**. **All statuses**\n  shows every encounter in the date window, including finished and cancelled ones.\n- The date filter: **Today**, **Yesterday** or **Date Range**. Choosing **Date Range** shows a button with\n  the range on it; press it to pick a start and end date on a two-month calendar. Pick one day for a\n  single-day range.\n\nEach row shows:\n\n| Column | What it shows |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Patient** | Name, then MRN and gender / age underneath. |\n| **Primary staff** | The encounter's primary clinician, or **Unassigned**. |\n| **Encounter class** | The class the encounter was opened with, as a grey badge. |\n| **Start time** | When the encounter started. |\n| **Status** | The encounter status as a coloured badge (see below). |\n| **Actions** | **Open triage**. |\n\nThe list shows 15 encounters a page. When there is more than one page, **Previous** and **Next** appear\nunder the table with \"Showing _n_ of _total_ encounters\".\n\n### Status colours\n\nThe same badge colours are used on the Triage Queue and on Clinical Charting.\n\n| Status | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **PLANNED** | Blue | Booked, patient not yet arrived. |\n| **ARRIVED** | Primary (theme colour) | Checked in, waiting for triage. |\n| **TRIAGED** | Yellow | Triage recorded, waiting for the clinician. |\n| **IN PROGRESS** | Green | Being seen. |\n| **FINISHED** | Grey | Encounter closed. |\n| **CANCELLED** | Red | Encounter cancelled. |\n\nAny other status shows in a neutral grey badge.\n\n**If instead** the list is empty, the screen says \"No encounters match your filters.\" with a hint:\n\n- \"Try adjusting your search or status filter.\" when you have typed a search or chosen a status.\n- \"No encounters scheduled for today. Try selecting yesterday or a date range.\" on **Today**.\n- \"No encounters scheduled for yesterday. Try selecting today or a date range.\" on **Yesterday**.\n- \"No encounters in the selected date range. Try widening your range.\" on **Date Range**.\n\nIf the encounters cannot be loaded, a red **Unable to load encounters** box shows the reason.\n\n## Triage Assessment\n\n**Triage Assessment** (`/encounters/{id}/triage`) is where you record the triage for one encounter. Open it\nwith **Open triage** on the queue. The back arrow beside the title returns you to the **Triage Queue**.\n\nThe top card names the patient and shows the encounter number, class and status, then **Age**, **Gender**\nand **MRN**. Underneath are the **Start time** and, once a triage exists, **Last triaged** with the time it\nwas last saved.\n\n### Record a triage\n\n1. Choose the **Triage level**. It starts on **Level 5 · Non-urgent**. The levels are:\n\n   | Level | Label |\n   | --- | --- |\n   | 1 | **Level 1 · Critical** |\n   | 2 | **Level 2 · Emergency** |\n   | 3 | **Level 3 · Urgent** |\n   | 4 | **Level 4 · Semi-urgent** |\n   | 5 | **Level 5 · Non-urgent** |\n\n2. Check the **Triage clinician**. It is filled with the encounter's primary clinician; change it to\n   whoever did the triage. Any active staff member can be chosen.\n3. Enter a **Pain score (0-10)** if you have one. It is optional.\n4. Write the **Chief complaint & HPI**. This is the one field you must fill.\n5. Enter the **Vital signs** you took. Every vital is optional; leave blank what you did not measure.\n6. Add **Allergies**: **Allergen**, **Reaction** and **Severity** for each. Press **Add allergy** for\n   another row and **Remove** to drop one (shown once there is more than one row). A row with no\n   allergen is ignored on save.\n7. Add **Current medications**: **Name**, **Dosage** and **Frequency**. **Add medication** and **Remove**\n   work the same way. A row with no name is ignored.\n8. Add **Additional notes** for observations or quick interventions.\n9. Press **Save triage**. The button reads **Saving...** while it works.\n\n**Check:** the toast says **Triage created** (\"Triage record saved successfully.\"), the footer now shows\nan **Existing triage record** badge, and the button changes to **Update triage**. If the encounter was\n**ARRIVED**, it moves to **TRIAGED**, which you will see on both queues.\n\n**If instead** nothing happens when you press **Save triage**, a required field is empty: the label of\n**Chief complaint & HPI** (or **Triage clinician**) turns red. Fill it and save again. If the server\nrefuses, the toast reads **Unable to save triage** with the server's reason, or \"Please try again later.\"\n\n### Which vital signs you see\n\nThe vital sign fields come from a vital signs template when one fits the encounter. The screen chooses\nthe care setting from the encounter class (emergency, ICU, inpatient, day care, otherwise outpatient)\nand the age group from the patient's date of birth (newborn, infant, child, adolescent, adult, elderly).\nWhen a template is found, a badge says **Loaded from template _code_** and the fields are the\ntemplate's, labelled as the template labels them.\n\nWith no matching template you get the standard set: **Temperature**, **Heart rate**, **Respiratory\nrate**, **Systolic BP**, **Diastolic BP**, **SpO₂ %**, **Weight**, **Height** and **BMI**. BMI is\ntyped in; it is not worked out from height and weight.\n\n### Change a triage\n\nOpen the encounter's triage again. The form loads what was saved. Change what you need and press\n**Update triage**.\n\n**Check:** the toast says **Triage updated** and **Last triaged** shows the new time.\n\nAn encounter has one triage. Updating replaces the saved values; the screen keeps no earlier versions.\n\n## Clinical Charting\n\n**Clinical Charting** (`/charting`) is the list you start charting from. It works exactly like the\nTriage Queue: the same **Search by patient or MRN** box, date filter (**Today**, **Yesterday**,\n**Date Range**), columns, status colours, empty-list messages and 15-a-page paging.\n\n**Go to:** **Clinical** > **Clinical Charting** in the sidebar.\n\nThe differences:\n\n- The status filter offers every status: **All statuses**, **In Progress**, **Arrived**, **Triaged**,\n  **Planned**, **Finished** and **Cancelled**.\n- Each row has two actions:\n  - **AI+** opens Patient AI+ for the patient, where you can read an AI summary and copy a draft into the\n    chart (see [Insert an AI draft](#insert-an-ai-draft)).\n  - **Open charting** opens the charting workspace. **It is greyed out for a FINISHED encounter**, so a\n    closed encounter cannot be charted from here.\n\n## The charting workspace\n\nThe charting workspace (`/encounters/{id}/charting`) has three parts, top to bottom: the patient header,\n**Smart Charting**, and **Forms**.\n\n**If instead** the page says **Encounter not found** (\"Please return to encounters and select another\nrecord.\"), the encounter id is wrong or no longer reachable. Go back to **Clinical Charting** and pick the\nencounter from the list.\n\n### The patient header\n\nThe header card says **Clinical Charting** and the encounter number, then the **Patient**, **Age**,\n**Gender**, **MRN** and encounter **Status**.\n\nWhen the encounter has been triaged, the right of the header also shows:\n\n- **Triage vitals**: a one-line summary such as \"Temp 37.2°C · HR 88 bpm · BP 120/80 · SpO2 98%\", or\n  \"No vitals recorded\".\n- **Allergies**: each allergen with its severity and reaction, or \"No allergies documented\".\n\nThese come from the triage and are read-only here. To correct them, change the triage (see\n[Change a triage](#change-a-triage)).\n\n## Smart Charting\n\n**Smart Charting** is the encounter note. It is built from blocks, one of each kind, which you add as you\nneed them. A badge by the **Save** button counts the blocks, for example **3 blocks**.\n\nThere are six kinds of block, each with a coloured bar down its left edge:\n\n| Block | Bar colour | What it holds | How it saves |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Chief Complaints and HPI** | Blue | Free text: presenting complaints and history of present illness. | With **Save**. |\n| **History** | Amber | Free text: past medical, surgical, family or social history. | With **Save**. |\n| **Notes** | Slate | Free text: clinical notes and observations. | With **Save**. |\n| **Diagnosis** | Rose | ICD-10 diagnoses from the catalogue. | Each one at once. |\n| **Orders** | Theme colour | Lab tests, imaging studies, procedures and packages. | Each one at once. |\n| **Prescription** | Emerald | Medicines from the medication catalogue. | Each one at once. |\n\n**The two kinds save differently.** Text in the three text blocks is kept only when you press **Save**.\nA diagnosis, order or prescription is recorded against the encounter the moment you pick it, and a toast\nconfirms it. There is no autosave and no warning if you leave with unsaved text, so press **Save** before\nyou go.\n\n### Add a block\n\n1. Press **Add Block** under the note, or press **⌘/** (**Ctrl+/** on Windows) anywhere on the page.\n2. Pick the block from the menu. Each entry has a short description.\n\nThe menu only offers blocks the note does not have yet. Once all six are in, **Add Block** disappears.\nWhile the note is empty you cannot type into it; the line \"Press ⌘/ or click the button to add blocks\"\nreminds you to add a block first.\n\nNew blocks go to the bottom of the note. A new text block puts your cursor straight into it.\n\n### Remove a block\n\nHover over the block's heading and press the bin icon at its right.\n\nRemoving a text block removes its text from the note when you next **Save**. **Removing a Diagnosis,\nOrders or Prescription block does not remove what is in it.** The diagnoses, orders and prescriptions\nstay on the encounter; add the block again and they are all still listed. To take one off, remove it\ninside the block (see below).\n\n### Save the note\n\nPress **Save** at the top right. It reads **Saving...** while it works.\n\n**Check:** the toast says **Saved** (\"Smart charting content saved successfully.\") and the toolbar shows\n**Saved just now**, which later reads \"Saved 5m ago\" and then the time.\n\n**If instead** the toast says **Unable to save**, the message under it is the reason. Your text is still\non screen; try **Save** again.\n\nThe note is filed under the encounter's primary clinician as a progress note titled \"Smart Charting\".\nOpening the chart again reloads the saved blocks.\n\n### Diagnosis\n\n1. Choose the type in the box beside the search: **primary**, **secondary**, **rule out** or\n   **differential**. It starts on **primary** and applies to the next diagnosis you add.\n2. Type into **Search ICD catalog...**. Matches show the description, the code and its chapter or category.\n3. Click a match.\n\n**Check:** the toast says **Diagnosis added** (\"_code_ attached to encounter.\") and the diagnosis appears\nunder the search with its code, its type badge and, for a chronic condition, a red **Chronic** badge.\n\nDiagnoses are ranked in the order you add them, and recorded as diagnosed by the encounter's primary\nclinician.\n\n**If instead**:\n\n- A code already on the encounter is greyed out in the results and cannot be picked again. If it does\n  get through, the toast reads **Diagnosis already added**.\n- \"No diagnoses found.\" means nothing in your ICD catalogue matches what you typed.\n- **Unable to add diagnosis** gives the server's reason.\n\nTo remove a diagnosis, hover over it and press its bin icon. The toast says **Diagnosis removed**.\n\n### Orders\n\n1. Choose what to order with the pills at the top: **Lab**, **Imaging**, **Procedure** or **Package**.\n   The line under the search says what the choice does, for example \"Add lab tests directly to the\n   encounter.\" or \"Packages create grouped orders for the encounter.\"\n2. Type into the search (**Search lab tests...**, **Search imaging studies...**, **Search procedures...** or\n   **Search packages...**).\n3. Click a match.\n\n**Check:** for a single order the toast says **Order added** (\"_name_ created.\"). For a package it says\n**Package added** (\"_name_ expanded into _n_ clinical orders.\").\n\nEvery order from this screen is placed at **Routine** priority and recorded as ordered by the\nencounter's primary clinician. There is no priority choice here.\n\nThe list under the search shows:\n\n- **Packages** first, in a box with an amber **Package** badge, the package name and code, how many\n  orders it made and of what kind (\"2 lab · 1 imaging\"), and its status. Press the arrow by the name to\n  show the orders inside it.\n- **Single orders**, each with a coloured type badge (Lab green, Imaging sky blue, Procedure violet), the\n  name and the code.\n- A blue **Results** link on any order that is completed or has a result. It opens that result (see\n  [Imaging and procedure results](results-reporting.md) and [Laboratory](lab.md)).\n\nTo take an order off, press its bin icon: the toast says **Order removed**. The bin on a package\n**cancels the package and its open orders**: the toast says **Package cancelled** (\"_name_ and its open\nchild orders were cancelled.\").\n\n**If instead**:\n\n- An item already on the encounter, or a package already added, is greyed out in the results. If it\n  does get through, the toast reads **Order already added** or **Package already added**.\n- \"No matches found.\" means nothing in the catalogue matches.\n- **Unable to add order**, **Unable to add package**, **Unable to remove order** or **Unable to cancel\n  package** give the server's reason.\n\n### Prescription\n\n1. Type into **Search medications...**. A list opens under the box as you type.\n2. Each medicine shows its name, code system and code, and the dose, route and frequency it will be\n   prescribed with.\n3. Click a medicine.\n\n**Check:** the toast says **Prescription added** (\"_name_ created.\") and the medicine appears in the list\nwith its status, dose, route and frequency.\n\n**The dose, route and frequency come from the medication catalogue and cannot be changed here.** A\nmedicine with no strength is prescribed \"As directed\", with no route \"oral\", and with no default\nfrequency \"once daily\". The prescription is recorded under the encounter's primary clinician.\n\n**If instead**:\n\n- A medicine already prescribed on the encounter is faded and says **Already prescribed**. If it does\n  get through, the toast reads **Prescription already exists**.\n- \"No medications found.\" means nothing in the medication catalogue matches.\n- **Unable to add prescription** gives the server's reason.\n\nTo remove a prescription, hover over it and press its bin icon. The toast says **Prescription removed**.\n\nFor dispensing, see [Pharmacy](pharmacy.md).\n\n### Insert an AI draft\n\nIf you used **Copy AI Summary to Chart** on Patient AI+ for this encounter, the chart opens with a violet\nbanner: **AI-drafted content is ready for this encounter**, with when it was generated.\n\n- **Insert AI Draft** adds the drafted **Chief Complaints and HPI**, **History** and **Notes** as new\n  blocks, skipping any of those the note already has. The toast says **AI draft inserted** (\"Review the\n  inserted content, then Save when ready.\").\n- **Dismiss** throws the draft away.\n\nNothing is saved until you press **Save**. The draft is held only in your browser tab, so it is lost if\nyou close the tab before inserting it.\n\n### AI Coding Suggestions\n\nShown when your organisation has AI clinical coding switched on.\n\nThe **AI Coding Suggestions** panel runs down the right of Smart Charting. It reads only the three text\nblocks (never diagnoses, orders or prescriptions) and suggests codes once you have written enough text\n(20 characters or more). It waits for you to pause typing before it asks.\n\nEach suggestion card shows:\n\n- the code, in blue for ICD-10;\n- the code system;\n- a type badge when it is not a diagnosis: **history** (amber), **symptom** (orange), **finding**\n  (teal) or **procedure**;\n- a red **Not in catalog** badge when the ICD-10 code is not in your catalogue, so it cannot be added;\n- a short description and the reason it was suggested;\n- a confidence bar: green at 80% or more, yellow from 50%, red below.\n\nPress **+** on an ICD-10 suggestion that is in your catalogue to add it as a **primary** diagnosis. The\ntoast says **Diagnosis added**. Change its type afterwards only by removing it and adding it again from\nthe Diagnosis block with the right type.\n\nThe refresh icon asks again. The arrow collapses the panel to a thin **AI Coding** strip; your choice is\nremembered in this browser.\n\nThe panel's messages:\n\n- \"Start typing clinical notes to get AI-powered coding suggestions.\" until there is enough text.\n- \"No coding suggestions for the current text.\"\n- \"Failed to fetch suggestions.\" with **Retry**.\n\nWhen you press **Save**, the suggestions on screen are kept with the encounter, marked as accepted\n(those you added) or suggested.\n\n## Forms\n\nThe **Forms** card under Smart Charting holds structured forms for the encounter. The forms on offer are\nthe active ones in the form master catalogue (see [Administration](administration.md)).\n\n- Forms already started are listed with their name, when they were last changed, and a status badge:\n  **draft**, **final** or **amended**. Press **Continue** on a draft or **View** on the others.\n- Forms not yet started are listed below in dashed boxes with their version. Press **Fill** to start\n  one.\n- \"No forms available.\" means there are no active forms and none started.\n\nEach form can be started once per encounter. If starting fails, the toast reads **Unable to start form**.\n\n### Fill a form\n\nThe form opens on its own page (`/encounters/{id}/forms/{formResponseId}`) with its name, version and code,\nand its status.\n\n1. Fill the fields.\n2. Press **Save Draft** to keep your progress. The toast says **Draft saved**.\n3. Press **Submit** when it is complete.\n\n**Check:** the toast says **Form submitted** (\"The response has been recorded.\") and you are taken back to\nthe charting workspace, where the form now shows **final**.\n\n**If instead** the toast says **Form incomplete** (\"Please fix the highlighted fields before\nsubmitting.\"), the fields with problems are now highlighted. Fix them and submit again.\n\nA submitted form is read-only: the page says \"This form has been submitted as final.\" and the buttons\nare gone. The back arrow returns you to the charting workspace.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Drag handles.** Blocks show a grip icon by their heading on hover, but reordering blocks by dragging\n  has not been confirmed to work. New blocks always go to the bottom.\n- **Order priority and prescription details.** Smart Charting places every order as **Routine** and\n  every prescription with the catalogue's default dose, route and frequency. There is no way to change\n  these on this screen.\n- **Author.** Notes, diagnoses, orders and prescriptions are recorded under the encounter's primary\n  clinician, not under the person signed in.\n",
  "getting-started.md": "---\ntitle: 'Getting started'\ngroup: 'Getting started'\norder: 10\nroutes: ['/dashboard', '/profile']\ndescription: 'Signing in and resetting your password, the Health AI+ dashboard, the sidebar and topbar, switching facility, patient search, notifications, your profile, and how to use this Help Centre.'\n---\n\nThis page covers what every user meets before any clinical or billing work: signing in, the\n**Health AI+** dashboard you land on, the sidebar and the bar across the top of every screen, and\nthis manual itself. Each module then has its own page, listed in the Help Centre.\n\n## Signing in\n\n**Sign in** (`/login`) asks for your **Email** and **Password**.\n\n1. Open the app. If you are not signed in, any screen you open sends you to the sign-in page.\n2. Enter your **Email** and **Password**.\n3. Press **Sign in**.\n\n**Check:** a green toast says **Welcome back**, and you land on the **Health AI+** dashboard\n(`/dashboard`). The app always opens the dashboard after sign-in, even when a link sent you to\nsign in from another screen; open that screen again from the sidebar.\n\n**If instead:** the message under the password box says why, and a red **Authentication failed**\ntoast repeats it:\n\n- The server's own reason, such as a wrong password or a locked account, is shown as it sends it.\n  When there is no reason to show, the message is **Unable to authenticate.**\n- **Multi-factor authentication required. Enter your code.** means your account needs a\n  verification code. See [Not live yet](#not-live-yet).\n- **Login response missing tokens.** means the sign-in service answered without a session. Try\n  again, and tell your administrator if it repeats.\n\nThe email box must hold a valid email address and the password must be at least 6 characters, or\nthe form does not send.\n\nYour session lasts until its token expires. When it has, the next screen you open sends you back to\n**Sign in**.\n\n### Forgot your password\n\n**Password reset** (`/reset-password`) is the **Forgot password?** link under the sign-in form.\n\n1. Enter your **Email address**.\n2. Press **Send reset link**.\n\n**Check:** the screen says **If the email is registered, a reset link has been sent.** It says this\nwhether or not the address has an account, so the screen never reveals who has one. The email\nholds a reset token.\n\n**If instead:** **Unable to request reset right now. Please try again later.** means the request\ndid not reach the sign-in service.\n\n### Setting a new password with a reset token\n\n**Complete password reset** (`/confirm-reset`) is the **I have a reset token** link under the\nsign-in form.\n\n1. Paste the token from the email into **Reset token**.\n2. Enter your **New password**, at least 8 characters.\n3. Press **Update password**.\n\n**Check:** **Password updated. You can now sign in with your new credentials.** Press **Return to\nsign in**.\n\n**If instead:** **Invalid or expired token. Please request a new reset.** Go back to **Forgot\npassword?** and ask for a fresh one.\n\n### Signing out\n\nOpen the person icon at the right of the topbar and choose **Sign out**. A toast says **Signed out\nsuccessfully** and you return to **Sign in**. If the server could not be reached, the toast says\n**Signed out locally** instead: your session is still cleared from this browser, so you are safely\nsigned out here.\n\n## The Health AI+ dashboard\n\n**Health AI+** (`/dashboard`) is the first entry in the sidebar and where you land after signing\nin. It is a snapshot of today's activity and this month's money for your facility, drawn from the\nReport Builder.\n\n**Go to:** **Health AI+** in the sidebar, or the Athma logo at the top of the sidebar.\n\n### The figures across the top\n\n| Tile | What it counts |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Total Patients** | Every registered patient, with the change on last month. |\n| **Revenue (MTD)** | Revenue so far this month, in your organisation's currency, with the change on last month. |\n| **Appts Today** | Appointments booked for today. |\n| **Unpaid Invoices** | Invoices not yet paid. |\n\nThe change is shown as a percentage beside the figure, marked **vs last month**.\n\n### AI Operations Brief\n\nA sentence summing up the day: how many appointments are scheduled, how many are completed so far,\nthe encounters recorded today and the patients registered this month (those last two only when\nthere are any). Under it, three small panels:\n\n- **Completion Rate**: completed appointments as a share of today's, such as \"12/20 appointments\".\n- **Encounters Today**: encounters recorded today.\n- **Revenue (MTD)**: the month's revenue so far.\n\n### Suggested Actions\n\nUp to three suggestions worked out from the figures, each numbered and marked **AI\nrecommendation**:\n\n- **Follow up on unpaid invoices**, when more than five invoices are unpaid (**Action due today**).\n- **Monitor appointment progress**, when fewer than half of today's appointments are completed\n  (**High impact**).\n- **Welcome new patients**, when patients have registered this month (**Plan ahead**).\n\nWith none of these, it suggests **Review daily operations** (**Routine**). The suggestions are\nfixed rules on the figures, not a model's reading of your records, and nothing on the card acts by\nitself.\n\n### Risk & Compliance Radar\n\nUp to three signals, each with a severity and a badge:\n\n| Signal | Shown when | Badge |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Overdue Invoices** | Any invoice is more than 30 days overdue. | **Alert** above 10, otherwise **Watchlist** |\n| **Unpaid Balance** | Any invoice is unpaid; gives the total owed. | **Alert** above 20, otherwise **Watchlist** |\n| **Low Completion Rate** | Under 80% of today's appointments are completed. | **Alert** under 60%, otherwise **Watchlist** |\n| **Operations Normal** | None of the above. | **Info** |\n\n**Alert** is red; **Watchlist** and **Info** are grey.\n\n### How fresh the figures are\n\nThe badge beside the heading says **Live** or **Cached** and the time the figures were worked out.\nThe figures are kept on the server for a few minutes so the dashboard opens quickly, and the page\nreads them again every 5 minutes and whenever you come back to the browser tab. Press **Refresh**\nto have the server recount them now. While figures load, grey placeholders stand in for each card.\n\n## Finding your way: the sidebar\n\nThe sidebar down the left lists every module you can use. It starts with **Health AI+**, then a\ngroup per module. Press a group to open it and show its pages; press it again to close it. The group\nholding the screen you are on opens by itself, and that screen is highlighted in your theme's\ncolour.\n\n| Group | Pages | Help |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Patient Access** | **Patients** · **Encounters** · **Appointments** · **Staff Calendar** · **Staff Scheduling** | [Patient access](patient-access.md) |\n| **Clinical** | **Clinical Charting** · **Triage** | [Charting and triage](clinical-charting.md) |\n| **Operating Theatre** | **OT Board** · **OT Requests** · **OT Schedules** · **OT Reports** · **OT Rooms** | [Operating theatre](operating-theatre.md) |\n| **Lab** | **Lab Results** · **Lab Collection** · **Lab Operations** | [Laboratory](lab.md) |\n| **Results & Reporting** | **Imaging Results** · **Procedure Results** | [Imaging and procedure results](results-reporting.md) |\n| **Inpatient Care** | **Admission** · **Discharges** · **Discharge Summaries** · **Bed Browser** · **Ward Board** | [Inpatient care](inpatient-care.md) |\n| **Billing** | **Invoices** · **Receipts** · **Refunds** · **Patient Ledger** · **Credit Notes** · **Debit Notes** · **Billing Workspace** · **Medical Coding** | [Billing](billing.md) |\n| **Revenue Cycle** | **Claims** · **Batches** · **Eligibility** · **PreAuth** · **Remittance** | [Revenue cycle and claims](revenue-cycle.md) |\n| **Pharmacy** | **Dispense** · **Stock Management** · **Alerts** | [Pharmacy](pharmacy.md) |\n| **Wellness** | **Wellness Dashboard** · **Assessments** · **Assessment Templates** · **Biomarkers** · **Programs** · **Longevity** · **Lifestyle** · **Device Sync** | [Wellness](wellness.md) |\n| **Membership** | **Membership Dashboard** · **Plans** · **Subscriptions** | [Membership](membership.md) |\n| **Patient Engagement** | **Events** · **Tasks** · **Messages** | [Patient engagement](patient-engagement.md) |\n| **Analytics** | **Report Builder** · **Clinical Search** | [Reports and search](analytics.md) |\n| **Oncology** | **Oncology Registry** · **Tumor Staging** · **Tumor Board** · **Care Plans** · **Chemo Protocols** · **Chemo Orders** · **Treatment Schedules** · **Day-care Worklist** · **Radiation Therapy** · **Catalogs** · **Cancer Timeline** | [Oncology](oncology.md) |\n| **Administration** | **Configurations** · **ABDM Settings** · **Clinical Catalogs** · **RCM Setup** · **PE Setup** | [Administration](administration.md), [Billing setup](billing-setup.md) |\n\n### Why a group may be missing\n\nSome groups are switched on per organisation. **Lab**, **Results & Reporting**, **Inpatient\nCare**, **Revenue Cycle**, **Pharmacy**, **Wellness**, **Membership**, **Patient Engagement**,\n**Analytics** and **Oncology** appear only when your organisation has that module switched on. If\nyou cannot see one, ask your administrator: it is a setting, not a missing screen. While the\nsettings are loading, the sidebar briefly shows every group and then drops the ones that are off.\n\n### Collapsing to the icon rail\n\nThe small round arrow on the sidebar's right edge (**Collapse sidebar**) shrinks it to a narrow rail\nof icons, giving the screen more room. The same arrow (**Expand sidebar**) brings it back. It stays\nthe way you leave it as you move between screens, until you reload the page, when it opens full\nwidth again.\n\nOn the rail, each group is a single icon. Hover over it, or click it, and its pages open in a small\npanel beside the rail, headed with the group's name. Pick a page and the panel closes. The icon is\nhighlighted when the screen you are on belongs to that group.\n\n### Your name and facility\n\nThe foot of the sidebar shows your initials, your name and the facility you are working in. On the\nrail, hover over the initials to read them.\n\n### On a phone or small screen\n\nThe sidebar is hidden. Press the menu icon at the left of the topbar to slide it in, always full\nwidth. Tap outside it to close it.\n\n## The topbar\n\nThe bar across the top of every screen holds, from the middle to the right:\n\n- **Patient search** and **New Patient**.\n- The theme toggle (sun or moon).\n- **Notifications** (the bell).\n- The settings menu (the cog), with the administration screens.\n- Your user menu (the person icon), with the facility switcher, **Profile** and **Sign out**.\n\n### Finding a patient\n\nThe search box reads **Search patients by name, MRN, phone**.\n\n1. Click the box and start typing a name, MRN or phone number.\n2. Up to eight matches appear beneath it, each with the name, phone number (or **No phone**),\n   gender, age and MRN.\n3. Click one, or move with the up and down arrow keys and press Enter. Escape closes the list.\n\n**Check:** the patient's **Patient AI+** view opens (`/patients-ai/{id}`), and the box clears for\nthe next search.\n\n**If instead:** **No matching patients found.** Check the spelling or try the MRN.\n\nThe box shows a **⌘K** hint, but the shortcut does not work yet: click in the box instead.\n\n**New Patient** beside it opens patient registration (`/patients/new`). See\n[Patient access](patient-access.md).\n\n### Switching facility\n\nIf you work in more than one facility, open the person icon. Under **Switch Facility** each of your\nfacilities is listed with its type; your home facility says **(Home)** and the one you are in has a\ntick.\n\n1. Click the facility you want.\n2. A toast says **Facility switched** and the page reloads.\n\nEverything you then see and record belongs to the new facility, and the foot of the sidebar shows\nits name. **Unable to switch facility** means the server refused; its reason follows, or **Please\ntry again.** With only one facility, the **Switch Facility** list does not appear.\n\n### Notifications\n\nThe bell shows a red count of unread notifications (**99+** above 99). Click it to open the list of\nyour latest 20:\n\n- Each notification has an icon by severity: blue for **Info**, your theme colour for **Action**,\n  amber for **Warning** and red for **Error**.\n- Unread ones are tinted, with a dot beside the title. Click one to mark it read.\n- **Mark all read** clears every unread one in the list.\n- **You're all caught up.** when there are none.\n\nNew notifications arrive while you work: each pops up as a toast (red for an error) and the count\ngoes up.\n\n### Light and dark theme\n\nThe sun or moon button switches between the light and dark theme. Your choice is remembered in this\nbrowser.\n\n### The settings menu\n\nThe cog opens **Administration**: **Tenants**, **Facilities**, **Users**, **Staff**, **Spaces**,\n**RBAC** and **Plugin Management**. See [Administration](administration.md).\n\n## Your profile\n\n**Profile & Compliance** (`/profile`), from **Profile** in the user menu, shows the **Email** you\nsigned in with, your **Tenant** and your **Roles** (or **No roles assigned**). It is read-only: ask\nyour administrator to change your roles.\n\nThe notice under it is a reminder that access to health records is recorded, and to report anything\nunusual to your security officer.\n\nThe user menu's header also shows your email and your first role.\n\n## Using this Help Centre\n\nThe manual you are reading has two ways in.\n\n### The Help Centre\n\n**Help Centre** (`/help`) lists every page of the manual by group: **Getting started**, **Clinical\ncare**, **Diagnostics and pharmacy**, **Billing and revenue**, **Engagement and wellness**,\n**Insights**, **Administration** and **Reference**. Type in the search box to find the page or\nheading that mentions what you are looking for, then open it. Links inside a page jump to the\nrelated page, often straight to the right heading.\n\n### Help for the screen you are on\n\nPress the `?` key on any screen (not while typing in a box), or press **Help** at the bottom of the\nsidebar. A drawer opens beside the screen with the manual's page for that screen, scrolled to the\nsection that explains it. Where another page also covers the screen, it is offered as related\nreading. Close the drawer to carry on where you were; nothing on the screen is lost.\n\nIf a screen has no page of its own, the drawer offers the Help Centre instead. The\n[Glossary](glossary.md) explains the terms used across the app.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Multi-factor codes.** When the server asks for a verification code, the sign-in page says so,\n  but the **MFA Code** box does not appear, so a code cannot be entered yet. Ask your administrator\n  if your account needs one.\n- **The ⌘K shortcut** shown in the patient search box does nothing yet.\n- **Returning to where you were.** After signing in you always land on the dashboard, not the screen\n  you were sent from.\n- **Debug Session Info**, the link at the foot of the sign-in page, is a technical page for support\n  staff showing the contents of your session. You do not need it for day-to-day work.\n",
  "glossary.md": "---\ntitle: 'Glossary'\ngroup: 'Reference'\norder: 900\nroutes: []\ndescription: 'The clinical, billing and setup terms you meet across the app, with the statuses and values each screen shows.'\n---\n\n**ABDM** — Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission, India's national digital health network. Switched on per tenant under **ABDM Settings** (`/configurations/abdm`), which needs the **Activation** switch and working **Gateway credentials**. See [Administration](administration.md).\n\n**ABHA** — a patient's ABDM health account (a 14-digit number). The **ABHA** button on **Identity Documents** verifies an existing ABHA by OTP or creates a new one from Aadhaar. See [Patient access](patient-access.md).\n\n**ABHA address** — the readable handle claimed for an ABHA (for example name@sbx). A newly created ABHA needs one before it can be used.\n\n**Accessioning** — the lab stage after **Receiving**, where a received specimen is registered into the lab workflow. Next comes **Processing**, then **Result Entry**. See [Laboratory](lab.md).\n\n**Adjustment** — a manual entry on the **Patient Ledger**: **Credit (reduce balance)** or **Debit (increase balance)**.\n\n**Admission** — an inpatient stay. Its type is Elective, Emergency, Maternity, Day case or Transfer. See [Inpatient care](inpatient-care.md).\n\n**Appointment** — a booked slot with a clinician. Statuses: **Scheduled**, **Confirmed**, **Checked In**, **In Progress**, **Completed**, **Cancelled**, **No Show**.\n\n**AUC** — area under the curve, the target used to dose carboplatin. The app works out the dose with the Calvert formula, AUC × (CrCl + 25), capping CrCl at 125 mL/min. It cannot calculate an AUC dose without a CrCl.\n\n**Batch** — a group of claims sent to a payer together. Types: professional, institutional, dental or pharmacy. Statuses: **Open**, **Closed**, **Submitting**, **Submitted**, **Acknowledged**, **Rejected**, **Partially processed**. See [Revenue cycle and claims](revenue-cycle.md).\n\n**Bed** — a bookable bed in a ward. In **Bed Browser** a bed shows as **Available** (green), **Occupied** (red), **Cleaning** (blue) or **Maintenance** (amber).\n\n**Billing item** — a priced service you can charge for, such as a lab test, consult, procedure, pharmacy item or package. Set up under **RCM Setup**. See [Billing setup](billing-setup.md).\n\n**Biomarker** — a measured health marker in Wellness, with reference ranges and optional optimal ranges. Categories: metabolic, hormonal, inflammatory, cardiovascular, genetic, cellular, nutrient. See [Wellness](wellness.md).\n\n**BSA** — body surface area in m². The app calculates it from height and weight with the Mosteller formula and uses it for mg/m² chemo doses.\n\n**Care Context** — a workspace that brings together one patient's encounters, timeline and external records. It opens from the round **Open Care Context** button, which only appears once the patient has enough encounters (5 unless your organisation has changed it).\n\n**Care plan** — an oncology plan of planned treatment for a cancer diagnosis. Statuses: draft, active, on hold, revised, completed, cancelled. See [Oncology](oncology.md).\n\n**Catalog** — a master list under **Clinical Catalogs** (`/catalogs`): Value Sets, Packages, Administrative Services, Vital Signs Templates, Medications, Diagnoses, Lab Tests, Observation Codes, Imaging Studies, Procedures, Note Templates, Checklist Templates and Form Master.\n\n**Catalog mapping** — the link between a clinical catalog entry and the billing item that charges for it.\n\n**Charge posting rule** — a rule that creates a charge automatically when something happens, such as a lab test being ordered, a medication being dispensed or the daily bed charge.\n\n**Checklist template** — a reusable checklist for a step of care: Admission, Discharge, Transfer, Pre-operative, Post-operative, Surgery, Procedure, Outpatient visit or Emergency.\n\n**Chemo order** — a single cycle of a chemo protocol ordered for a patient, with the BSA it was dosed on. Statuses: **Pending**, **Approved**, **Verified**, **In Progress**, **Completed**, **Held**, **Cancelled**.\n\n**Chemo protocol** — a reusable chemotherapy template, made up of phases of identical cycles, each with its drug lines, doses, days and dose basis. See [Oncology](oncology.md).\n\n**Claim** — a request for payment sent to a payer. Statuses run from **Draft**, **Pending**, **Ready**, **Scrubbing**, **Validated** or **Failed validation**, through **Submitted**, **Acknowledged**, **Pending adjudication** and **Adjudicated**, to **Paid**, **Partially paid**, **Rejected**, **Denied**, **Appealed** or **Cancelled**.\n\n**Coding session** — a piece of medical coding work on an encounter. Statuses: New, Pending, In Review, Returned, Completed, Submitted. See [Billing](billing.md).\n\n**Configuration level** — the layer a setting comes from in **System Configurations**: **Instance Level**, then tenant, then facility. The **Effective** view shows the values actually in use.\n\n**Consent** — a patient's permission to pull their health records from an outside exchange. **External records** shows it as **Consent granted**, **Consent expiring**, **Consent expired**, **Consent revoked** or **No consent**.\n\n**CPT** — the procedure coding system used by default for procedure codes in medical coding, and in the imaging and procedure catalogs.\n\n**CrCl** — creatinine clearance in mL/min. You enter it when you create a treatment schedule. It is optional unless the protocol has AUC-dosed drugs.\n\n**Credit note** — a document that reduces what a patient owes on a bill. Statuses: **Draft**, **Posted**, **Voided**.\n\n**Critical value** — a lab result far outside its reference range: at or below half the low limit, or at or above one and a half times the high limit. Result entry flags it **critical_low** or **critical_high** in red. Results that are only out of range show **low** or **high** in amber.\n\n**Cycle** — one repeat of a chemo protocol phase, lasting a set number of days. Doses are planned by cycle and day, for example C1 D1.\n\n**Day-care Worklist** — the list of every scheduled treatment day on a given date, for the day-care unit and pharmacy.\n\n**Debit note** — a document that adds to what a patient owes. Statuses: **Draft**, **Posted**, **Voided**.\n\n**Discharge** — the process of ending an admission. Statuses: **Planning**, **Ready**, **Approved**, **Discharged**, **Cancelled**. **Start Planning** begins it.\n\n**Discharge summary** — the clinical document written at discharge, listed under **Discharge Summaries**.\n\n**Dispensing** — issuing medicines against a prescription or over the counter. Statuses: queued, verified, dispensed, partially dispensed, cancelled, returned. See [Pharmacy](pharmacy.md).\n\n**Dose level** — a preset dose reduction on a protocol drug line, such as DL−1 or DL−2, used when a patient cannot take the full dose.\n\n**Eligibility** — a check with a payer on whether a patient's cover is active and what it pays for. Results: **Accepted**, **Rejected**, **Error**.\n\n**Encounter** — one episode of care for a patient: a clinic visit, emergency attendance, admission or virtual consult. See [Patient access](patient-access.md).\n\n**Encounter class** — the care setting of an encounter. When you create one you choose **Ambulatory**, **Emergency**, **Virtual**, **Inpatient** or **Home Health**.\n\n**Encounter priority** — **Routine**, **Urgent**, **ASAP**, **STAT** or **Elective**.\n\n**Encounter status** — where an encounter has got to: **Planned**, **Arrived**, **Triaged**, **In Progress**, **Finished** or **Cancelled**.\n\n**Facility** — one hospital or clinic site within a tenant. Its structure runs facility, department, ward or clinic, then space. The facility you are working in shows at the foot of the sidebar.\n\n**Fee schedule** — a price list. **Authority** schedules come from a regulator (DHA, DOH, MOHAP, HAAD), **Tenant** schedules are your own prices, and **Contract** schedules belong to a payer contract. Statuses: active, inactive, draft, expired.\n\n**Form Master** — OpenMedForm form definitions that you upload so clinicians can fill them in against a patient's encounter.\n\n**Fraction** — one radiation treatment session. Statuses: scheduled, delivered, missed, cancelled, rescheduled.\n\n**HIP** — Health Information Provider, the ABDM role a facility plays when it holds records. Each facility is usually its own HIP, so ABDM credentials are normally stored per facility.\n\n**ICD-10** — the diagnosis coding system used by default for diagnosis codes in medical coding. The **Diagnoses** catalog holds the ICD releases.\n\n**Invoice** — a patient bill. Statuses: **Unpaid** (orange), **Partial** (amber), **Paid** (green), **Cancelled** (rose). See [Billing](billing.md).\n\n**Lab report status** — **Draft**, **Preliminary**, **Final**, **Amended**, **Corrected** or **Cancelled**.\n\n**LOINC** — the coding system used for lab tests in the **Lab Tests** catalog.\n\n**Membership plan** — a paid plan in a tier (basic, standard, premium, platinum, VIP) with benefits and a billing cycle: monthly, quarterly, semi-annual or annual. See [Membership](membership.md).\n\n**MRN** — medical record number, the patient's identifier within your organisation. It appears in patient headers, and you can search by it on most patient lists.\n\n**OT Board** — the live view of the operating theatres, showing what is happening in each room on the day.\n\n**OT report** — the operation note for a case. Statuses: Draft, Signed, Amended, Cancelled.\n\n**OT request** — a request to book surgery. Priority: Elective, Urgent or Emergency. Statuses: **Draft**, **Requested**, **Under Review**, **Approved**, **Rejected**, **Scheduled**, **Cancelled**, **Completed**. See [Operating theatre](operating-theatre.md).\n\n**OT room** — a space set up for use as an operating theatre, with an optional specialty.\n\n**OT schedule** — the booked slot for an approved request. Day-of-surgery statuses: **Planned**, **Confirmed**, **Patient Ready**, **Patient In Ot**, **Anaesthesia Started**, **Surgery Started**, **Surgery Completed**, **Patient Shifted To Recovery**, plus **Cancelled** and **Postponed**.\n\n**Package** — a bundle of services sold as one item: health check, surgical bundle, maternity bundle, pre-employment, annual checkup or custom.\n\n**Patient Ledger** — a patient's running account of invoices, receipts, credit notes, debit notes, refunds, adjustments and opening balance. Entries can be reversed.\n\n**Payer** — whoever pays the bill: insurance, TPA, government, corporate, or self/cash. See [Billing setup](billing-setup.md).\n\n**Payer contract** — your agreement with a payer: fee-for-service, capitation, bundled or value-based. Statuses: active, draft, suspended, expired.\n\n**Permission** — a single allowed action in **RBAC**. Permissions are grouped into roles.\n\n**Phase** — a block of identical cycles within a chemo protocol, for example AC then Paclitaxel. Each phase has its own colour on the schedule.\n\n**Plugin** — an optional module that is switched on in **Plugin Management**. Oncology is a plugin.\n\n**PreAuth** — prior approval from a payer before treatment. Urgency: routine, urgent or emergency. Statuses: **Draft**, **Pending**, **Submitted**, **Approved**, **Partially approved**, **Denied**, **Cancelled**, **Expired**.\n\n**PRM event** — something that happened to a patient (for example an appointment confirmed, a lab result ready or a payment overdue) that engagement rules can react to. See [Patient engagement](patient-engagement.md).\n\n**PRM rule** — an engagement rule. When a matching event arrives it either sends a message or creates a task, straight away or after a delay.\n\n**PRM task** — a follow-up job created by a rule. Statuses: pending, in progress, completed, cancelled, skipped.\n\n**PRM template** — message wording for a channel (SMS, WhatsApp, email, in-app or push). Statuses: draft, pending, approved, rejected.\n\n**Receipt** — a record of payment allocated against one or more invoices. Methods: **Cash**, **Card**, **UPI**, **Bank transfer**, **Wallet**.\n\n**Refund** — money returned to a patient. Statuses: **Pending**, **Approved**, **Rejected**, **Processed**, **Voided**. Methods: Cash, Card reversal, Bank transfer, Cheque, Wallet, Other.\n\n**Regimen** — the drugs, doses, routes and days in a chemo protocol, laid out phase by phase.\n\n**Remittance** — a payer's payment advice explaining what was paid on which claims. Statuses: **Received**, **Processing**, **Processed**, **Reconciled**, **Error**.\n\n**Role** — a named set of permissions given to users, managed in **RBAC**. See [Administration](administration.md).\n\n**Space** — a physical room or area in a facility, such as a bed, consulting room or theatre, with a type, floor and capacity.\n\n**Specimen** — the sample taken for a lab test. It moves through **Lab Collection**, then **Receiving** (where it can be rejected with a reason), **Accessioning**, **Processing** and **Result Entry**.\n\n**Staging system** — the scheme used to stage a cancer: AJCC TNM, UICC TNM, FIGO, Ann Arbor, Rai, Binet, Dukes, Gleason or Other.\n\n**Subscription** — a patient's membership of a plan. Statuses: pending, active, paused, past due, cancelled, expired.\n\n**Tenant** — your organisation's separate space in the app. Its data, settings, roles and catalogs are kept apart from every other tenant's.\n\n**TNM** — tumour, node and metastasis staging. You record it as **Clinical**, **Pathological**, **Restaging** or **Recurrence**. A staging record's status is **Active**, **Superseded** or **Entered in Error**.\n\n**Treatment day** — one day of a treatment schedule. Statuses: planned, ordered, given, held, skipped, cancelled.\n\n**Treatment intent** — why a cancer is being treated: curative, adjuvant, neoadjuvant, palliative, surveillance or supportive.\n\n**Treatment modality** — the kind of cancer treatment: surgery, chemotherapy, radiation, immunotherapy, hormonal, targeted, palliative or surveillance.\n\n**Treatment schedule** — a patient's dated chemo course, built from a protocol, a C1 D1 date, height, weight and (if needed) CrCl. Statuses: active, on hold, completed, cancelled.\n\n**Triage level** — urgency on a five-level scale: **Level 1 · Critical**, **Level 2 · Emergency**, **Level 3 · Urgent**, **Level 4 · Semi-urgent**, **Level 5 · Non-urgent**. New triage forms start at Level 5. See [Charting and triage](clinical-charting.md).\n\n**Tumor board** — a multidisciplinary meeting that reviews a cancer case and agrees a recommendation. Statuses: scheduled, in review, completed, deferred.\n\n**Value set** — a controlled list of coded options (concepts) behind dropdowns across the app. It can be translated and overridden per tenant.\n\n**Ward** — an inpatient unit made up of beds. **Ward Board** shows each patient with flags such as **NPO**, **Telemetry**, **Isolation**, **Allergies** and fall risk.\n",
  "inpatient-care.md": "---\ntitle: 'Inpatient care'\ngroup: 'Clinical care'\norder: 40\nroutes: ['/inpatient']\ndescription: 'Admitting a patient to a bed, the Ward Board and inpatient channel, transfers, bed status on the Bed Browser, discharges and discharge summaries.'\n---\n\nShown when your organisation has Inpatient Care switched on.\n\nEverything here sits under **Inpatient Care** in the sidebar:\n\n- **Admission** (`/inpatient/admissions`): find admissions and create new ones.\n- **Discharges** (`/inpatient/discharges`): start, track and execute discharges.\n- **Discharge Summaries** (`/inpatient/discharge-summaries`): write the summary document.\n- **Bed Browser** (`/inpatient/bed-browser`): every bed's status, and cleaning or maintenance.\n- **Ward Board** (`/inpatient/wards`): the live board of beds and patients, with meds, orders and transfers.\n\nA typical stay runs: admit to a bed, care for the patient from the Ward Board and the inpatient channel,\ntransfer if needed, start the discharge, write the summary, execute the discharge, then send the bed for\ncleaning on the Bed Browser.\n\n## Admissions\n\n**Inpatient Admissions** (`/inpatient/admissions`) lists admissions, newest first, 20 to a page.\n\nFilter with:\n\n- **Search**: patient name or MRN.\n- **Ward**: **All wards** or one ward.\n- **Admission Status**: **Admitted**, **Transferred**, **Discharged**, **Deceased**, **Absconded**.\n- **Discharge Status**: **Not Started**, **Planning Initiated**, **Ready for Discharge**, **Discharged**.\n- **Date**: **Today** (the default), **Yesterday** or **Date Range**, by admission date.\n\nThe list opens on today's admissions only. To find a patient admitted last week, switch **Date** to\n**Date Range** and pick the dates.\n\nColumns are **Admission #**, **Patient** (name, MRN, gender and age), **Bed** (ward and bed), **Status**\nand **Discharge Status**. Click a row to open the admission.\n\n| Discharge Status badge | Look | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Ready** | Green, tick | Discharge marked ready |\n| **Planning** | Grey, clock | Discharge planning started |\n| **Discharged** | Outlined | Discharge confirmed |\n| — | Dash | Not started |\n\n**Discharge Status** filters only the page of results already loaded, not the whole list. If a patient you\nexpect is missing, clear that filter and use the search instead.\n\n### Admit a patient\n\n**Go to:** **New Admission** on the list, **+ Admit Patient** on the Bed Browser, or **Admit Patient To This\nBed** on a free bed in the Bed Browser. The form is **Create Admission** (`/inpatient/admissions/new`).\n\n1. **Search Patient** and pick the patient. Picking a different patient clears the bed and encounter you had\n   chosen.\n2. **Attending Physician**: type a name and pick from the active physicians listed. **Change** lets you pick\n   again.\n3. **Encounter Option**:\n   - **Create New Encounter** (the default) starts a new inpatient encounter.\n   - **Link to Existing Encounter** links an active ER or OPD encounter. Press **Select Encounter** and pick\n     one from **Select Active Encounter**. It says \"No active encounters found.\" if there are none.\n4. **Admission Date** defaults to now. Set **Admission Type** (Elective, Emergency, Maternity, Day case,\n   Transfer) and **Admission Source** (Emergency room, Outpatient dept, Transfer in, Direct, Physician\n   referral).\n5. Find a bed. Optionally narrow by **Bed Type** (General, ICU, Isolation, Pediatric, Maternity) and\n   **Gender Restriction** (Male only, Female only, Mixed), then press **Search Beds**. The patient's gender\n   is shown under the filters to help you choose. Click a bed to select it; **Change bed** clears it.\n   If you came from the Bed Browser the bed is already selected.\n6. Optional: **Isolation Type**, **Vitals Frequency** (Q1H to DAILY), **Fall Risk Score** (1 to 5),\n   **Insurance Auth Number**, **Estimated Cost**, and **Clinical Alerts** (separate several with commas).\n7. Press **Create Admission**.\n\n**Check:** \"Admission created\" appears and the admission opens.\n\n**If instead:** the form stays put and says why:\n\n- \"Patient ID must be a UUID\": no patient picked.\n- \"Attending physician ID must be a UUID\": no physician picked.\n- \"Select an available bed.\": no bed picked.\n- \"Select an active encounter.\": you chose **Link to Existing Encounter** but did not pick one.\n- \"Admission date is required\".\n- \"No available beds match the filters.\": loosen the filters and search again.\n- \"Facility context missing. Re-login to load facility-specific beds.\": sign out and back in.\n- \"Admission failed\" with the server's reason.\n\nA **Fall Risk Score** outside 1 to 5 blocks the save without showing a message by the field. If **Create\nAdmission** seems to do nothing, check that score.\n\n### Admission details\n\n**Admission Details** (`/inpatient/admissions/{id}`) shows the **Admission Number**, **Admission Status**,\n**Discharge Status**, **Patient** (name, MRN, age, gender), **Attending Physician** and **Current Bed**.\n\nUnder **Update Admission** you can change **Isolation Type**, **Vitals Frequency**, **Fall Risk Score**,\n**Expected Discharge Date**, **Discharge Notes**, **Insurance Auth Number**, **Estimated Cost** and\n**Clinical Alerts**, then press **Update Admission**. The attending physician is shown but cannot be changed\nhere. Success says \"Admission updated\"; a refusal says \"Update failed\" with the reason.\n\nTo move or discharge the patient, use the [Ward Board](#ward-board) and [Discharges](#discharges).\n\n## Ward Board\n\n**Ward Board** (`/inpatient/wards`) shows every bed in your facility, grouped by ward, with the patient in\neach.\n\n- **Grid** and **List** switch the layout.\n- **Ward Filter**: **All Wards** shows the whole facility. Click ward chips to show only those wards;\n  **Select All** picks every chip. The heading says \"All wards\" or how many are selected.\n- Status chips filter the beds, each with its count: **All Beds**, **Occupied**, **Available**,\n  **Cleaning**, **Maintenance**.\n\nEach ward heading counts its occupied, available and cleaning beds.\n\n| Bed badge | Edge | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Occupied** | Green | A patient is in the bed |\n| **Available** | Grey, dashed card | \"Bed Available\", ready for admission |\n| **Cleaning** | Amber | \"Bed in Cleaning\", awaiting environmental services |\n| **Maintenance** | Amber | Out of use for maintenance |\n\nAn occupied bed shows the patient's initials, name, age, gender and MRN, and up to three flags (a **+n**\nchip counts the rest):\n\n| Flag | Colour |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Low / Moderate / High Fall Risk** | Amber |\n| **NPO** | Rose |\n| **Telemetry** | Sky |\n| **Isolation** | Theme colour |\n| **Allergies** | Orange |\n\nOccupied beds carry four buttons: **Meds**, **Orders**, **Transfer** and **Channel**.\n\n### Prescribe from the board\n\n1. Press **Meds** on the bed. **Medications** opens for that patient.\n2. Type in **Search medications to prescribe...** and click a medicine. Each shows its code, dose, route and\n   frequency. One already prescribed is greyed and says **Already prescribed**.\n3. \"Prescription added\" confirms it. It joins the list of active prescriptions below, with its status.\n   The bin icon removes one (\"Prescription removed\").\n\n**If instead:** \"Cannot open Meds\" with \"No encounter or patient is linked to this admission.\" or\n\"Prescriber identity is missing or invalid.\". The prescriber is the attending physician, or you if none is\nset. \"Prescription already exists\" means that drug code is already on the encounter.\n\n### Order from the board\n\n1. Press **Orders**. **Orders** opens for that patient.\n2. Choose the catalogue: **Lab Tests**, **Imaging Studies** or **Procedures**, then search and pick an item.\n   It is added as a routine order (\"Order added\").\n3. Each order shows its type, name, code and **Priority**. The pencil opens **Edit clinical order**: add\n   **Clinical indications** or **Special instructions** and press **Save changes**. Saving with both empty\n   says \"No updates provided\". The bin removes the order.\n\n**If instead:** \"Cannot open Orders\" with the same reasons as Meds, or \"Order already added\" if that code\nis already on the encounter.\n\n### Transfer a patient\n\n1. Press **Transfer** on the bed. **Transfer Patient** shows **Transfer From** (the current bed).\n2. Under **Transfer To**, **Select available bed**. Only free beds in your facility are listed, as\n   \"Ward · Bed\".\n3. Pick a **Transfer Type**: **Clinical need**, **Bed availability**, **Patient request** or **Infection\n   control**.\n4. Enter a **Transfer Reason** (required) and any **Notes**.\n5. Press **Transfer Patient**. It stays greyed until a bed and a reason are given.\n\n**Check:** \"Transfer completed\" and the patient appears in the new bed.\n\n**If instead:** \"Missing transfer details\", \"Invalid target bed\", \"No available beds found for this\nfacility.\" or \"Transfer failed\" with the server's reason.\n\n**View History** in the dialog lists earlier transfers for this admission: type, when, ward and bed, who\nassigned it, when the bed was released, and the reason and notes. It says \"No transfers recorded for this\nadmission.\" if there are none.\n\n## Inpatient channel\n\nPress **Channel** on an occupied bed to open **Inpatient Channel** (`/inpatient/ward-board/{admissionId}`),\nthe patient's page for the stay.\n\nThe header shows the admission status, **Acuity**, and the discharge badge (**Ready for Discharge**,\n**Planning Initiated** or **Discharged**) once discharge has started. Below it are the patient (age, gender,\nMRN), **Location**, **Provider** (attending physician), **Admission Date** and **Discharge Status**.\n\n### Care Channel\n\nThe **Care Channel** is the care team's message thread for this admission, with its status badge.\n\n- Messages are edged by kind: blue for team messages, amber for clinical events, grey for system events\n  (such as \"Patient transferred from ... to ...\"). **High** messages are marked amber, **Urgent** rose.\n- **Send message**: type in **Type an update or note for the care team...**, choose **Normal**, **High** or\n  **Urgent**, and press **Send**. Sending needs the channel to be **ACTIVE** and the admission to have an\n  attending physician.\n- **Meds**, **Orders** and **Transfer** here open the same dialogs as on the Ward Board. A prescription,\n  order or transfer made from here also posts a line into the channel, such as \"Transfer completed: ...\".\n- **Team** opens **Care Team Members**. Under **Add Team Member** pick a staff member and a role (Attending\n  Physician, Resident Physician, Consulting Physician, Primary Nurse, Charge Nurse, Staff Nurse, Pharmacist,\n  Case Manager, Respiratory Therapist, Physical Therapist, Dietitian, Other) and press **Add**. Removed\n  members show when they were removed.\n- **Sync** refreshes the member list from the admission.\n\nIf there is no channel the card says \"No care channel found for this admission.\".\n\n### Active Medications and Checklists\n\n**Active Medications** lists the encounter's prescriptions with dose, route, frequency and status, or says\n\"No clinical encounter linked\" or \"No active prescriptions\".\n\n**Checklists** lists the admission's checklists with category, percent complete, due time and status.\n**Open** takes you to the checklist.\n\n### Fill in a checklist\n\n**Checklist** (`/inpatient/checklists/{instanceId}`) shows the template's items by section. Required items\ncarry a red asterisk. Some items only appear once an earlier answer makes them relevant.\n\n1. Answer the items: tick boxes, text, numbers, dates, choices or a staff member.\n2. **Save Draft** saves your answers (\"Checklist saved\"). With nothing filled in it says \"Nothing to save\".\n3. **Mark Complete** when done, then a second person can **Verify**. **Cancel** cancels the checklist.\n\n**Checklist Actions** shows the **Status**, **Completion** percentage and **Due** time. Once a checklist is\ncompleted, verified or cancelled it becomes read-only. File-upload items say \"File upload not yet\nsupported.\"\n\n## Bed Browser\n\n**Central Bed Management** (`/inpatient/bed-browser`) is every bed in the facility at a glance.\n\nTiles count **Total Beds**, **Available**, **Occupied**, **Cleaning** and **Maintenance**. Filter by ward\nand by status. Each bed is a small card with its number, a coloured dot and badge, and the occupant's name:\n\n| Status | Colour |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Available** | Green |\n| **Occupied** | Rose |\n| **Cleaning** | Blue |\n| **Maintenance** | Amber |\n\nClick a bed to open its details: ward, status, and for an occupied bed the patient, age and gender, MRN,\nadmission number, admission status and admission date.\n\n### Change a bed's status\n\nFrom the bed's details:\n\n- **Mark Cleaning Required** sends it to **Cleaning**; on a bed in cleaning, **Mark Cleaning Complete**\n  frees it.\n- **Mark Maintenance** takes it out of use; **Clear Maintenance** brings it back.\n- On an available bed, **Admit Patient To This Bed** opens [Create Admission](#admit-a-patient) with this bed\n  already chosen.\n\nThe dialog closes and the board refreshes when the change succeeds. If it fails, the dialog stays open with\nno message; check the bed's status before trying again.\n\n## Discharges\n\n**Discharges** (`/inpatient/discharges`) tracks each discharge from planning to execution.\n\nSearch by patient name or MRN, filter by status, and pick dates with **Today**, **Yesterday** or **Range**.\nThe dates filter on the actual discharge date and default to **Today**, so discharges still in planning may\nnot show until you widen the range.\n\nColumns are **Admission #**, **Patient**, **Admission Date**, **Bed**, **Discharge Status** and **Actions**\n(**View**).\n\n| Status | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Planning** | Amber | Discharge started, being prepared |\n| **Ready** | Green | Marked ready |\n| **Approved** | Sky | Approved (only when approval is required) |\n| **Discharged** | Dark slate | Executed; the patient has left |\n| **Cancelled** | Rose | Discharge called off |\n\n### Start a discharge\n\n**Go to:** **New Discharge** on Discharges or Discharge Summaries. This opens **Create Discharge**\n(`/inpatient/discharges/new`).\n\n1. Search by patient name or MRN. Only patients currently admitted are listed.\n2. Press **Select** on the admission (it changes to **Selected**).\n3. Press **Start Discharge**.\n\n**Check:** \"Discharge initiated\" and the discharge opens in **Planning**.\n\n**If instead:** \"Discharge failed\" with the reason, for example when one is already under way.\n\n### Work a discharge\n\n**Discharge Overview** (`/inpatient/discharges/{admissionId}`) shows the patient, admission, **Discharge\nStatus**, **Approval Required** and **Length of Stay**. **Discharge Summary** opens the summary.\n\nA stepper runs **Initiate**, **Mark Ready**, **Approve**, **Execute**. Each step reads **Completed** (green),\n**Active** (blue), **Upcoming** or **Skipped**. **Approve** is skipped when approval is not required.\n\n1. **Initiate Discharge**: if the discharge was not started yet, set a **Target date** and **Target time**,\n   toggle **Approval required** (**Required** / **Not required**), add **Internal notes**, and press\n   **Start Planning**.\n2. **Mark Ready**: add optional remarks and press **Mark Ready** (\"Marked ready\").\n3. **Approve Discharge**: only when approval is required. Add remarks and press **Approve** (\"Discharge\n   approved\").\n4. **Execute Discharge**: choose a **Discharge type** (Routine, Against Medical Advice, Transfer Acute Care,\n   Transfer Snf, Transfer Rehabilitation, Expired, Eloped, Left Without Being Seen) and **Discharge\n   destination** (Home, Home With Home Health, Skilled Nursing Facility, Acute Care Hospital, Rehabilitation\n   Facility, Hospice Home, Hospice Facility, Psychiatric Facility, Deceased, Other). Optionally add the\n   **Discharge summary ID** and **Follow-up instructions**. Press **Execute Discharge**.\n\n**Check:** \"Discharge executed\" and the status turns **Discharged**. Afterwards, mark the bed for cleaning\non the [Bed Browser](#change-a-beds-status).\n\n**If instead:** each button stays greyed until the discharge is at its step: **Mark Ready** needs\n**Planning**; **Approve** needs **Ready** with approval required; **Execute Discharge** needs **Ready** or\n**Approved**. A refusal from the server shows as \"Update failed\", \"Approval failed\" or \"Execution failed\"\nwith the reason.\n\n**Back** on each step lets you look at the previous step.\n\n### Cancel a discharge\n\nUnder **Cancel Discharge**, type a **Cancellation reason** and press **Cancel Discharge**. The button stays\ngreyed until there is a reason, and is not offered once the patient is discharged.\n\n**Discharge Timeline** on the right records when the discharge was **Initiated**, **Ready**, **Approved**\n(or \"Skipped (approval not required)\") and **Executed / Cancelled**.\n\n## Discharge summaries\n\n**Discharge Summaries** (`/inpatient/discharge-summaries`) lists patients still admitted whose discharge has\nstarted, with **Admission #** and date, **Patient** and **Status**. Press **Open Summary**.\n\n### Write a discharge summary\n\n**Discharge Summary** (`/inpatient/discharge-summaries/{admissionId}`) is headed with the patient, MRN and\nadmission, and a status badge (**Draft** until saved otherwise).\n\n1. Under **Load Template**, pick a **Note Template**. Templates are discharge-summary note templates kept in\n   the **Note Templates catalog** (linked under the box). Loading one replaces the sections on screen with\n   empty ones, so load the template before you write.\n2. Fill in each section under **Summary Document**. Required sections carry a red asterisk.\n3. Optionally add a **Change reason**.\n4. Press **Save Discharge Summary**.\n\n**Check:** \"Summary saved\" and a new row in **Version History** (version, when, author and change reason).\nEach save is a new version; reopening the summary shows the latest version's sections.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"No content\": load a template first.\n- \"Required fields missing\" names the sections still empty.\n- \"Template not found\", \"No template version\", \"Template has no schema\" or \"Invalid template\": the template\n  is not usable; fix it in the catalogue.\n- **Save Discharge Summary** stays greyed if no summary record exists yet for the admission.\n- \"Save failed\" with the reason.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Admit Patient** on a free bed in the Ward Board does nothing yet. Use **Admit Patient To This Bed** on\n  the [Bed Browser](#bed-browser) or **New Admission** on [Admissions](#admissions).\n- Some screens exist but are not linked from anywhere and are early or technical. Reach them only by\n  typing the address:\n  - **Transfer Patient** (`/inpatient/admissions/{id}/transfer`) asks for raw ward and bed IDs. Use\n    [Transfer](#transfer-a-patient) on the Ward Board instead.\n  - **Event Timeline** (`/inpatient/admissions/{id}/events`) shows and creates admission events as raw\n    JSON.\n  - **Discharge Planning** (`/inpatient/admissions/{id}/discharge`) is an older checklist-based discharge\n    (medical clearance, medications, follow-up, education, equipment, transport, administrative, then\n    **Proceed to Discharge Patient**). Use [Discharges](#discharges) instead.\n  - **Ward Dashboard** (`/inpatient/wards/{wardId}/dashboard`) shows raw data; **Ward Patients**\n    (`/inpatient/wards/{wardId}/patients`) shows IDs, not names.\n  - A single-ward board (`/inpatient/wards/{wardId}/bed-board`) with **Critical** and **Pending\n    Discharge** filters and **Include Discharged Today**.\n",
  "lab.md": "---\ntitle: 'Laboratory'\ngroup: 'Diagnostics and pharmacy'\norder: 70\nroutes: ['/results/lab']\ndescription: 'Taking specimens on Lab Collection, moving them through receiving, accessioning, processing and result entry on Lab Operations, and writing, finalising and printing reports from Lab Results.'\n---\n\nShown when your organisation has Results & Reporting switched on.\n\nThe **Lab** section of the sidebar has three screens, used in this order as a specimen travels:\n\n1. **Lab Collection**: the ward or phlebotomy desk prepares a barcode label, takes the specimen and marks\n   it collected.\n2. **Lab Operations**: the lab receives the specimen, registers it (accessions it), starts processing, and\n   opens it for result entry.\n3. **Lab Results**: the report itself. Results are entered, submitted as preliminary, finalised and\n   printed here, and clinicians read them here.\n\nLab tests are ordered from the patient's chart; see [Charting and triage](clinical-charting.md). Imaging\nand procedure reports have their own screens; see [Imaging and procedure results](results-reporting.md).\n\nTwo kinds of lab test are reported differently, and the screens switch between them for you:\n\n- **Structured** tests (a blood count, chemistry, tumour markers) are reported as rows of analytes, each\n  with a value, unit and reference range.\n- **Narrative** tests (histopathology and similar) are reported as written sections: history, gross and\n  microscopic description, diagnosis.\n\nWhich kind a test is comes from the lab test catalogue, not from anything you choose on these screens.\n\n## Lab Collection\n\n**Lab Collection** (`/results/lab/collection`) is the collection queue for nurses and phlebotomists:\nevery lab test that still needs a specimen, before it is handed to the lab.\n\n**Go to:** **Lab** > **Lab Collection** in the sidebar.\n\n### What the queue shows\n\nTests are grouped so that one row is one specimen to take: tests for the same patient that need the same\nkind of specimen are collected together, even if they came from different orders. Each row shows:\n\n- **Patient**: name, age, gender and MRN.\n- **Specimen Group**: how many tests across how many orders the specimen covers (\"3 tests across 2\n  orders\"), with a chip for each order.\n- **Collection Profile**: the specimen type, the collection method, and a fasting badge.\n- **Status**: where the row is in collection, and the barcode once a label has been prepared.\n- **Tests**: a chip for each test the specimen is for.\n- **Ordered**: when the order was placed.\n\n| Badge | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Ready to Prepare** | Amber | No label yet. Prepare one before you collect. |\n| **Label Prepared** | Green | A barcode exists. Print it if needed, take the specimen, mark it collected. |\n| **Fasting** (with hours, such as **Fasting 12h**) | Orange | The patient must have fasted. Check before you draw. |\n| **No fasting** | Grey | No fasting requirement. |\n\nTo see one patient's specimens only, use **Patient Filter** and search by name, MRN or mobile. Clear the\nsearch to see the whole queue again.\n\nWhen nothing is waiting, the queue says \"No pending lab tests require specimen collection.\"\n\n### Prepare a label and collect\n\n1. Find the row. Its status is **Ready to Prepare**.\n2. Press **Prepare Label**. A specimen and barcode are created for the tests in that row and the label is\n   sent to print straight away.\n3. Stick the label on the container and take the specimen.\n4. Press **Mark Collected**. The row leaves the collection queue and appears on the **Receiving** tab of\n   [Lab Operations](#lab-operations).\n\nIf the label did not come out, press **Print Label** on the row (shown once the status is **Label\nPrepared**) to send it again.\n\n**Check:** a toast says **Label ready** and \"Barcode label sent to printer.\" The barcode appears under the\n**Label Prepared** badge.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- The toast says **Label ready** and \"No printer bridge detected. Downloaded the PRN file instead.\" Your\n  computer is not connected to the label printer, so the browser has downloaded the label as a `.prn`\n  file. Send that file to the label printer, or ask IT to set up the printer bridge on this machine.\n- The toast says **Label print failed**. The printer refused the label; the toast gives its reason, or\n  \"Unable to print barcode label.\" Fix the printer and press **Print Label**.\n\n## Lab Operations\n\n**Lab Operations** (`/results/lab/operations`) holds the lab's internal worklists. It has four tabs, one\nper stage, each with a count of what is waiting in it:\n\n| Tab | What it is for | What moves a row on |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Receiving** | Collected specimens that have not yet physically arrived in the lab. | **Receive** |\n| **Accessioning** | Received specimens to register into the lab workflow. | **Accession** |\n| **Processing** | Accessioned work, one row per test, to start on the analyser or by hand. | **Start Processing** |\n| **Result Entry** | Tests ready for results to be entered. | **Open Result Entry** |\n\n**Go to:** **Lab** > **Lab Operations** in the sidebar.\n\n### Receiving and Accessioning\n\nBoth tabs list specimens with the same columns:\n\n- **Specimen**: the barcode and the specimen type.\n- **Order**: the order name and code.\n- **Tests**: a chip for each test on the specimen.\n- **Collected**: when it was marked collected.\n\n1. On **Receiving**, when the specimen is in your hands, press **Receive**. It moves to **Accessioning**.\n2. On **Accessioning**, press **Accession**. The specimen is given an accession number and its tests move\n   to **Processing**.\n\nWhen a tab is empty it says \"No specimens are waiting in this stage.\"\n\n### Reject a specimen\n\nA specimen can be rejected from **Receiving** or **Accessioning** (for example, haemolysed, unlabelled or\nthe wrong container).\n\n1. Press **Reject** on its row. The **Reject Specimen** dialog opens, naming the specimen's barcode.\n2. Type the **Rejection reason**. It is required.\n3. Press **Reject**.\n\nThe specimen is removed from the worklists. A new specimen has to be collected for the tests.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"Rejection reason is required\": you pressed **Reject** with the reason empty.\n- **Failed to reject specimen**: the server refused; the toast gives the reason, or \"Try again.\"\n\n### Processing\n\nOne row per test on each accessioned specimen:\n\n- **Specimen**: barcode and type.\n- **Test**: name and code.\n- **Accession**: the accession number, or **Pending**.\n- **Status**: the test's current state, such as **accessioned** or **processing**.\n\nPress **Start Processing** when the test goes on the analyser or bench. The button then reads **Processing\nStarted** and is greyed out. It is only available while the status is **accessioned**.\n\nFrom the moment processing starts, the order also appears on [Lab Results](#lab-results).\n\nWhen the tab is empty it says \"No accessioned work is waiting for processing.\"\n\n### Result Entry\n\nLists tests ready for results with their **Order**, **Test**, **Specimen** and **Accession**. Press\n**Open Result Entry** to go to the order's report on [Lab results for an order](#lab-results-for-an-order),\nwhere you enter the results.\n\nWhen the tab is empty it says \"No processed tests are waiting for result entry.\"\n\n## Lab Results\n\n**Lab Results** (`/results/lab`) lists lab reports across all patients, newest work included as soon as\nprocessing starts.\n\n**Go to:** **Lab** > **Lab Results** in the sidebar.\n\nThe count above the table says how many results match (\"Found 42 lab results\"). Each row shows:\n\n- **Order Name** and **Patient**.\n- **Specimen**: the specimen type, with the specimen number underneath.\n- **Items**: how many analyte rows the report has, or **Narrative** for a narrative test.\n- **Abnormal**: how many results are abnormal, in amber, with the number of critical ones in red after it\n  (\"(2 critical)\"). A grey **0** means nothing is flagged.\n- **Status**: the report status (below).\n- **Reported**: when the report was issued, or **-** if it has not been.\n\nClick a row, or press **View**, to open the report.\n\n### Find a result\n\n- Type in **Search lab results...** to search the list. It updates as you type.\n- Use the status filter (**All Statuses**, **Draft**, **Preliminary**, **Final**, **Amended**,\n  **Cancelled**) to narrow it.\n- Twenty rows show at a time. Use **Previous** and **Next** under the table to page through.\n\nWhen nothing matches the list says \"No lab results found\".\n\n### Report statuses\n\n| Status | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Draft** | Grey | Results are being entered. Not yet released. |\n| **Preliminary** | Amber | Released early, still open for changes before sign-off. |\n| **Final** | Green | Signed off. Read-only. |\n| **Amended** | Blue | A final report that was later changed. Read-only. |\n| **Corrected** | Orange | A final report that was corrected. Read-only. |\n| **Cancelled** | Red | The report was cancelled. |\n\n### Start a report for an order\n\nUsually a report already exists by the time an order reaches you. If it does not, press **New Lab Report**\nat the top of **Lab Results**.\n\n1. The **Create New Lab Report** dialog lists lab orders that have no report yet. Search by order name if\n   the list is long.\n2. Press **Create** on the order.\n3. The report opens, ready for results.\n\nIf the dialog says **No reportable orders found**, every lab order already has a report, or there are no\nlab orders that match your search.\n\n## Lab results for an order\n\nThe report page (`/results/lab/{orderId}`) is where results are entered and read. It is the same page\nwhether you came from **Lab Results** or from the **Result Entry** tab of Lab Operations.\n\nIf no report exists yet, the page says \"No lab report exists for this order yet.\" Press **Create Lab\nReport** to start one.\n\n### The header\n\nThe top card shows **Lab Report**, the report status, the encounter number, and the patient's name, age,\ngender and MRN. If the patient was triaged in this encounter it also shows their **Triage Vitals** and\ntheir **Allergies** in red. The back arrow returns you to **Lab Results**.\n\n### Order & Specimen Summary\n\nThis card shows where the order is and whether it is on time:\n\n- **Order**: the order name, with badges for its priority (green for routine, red for stat, urgent or\n  critical, amber otherwise), order code and order status, and how many lab orders the encounter has.\n- **Specimen No.** and **Accession No.**, each with its current status.\n- **Turnaround Time**: the target for this test (\"4 hour target from order time\"), with **Elapsed**, **Due\n  By** and **Remaining** (or how long it is **overdue**).\n- **Workflow Timeline**: when the order was **Ordered**, **Collected**, **Received**, **Processing** and\n  **Reported**. Steps not reached yet say **Pending**. Press **Show timeline** for a larger view with\n  separate dates and times; **Hide timeline** folds it back.\n- When the order has more than one test, a table lists each **Ordered Test** with its **Code**,\n  **Specimen**, **Accession** and **Status**.\n\n| Turnaround badge | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Within TAT** | Green | Not yet reported and comfortably inside the target. |\n| **Approaching TAT** | Amber | Not yet reported and within an hour (or a fifth of the target, if shorter) of the due time. |\n| **TAT breached** | Red | Past the due time, whether or not it has since been reported. |\n| **TAT met** | Green | Reported before the due time. |\n| **TAT not configured** | Grey | The test has no turnaround target in the catalogue (\"No lab test TAT configured\"). |\n\n### Enter structured results\n\nWhile the report is **Draft** or **Preliminary**, the **Results** section is editable. A step indicator\non the right shows **Draft**, **Preliminary**, **Final** with the current step highlighted.\n\n**Result Items** lists the analytes for the test, grouped under panel headings where the test has them\n(for example, a blood count's differential). For each row you can edit:\n\n- **Test Name** and **Code**.\n- **Value** (numbers only), **Unit**, **Ref Low** and **Ref High**.\n- **Comment**.\n\n**Interpretation** fills itself in as you type a value against a low and high range:\n\n| Interpretation | Row colour | When |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **normal** | Green label | Inside the range. |\n| **low** / **high** | Amber | Below the low limit or above the high limit. |\n| **critical_low** | Red, bold | At or below half the low limit. |\n| **critical_high** | Red, bold | At or above one and a half times the high limit. |\n\nThese flags feed the **Abnormal** count on Lab Results.\n\nPress **+ Add Item** to add an analyte row that is not on the template, and **x** at the end of a row to\nremove it. Add any free-text remarks under **Comments**.\n\nWhen you are ready:\n\n1. **Save Draft** keeps your entries without changing the status.\n2. **Submit as Preliminary** saves, marks the test as resulted in the lab worklists, and moves the report\n   to **Preliminary**. It is only offered on a **Draft**.\n3. **Finalize Report** saves, marks the test as resulted, and moves the report to **Final**. The report\n   becomes read-only.\n\n**Check:** after finalising, the page shows the read-only **Lab Report** with a **Print Result** button,\nthe order drops off the **Result Entry** tab, and the **Reported** time appears on Lab Results.\n\n### Enter a narrative (pathology) report\n\nFor a narrative test the **Results** section is **Pathology Report Sections** instead:\n\n- **Specimen Type**, **Collection Date** and **Received Date**.\n- **Clinical History**, **Specimen Received**, **Gross Description**, **Microscopic Description** and\n  **Diagnosis**.\n- **Comment**, which is printed on the report.\n- **Internal Notes**, which are not intended for the printed report.\n\nThe buttons are the same as for structured results: **Save Draft**, **Submit as Preliminary** (from Draft\nonly) and **Finalize Report**.\n\n### Read and print a final report\n\nA **Final** or **Amended** report opens read-only.\n\n- A structured report shows a table of **Test Name**, **Code**, **Result**, **Unit**, **Reference\n  Range**, **Status** and **Comment**. Abnormal rows are tinted amber and critical rows red, with the\n  result in bold. Any **Comments** follow, then the **Reported** and **Verified** times.\n- A narrative report shows each filled-in section under **Pathology Report**. Empty sections are left\n  out, and **Internal Notes** are not shown.\n- A version marker such as **v2 (amended)** appears next to the status when the report has been revised.\n\nPress **Print Result** to print. The buttons and step indicator are left off the printout.\n\n## Result entry by test\n\nThe page `/results/lab/operations/result-entry/{labOrderTestId}` opens result entry for a single ordered\ntest rather than the whole order. Nothing in the app links to it any more (the **Result Entry** tab opens\nthe order's report instead), but an old bookmark still works.\n\nIt shows the patient header, an **Ordered Test Context** card (**Order**, **Test**, **Specimen**,\n**Accession**), and the same structured results form described in\n[Enter structured results](#enter-structured-results). Two extra buttons sit at the bottom:\n\n- **Back to Worklists** returns to [Lab Operations](#lab-operations).\n- **Mark Result Entered** marks the test as resulted and returns to Lab Operations.\n\nWhile it loads it says \"Preparing result entry...\"; if the test cannot be opened it says \"Unable to open\nlab result entry for this ordered test.\"\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **No amend or correct action.** Final reports can be read and printed, and **Amended** and **Corrected**\n  statuses display, but there is no button on these screens to amend a final report.\n- **No confirmation on worklist actions.** **Receive**, **Accession**, **Start Processing** and **Mark\n  Collected** show no success or error message; the row simply moves on. If a row does not move, refresh\n  the page.\n- **Structured and narrative rows look the same** on the Lab Operations worklists.\n- **No lab-wide turnaround view.** Turnaround status is shown only on each order's report page.\n- **Specimen type and dates on structured reports** are not editable on the results form; they come from\n  the collection and lab workflow.\n",
  "membership.md": "---\ntitle: 'Membership'\ngroup: 'Engagement and wellness'\norder: 140\nroutes: ['/membership']\ndescription: 'The Membership section: the Membership Dashboard, creating membership plans on Plans, and enrolling patients on Subscriptions.'\n---\n\nShown when your organisation has Membership switched on.\n\nThe **Membership** section of the sidebar holds three screens: **Membership Dashboard**, **Plans** and\n**Subscriptions**. A membership plan is a paid tier (Basic, Standard, Premium, Platinum or VIP) with a monthly\nand a yearly price and a list of benefits. A subscription enrols one patient in one plan.\n\nToday the working parts are the **New Plan** dialog, the **New Subscription** dialog and the list of\nsubscriptions. The dashboard figures and the plan catalogue are not live yet; see\n[Not live yet](#not-live-yet) before you rely on them.\n\n## Membership Dashboard\n\n**Membership Dashboard** (`/membership`) is a launch page headed \"Membership plans and subscription\nmanagement\".\n\nThree tiles sit across the top: **Active Plans**, **Active Subscriptions** and **Monthly Revenue**. They\nalways show a dash today; they do not count anything yet.\n\nUnder them are two cards. Press a card to open it:\n\n| Card | Opens | What it says |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Plans** | `/membership/plans` | Create and manage membership tiers and benefits |\n| **Subscriptions** | `/membership/subscriptions` | Manage patient subscriptions and billing |\n\n## Plans\n\n**Plans** (`/membership/plans`) is where you create membership plans. The page is headed \"Create and manage\nmembership tiers and benefits\" and has a **New Plan** button at the top right.\n\nThe **Membership Plans** panel below the button is placeholder text. Plans you create are saved, but this\npage does not list them. To check a plan was saved, open **New Subscription** on\n[Subscriptions](#subscriptions): every active plan appears in its **Membership Plan** list.\n\n### Create a plan\n\n**Go to:** **Membership** > **Plans**, then **New Plan**.\n\nThe **Create Membership Plan** dialog opens, headed \"Define a new membership tier, pricing, and benefits.\"\n\n1. Type a **Plan Name** (for example \"Platinum Health\") and a **Plan Code** (for example \"PLAT_V1\"). The code\n   must be unique in your organisation.\n2. Choose the **Tier**: **Basic**, **Standard**, **Premium**, **Platinum** or **VIP**. It starts at\n   **Standard**.\n3. Enter the **Monthly Price** and the **Yearly Price**. Both start at 0 and take decimals. Neither can be\n   negative.\n4. **Currency** shows USD and cannot be changed.\n5. Add a **Description** if you want one.\n6. Under **Benefits**, one row is already filled in: \"Basic Monitoring\". For each benefit:\n   - type the **Benefit Name**;\n   - optionally a **Value**, such as \"5 sessions\";\n   - leave **Included in plan** ticked, or untick it to list a benefit the plan does not include.\n\n   Press **Add Benefit** for another row. The bin button removes a row; it is greyed out when only one row\n   is left, because a plan needs at least one benefit.\n7. Press **Save Membership Plan**. The button spins while it saves.\n\n**Check:** the dialog closes and a green toast says \"Membership plan created successfully\". The new plan is\nactive straight away and can be picked in **New Subscription**.\n\n**If instead:** the dialog stays open and says what is missing under the field:\n\n- \"Plan name is required\"\n- \"Plan code is required\"\n- \"Benefit name is required\", under any benefit row left blank. Fill it in or remove the row.\n\nIf the server refuses the plan, a red toast says \"Failed to create membership plan\" and the dialog stays\nopen with what you typed. The most common reason is a **Plan Code** already used by another plan; the toast\ndoes not say which, so try a different code.\n\nClosing the dialog without saving keeps what you typed until you next save or reload the page.\n\n## Subscriptions\n\n**Subscriptions** (`/membership/subscriptions`) lists every patient enrolled in a membership plan, newest\nfirst. The page is headed \"Manage patient subscriptions and recurring billing\" and has a **New\nSubscription** button at the top right.\n\n### Reading the list\n\nThe **Patient Subscriptions** panel shows one row per subscription:\n\n- a round icon: a green tick for an active subscription, a grey cross for anything else;\n- the patient's name, with the plan's name in a small badge beside it;\n- **Started** and the start date, the billing cycle (**monthly billing** or **yearly billing**) and, when the\n  server has one, **Next billing** and its date;\n- on the right, the price for that cycle (the plan's monthly price for monthly billing, its yearly price\n  for yearly billing) and its currency;\n- under the price, the status in capitals.\n\n| Status | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **ACTIVE** | Green, green tick | The patient is a current member. |\n| **PENDING** | Grey, grey cross | Not yet active. |\n| **CANCELLED** | Grey, grey cross | Cancelled. |\n| **EXPIRED** | Grey, grey cross | No longer current. |\n\nThe list shows all subscriptions, not only active ones. It has no search, filter or paging, and rows do not\nopen.\n\nWhen nobody is enrolled yet the panel says \"No subscriptions found\" and \"Enroll patients into membership\nplans to track their subscriptions here.\"\n\n### Enrol a patient\n\n**Go to:** **Membership** > **Subscriptions**, then **New Subscription**.\n\nThe **New Subscription** dialog opens, headed \"Enroll a patient into a membership plan.\"\n\n1. Find the patient: type a name, MRN or mobile number into **Search by name, MRN, or mobile**. Matches appear\n   as you type, each with name, phone, sex, age and MRN; if none match it says \"No patients found.\". Pick\n   one and a card shows the patient. Clear it to pick someone else.\n2. Choose the **Membership Plan** from **Select a plan**. Only active plans are listed, each as its name with\n   the tier in capitals, for example \"Platinum Health (PLATINUM)\", cheapest first. While they load it says\n   \"Loading plans...\".\n3. Choose the **Billing Cycle**: **Monthly** (the default) or **Yearly**.\n4. Check the **Start Date**. It starts at today; press it and pick another day from the calendar. Past dates\n   are allowed.\n5. **Auto-Renew** (\"Automatically renew subscription at the end of cycle.\") is on by default. Switch it off\n   if the membership should not renew.\n6. Press **Subscribe Patient**. The button spins while it saves. **Cancel** closes the dialog without\n   saving.\n\n**Check:** the dialog closes and a green toast says \"Subscription created successfully\" with \"The patient has\nbeen enrolled in the membership plan.\". The new row appears at the top of the list as **ACTIVE**.\n\n**If instead:** the dialog stays open and says what is missing:\n\n- \"Patient is required\"\n- \"Membership plan is required\"\n- \"Start date is required\"\n\nIf the server refuses, a red toast says \"Subscription failed\" with \"An error occurred while creating the\nsubscription.\" and the dialog stays open. The server refuses when:\n\n- the patient already has an active subscription. A patient can hold only one active subscription at a\n  time;\n- the chosen plan no longer exists.\n\nThe toast does not say which of these happened. Check the list for an existing **ACTIVE** row for the\npatient first.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Membership Dashboard** (`/membership`): **Active Plans**, **Active Subscriptions** and **Monthly\n  Revenue** always show a dash. No figures are calculated.\n- **Plans** (`/membership/plans`): the **Membership Plans** panel is placeholder text (\"... will be\n  displayed here.\"). Saved plans are not listed, and there is no way here to open, edit, deactivate or\n  delete a plan.\n- **Currency** on a new plan is fixed at USD, whatever currency your organisation bills in.\n- **Subscriptions** (`/membership/subscriptions`): there is no way to cancel, change or renew a subscription\n  from this screen, and **Auto-Renew** is recorded but nothing on screen acts on it.\n- Membership is not connected to billing: subscribing a patient does not raise an invoice. Bill the\n  membership through [Billing](billing.md) if your organisation charges for it.\n- When a save fails, the red toast never gives the server's reason (such as a duplicate plan code or an\n  existing active subscription).\n\nMembership plans are separate from wellness programs; for those see [Wellness](wellness.md). For outreach to\nmembers, see [Patient engagement](patient-engagement.md). Terms are explained in the\n[Glossary](glossary.md).\n",
  "oncology.md": "---\ntitle: 'Oncology'\ngroup: 'Clinical care'\norder: 60\nroutes: ['/oncology/registry', '/oncology/staging', '/oncology/tumor-board', '/oncology/care-plans', '/oncology/protocols', '/oncology/orders', '/oncology/treatment-plans', '/oncology/day-care', '/oncology/radiation', '/oncology/catalogs', '/oncology/timeline']\ndescription: 'The cancer pathway from the Oncology Registry through staging, tumour board and care plans to chemo protocols, chemo orders, treatment schedules, the day-care worklist, radiation therapy, catalogs and the cancer timeline.'\n---\n\nShown when your organisation has Oncology switched on.\n\nThe **Oncology** section of the sidebar follows a patient's cancer care in the order it happens. Each\nstep leans on the one before it:\n\n1. **Oncology Registry**: record the cancer diagnosis. Everything else hangs off this record.\n2. **Tumor Staging**: record a TNM (or other) staging against the diagnosis.\n3. **Tumor Board**: schedule the case for the multidisciplinary team (MDT) and record its decision.\n4. **Care Plans**: write the treatment plan, one row per modality (surgery, chemotherapy, radiation…),\n   each with its own schedule and, for chemotherapy, its protocol.\n5. **Chemo Protocols**: the hospital's library of regimens the care plan picks from.\n6. **Treatment Schedules**: turn the care plan's chemotherapy into a dated, cycle-by-cycle schedule.\n7. **Chemo Orders**: one order per treatment day, taken through approval, verification and\n   administration.\n8. **Day-care Worklist**: everyone due on a date, with administration sheets and a pharmacy prep list.\n9. **Radiation Therapy**: prescriptions, simulation, planning, fractions and reviews.\n10. **Catalogs**: the reference lists (cancer types, sites, histologies) the forms pick from.\n11. **Cancer Timeline**: one patient's whole journey on a single line.\n\nA patient can have more than one diagnosis (a second primary, or a recurrence tracked separately).\nStaging, tumour board cases, care plans and orders always belong to one diagnosis.\n\n### Picking the patient and the diagnosis\n\nThe **Add** and **Create** forms on these screens start the same way:\n\n1. Under **Select Patient**, search for the patient. Once chosen, they show in a blue banner with their\n   MRN, age, gender and phone. **Change** picks someone else.\n2. If the patient has exactly one active diagnosis it is chosen for you and shows in a rose banner\n   (cancer type, site, laterality, diagnosis date, spread, status, grade, histology). If they have more\n   than one, pick it under **Select Cancer Diagnosis**; **Change** on the banner lets you pick again.\n3. The rest of the form appears only once a diagnosis is chosen.\n\n**If instead:** the card says \"No active cancer diagnoses found.\" Only diagnoses with clinical status\n**Active** are offered. Press **Add one first** to register the diagnosis, or edit the existing one back\nto Active.\n\nThe save button stays greyed out until every field marked with `*` is filled. These screens do not show a\nmessage when the server refuses a save: the form simply stays open. If that happens, check your entries\nand try again, and report it if it persists.\n\n### Status colours\n\nStatuses on the care plan, tumour board, chemo order, treatment schedule and treatment day screens share\none set of colours, shown as a badge with a dot:\n\n| Status | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **draft** | Slate | Not yet in use |\n| **planned** | Sky | A treatment day still to come |\n| **pending** | Amber | A chemo order waiting for approval |\n| **in review** | Purple | A tumour board case being discussed |\n| **revised** | Cyan | A care plan replaced by a newer version |\n| **scheduled**, **ordered** | Blue | Booked; a treatment day with an order |\n| **approved** | Indigo | Order signed off by pharmacist or physician |\n| **verified** | Teal | Order checked by the nurse at the bedside |\n| **active** | Green | In force |\n| **in progress** | Violet | Infusion under way |\n| **given**, **completed** | Emerald | Done |\n| **held**, **on hold** | Orange | Paused |\n| **deferred** | Amber | Tumour board case put off |\n| **skipped** | Slate | Deliberately left out |\n| **cancelled** | Rose | Will not happen |\n\nTreatment intents have their own colours: **curative** green, **adjuvant** blue, **neoadjuvant** indigo,\n**palliative** purple, **surveillance** amber, **supportive** teal. So do modalities on a care plan:\nsurgery rose, chemotherapy violet, radiation amber, immunotherapy teal, hormonal fuchsia, targeted indigo,\npalliative purple, surveillance sky.\n\nA chemo protocol's phases are coloured by their position, in this order: sky, violet, emerald, orange,\nfuchsia. The same colour marks the phase in the protocol builder, on the treatment schedule's cycle\ncards and on the day-care worklist.\n\n## Oncology Registry\n\n**Oncology Registry** (`/oncology/registry`) lists every registered cancer diagnosis, one row per\ndiagnosis.\n\n- **Patient**: name, MRN, gender and age.\n- **Cancer Type** and **Primary Site**.\n- **Stage**: the latest stage group, such as **Stage IIIA**, or **Not staged**.\n- **Metastatic**: **localized** green, **regional** yellow, **distant** red, **unknown** grey.\n- **Clinical Status**: active, remission, recurrence, relapsed or resolved.\n- **Diagnosis Date**.\n- **Care Plan**: the status of the diagnosis's care plan, or **None**.\n- A pencil at the end of the row opens the diagnosis for editing.\n\nSearch by patient name or MRN, and filter by clinical status. The count of patients is under the table.\n\n### Add a diagnosis\n\n**Go to:** **Add to Registry** (`/oncology/registry/new`).\n\n1. Pick the patient.\n2. Under **Cancer Identity**, choose the **Cancer Type** (type to search). The **Primary Site** list then\n   shows only the sites mapped to that cancer type, marked \"(filtered for selected cancer type)\", with the\n   usual site marked **(default)**. If the cancer type has no mapped sites, every site is offered.\n3. Choosing a site fills **Site Code (ICD-O-3)**; choosing a **Histology / Morphology** fills\n   **Morphology Code (ICD-O-3)**. You can overwrite either code.\n4. Optionally set **Laterality** and **ICD-10 Code**.\n5. Under **Clinical Assessment**, enter the **Diagnosis Date** and, if known, **Grade** (G1 to G4, GX),\n   **Metastatic Status**, **ECOG Performance Status**, **Clinical Status** (default Active),\n   **Verification Status** (default Confirmed), **This is a recurrence** and **Notes**.\n6. Press **Add to Registry**.\n\n**Check:** you are back on the registry and the patient is listed.\n\nThe cancer types, sites and histologies come from [Catalogs](#catalogs). If what you need is missing,\nask whoever maintains the catalogs to add it.\n\n### Edit a diagnosis\n\nThe pencil opens **Edit Diagnosis** (`/oncology/registry/diagnosis/{id}/edit`), headed with the patient.\nThe fields are the same, except that the patient cannot be changed and the cancer type is a plain\ndrop-down. **Cancer Type**, **Primary Site** and **Diagnosis Date** must stay filled. Set the clinical\nstatus here when a patient goes into remission or relapses.\n\n### Patient Cancer Profile\n\n**Patient Cancer Profile** (`/oncology/registry/{patientId}`) is the whole oncology history of one\npatient on one page:\n\n- **Cancer Diagnoses**: a card per diagnosis with status, site, grade, laterality, spread, a red\n  **Recurrence** tag where it applies, the diagnosis date and ECOG.\n- **Recent Oncology Labs**: test, result, reference range and a flag: **Critical** red, **Abnormal**\n  amber, **Normal** green.\n- **Surgical History (OT)**: the patient's theatre requests with priority, status, preferred date and\n  report status.\n- **Staging History**, **Tumor Board Decisions** and **Care Plans** (plan number, version, intent,\n  subspecialty and approval date).\n\n**View Timeline** opens the patient's [Cancer Timeline](#cancer-timeline). The registry rows do not link\nhere; you reach the profile from the back arrow on the cancer timeline, or from a **Diagnosis** event on\nit.\n\n## Tumor Staging\n\n**Tumor Staging** (`/oncology/staging`) lists staging assessments across all patients: **Patient**,\n**Cancer Type** (with site), **System** (with edition), **Type**, **Stage**, **TNM**, **Grade**,\n**Date** and **Status**.\n\n- **Type** is coloured: **Clinical** blue, **Pathological** purple, **Restaging** yellow, **Recurrence**\n  orange.\n- **Status** is **active** green, **superseded** grey or **entered in error** red.\n- TNM shows as `T2 N1 M0`, with `?` for a category not recorded.\n\nSearch by patient, MRN, cancer type, site, stage or system, and filter by staging type.\n\n### Add a staging\n\n**Go to:** **Add Staging** (`/oncology/staging/new`).\n\n1. Pick the patient and the diagnosis.\n2. Under **Staging**, choose the **System** (AJCC TNM, UICC TNM, FIGO, Ann Arbor, Dukes, Gleason or\n   Other), and optionally the **Edition**, **Staging Type** (default Clinical), **Stage Group** and the\n   **T Category**, **N Category** and **M Category**.\n3. Under **Date & Status**, enter the **Staging Date**. **Status** defaults to Active. Add **Notes** if\n   needed.\n4. Press **Add Staging**.\n\n**System** and **Staging Date** are required.\n\nWhen a patient is restaged, add a new record with type **Restaging** and set the old one to\n**Superseded** from its pencil (**Edit Tumor Staging**, `/oncology/staging/{id}/edit`, saved with **Save\nChanges**). Use **Entered in Error** for a record made against the wrong patient or diagnosis.\n\nThe latest staging also shows on the patient's encounter, in the **Oncology Staging** panel of the chart.\n\n## Tumor Board\n\n**Tumor Board** (`/oncology/tumor-board`) lists MDT cases: **Patient**, **Cancer Type**, **Meeting\nDate**, **Attendees** (the first two roles, then \"+n more\"), **Treatment Intent**, **MDT\nRecommendation** (**Pending** until written), **Outcome** and **Status**.\n\n**Outcome** is coloured: **Approved** green, **More Info Needed** yellow, **Second Opinion** orange,\n**Clinical Trial** blue. Filter by status (Scheduled, In Review, Completed, Deferred) and search by\npatient, cancer type, recommendation or intent.\n\n### Schedule a case\n\n**Go to:** **Schedule Case** (`/oncology/tumor-board/new`).\n\n1. Pick the patient and the diagnosis.\n2. Under **Meeting Details**, enter the **Meeting Date**. **Status** starts as Scheduled.\n3. Under **Attendees**, choose a role and specialty for each person; **Add** gives another row. Rows with\n   neither filled are dropped.\n4. Under **Clinical Information**, fill in what will be presented: **Clinical Summary**, **Imaging\n   Findings**, **Pathology Report** and **Molecular / Genomic Profile**.\n5. Leave **MDT Decision** empty for now, or fill it in if the board has already met.\n6. Press **Schedule Case**.\n\n### Record the decision\n\nAfter the meeting, open the case with its pencil (**Edit Tumor Board Case**,\n`/oncology/tumor-board/{id}/edit`):\n\n1. Set **Status** to **Completed** (or **Deferred**).\n2. Under **MDT Decision**, choose the **Treatment Intent** and **Review Outcome** (Approved, Deferred —\n   Needs more info, Second opinion required, Clinical trial referral) and write the **MDT\n   Recommendation**.\n3. Press **Save Changes**.\n\nThe decision does not create a care plan by itself. Write the care plan next.\n\n## Care Plans\n\n**Oncology Care Plans** (`/oncology/care-plans`) lists treatment plans: **Plan #** (numbered\n`OCP-YYYYMM-NNNNN`), **Cancer Type**, **Treatment Intent**, **Subspecialty**, **Modalities** (the first\nthree as coloured tags, with the chemo protocol code in brackets), **Cycles** (such as `6 × 21d`, taken\nfrom the chemotherapy modality), **Status** and **Version**.\n\nFilter by status and intent; search by plan number, cancer type, site or notes. Each row has a pencil to\nedit, and a plan with chemotherapy also has a calendar button, **Treatment schedule**, which opens the\nplan's running schedule or starts one (see [Treatment Schedules](#treatment-schedules)).\n\n### Create a care plan\n\n**Go to:** **Create Care Plan** (`/oncology/care-plans/new`).\n\n1. Pick the patient and the diagnosis.\n2. Under **Treatment Plan**, choose the **Treatment Intent** (required) and optionally the **Oncology\n   Subspecialty**.\n3. Under **Planned Modalities**, set one row per modality, in the order they will happen. **Add** gives\n   another row and the bin removes one. For each row:\n   - choose the modality and its status (**Planned**, **In Progress**, **Completed**, **Cancelled**);\n   - for **Chemotherapy**, choose the **Chemo Protocol**. Only protocols for the diagnosis's cancer type\n     are offered. Choosing one fills **Planned Cycles** and **Cycle Duration (days)** from the protocol,\n     and shows its cycles and emetogenic risk; you can still change the numbers. \"No protocols found for\n     …\" means the library has none for that cancer type yet;\n   - for **Immunotherapy**, **Hormonal Therapy** and **Targeted Therapy**, enter **Planned Cycles** and\n     **Cycle Duration (days)**;\n   - for **Radiation**, enter **Planned Fractions**;\n   - for any modality, a **Start Date**, **End Date** and **Details (optional)**.\n4. Optionally set the plan's overall **Start Date** and **End Date** under **Overall Schedule**.\n5. Under **Status & Notes**, set the **Plan Status** (default **Draft**) and any notes.\n6. Press **Create Care Plan**.\n\n**Check:** the plan is listed with its modalities and, for chemotherapy, the protocol code.\n\nSet the plan to **Active** when treatment is agreed. Only active care plans can start a treatment\nschedule, and new chemo orders link to an active plan with a chemo protocol first.\n\n### Edit a care plan\n\nThe pencil opens **Edit Care Plan** (`/oncology/care-plans/{id}/edit`), headed with the plan number and\npatient. Change the intent, subspecialty, modalities, dates, status or notes, then **Save Changes**.\nChanging the protocol here does not change a treatment schedule already started from the plan.\n\n## Chemo Protocols\n\n**Chemo Protocols** (`/oncology/protocols`) is the hospital's regimen library: **Code**, **Protocol\nName**, **Cancer Type**, **Intent**, **Cycles**, **Emetogenic Risk**, **Drugs** (how many different\ndrugs) and **Status**.\n\n- **Cycles** shows each phase for a phased protocol, such as `AC 4×14d → Paclitaxel 4×14d`, or simply\n  `6 × 21d`.\n- **Emetogenic Risk**: **minimal** green, **low** blue, **moderate** amber, **high** red.\n- **Status** is **Active** or **Inactive**.\n\nSearch by code, name or cancer type, and type a cancer type into **Filter by cancer type**. Inactive\nprotocols are hidden unless you tick **Show inactive**.\n\nThe power button on an active row deactivates the protocol at once, without asking. It is then no longer\noffered on new care plans and orders; existing orders and schedules keep it. There is no button to\nreactivate it.\n\n### Create a protocol\n\n**Go to:** **Create Protocol** (`/oncology/protocols/new`).\n\n1. Under **Protocol Identity**, enter the **Protocol Code** (unique in your hospital, turned into capitals,\n   such as `AC-T`), the **Protocol Name**, the **Cancer Type** (from the catalog), the **Treatment Intent**\n   and optionally the **Emetogenic Risk** and a **Description**.\n2. Build the regimen under **Regimen & Cycles** (below).\n3. Add **Pre-medications**, a **Hydration Protocol** (fluid, rate in mL/hr, duration in hours, and **Pre**,\n   **Concurrent** or **Post**), **Lab Prerequisites** (test, parameter, minimum value, unit, and how\n   recent: within 24h, 48h, 7 days or 14 days) and **Supportive Care**.\n4. Press **Create Protocol**.\n\n**Protocol Code**, **Protocol Name**, **Cancer Type** and **Treatment Intent** are required.\n\n### Phases\n\nA protocol is split into **phases** when the drugs change partway through, for example AC × 4 then\nPaclitaxel × 4. Each phase is a card with its own **Phase name**, **Cycles** and **Cycle length\n(days)**, and a badge showing which cycles it covers, such as **C5–C8**: cycles are numbered straight\nthrough all the phases. The heading totals the cycles and days.\n\n- **Add Phase** adds a phase after the last, with the same cycle length.\n- The arrows move a phase up or down, the copy button duplicates it (named \"(copy)\"), and the bin removes\n  it.\n\nEach phase has one line per drug: **Drug**, **Dose**, **Unit** (mg/m², mg/kg, mg, units/m², AUC),\n**Route** (IV, PO, SC, IM, IT), **Days** in the cycle (such as `1, 8`), **Formula** (**BSA**,\n**Weight**, **Fixed** or **AUC**) and **Infusion (min)**. **Add drug to …** adds a line.\n\nThe arrow at the end of a drug line opens its extras, with a count when any are set:\n\n- **Diluent** and **Max dose (mg)**, which caps the calculated dose.\n- **Dose level −1** and **Dose level −2**, the reduced doses used when a cycle is dose-reduced. The boxes\n  suggest 80% and 60% of the dose.\n- **Cycle-specific changes**: press **Add change**, then give the **Phase cycles** it applies to\n  (numbered within the phase; the matching overall cycles show underneath, such as \"= C1\") and a new\n  **Dose**, **Days** or **Infusion (min)**, or tick **Omit**. Leave a field blank to keep the phase\n  default. A note such as \"loading dose\" is shown wherever the change appears. With no changes the line\n  reads \"Same dose and days every cycle.\"\n\n### The cycle matrix\n\nUnder **Plan Overview**, the **Cycle matrix** tab is a grid of drugs against cycles, grouped under the\nphase names. Each cell shows the dose and days for that cycle; a dash means the drug is not in that\nphase, and **omit** means it is left out that cycle.\n\nClick a cell to change that one cycle: set **Dose**, **Days** or **Infusion (min)**, tick **Omit this\ncycle**, add a **Note**, and press **Apply**. A changed cell is tinted amber. **Reset** puts it back to the\nphase default. This writes the same cycle-specific change as the drug line's extras.\n\n### Day-by-day schedule\n\nThe **Day-by-day schedule** tab lists every treatment day counted from Day 1 of Cycle 1: **Day**,\n**Week**, `C2 D8` with its phase dot, the phase name and each drug's dose, route, infusion time and\ndiluent. A changed dose is in amber. The top line totals the treatment days and weeks.\n\nEnter a **Sample start date (optional)** to see calendar dates; a date falling on a weekend is in amber\nand marked \"weekend\". This is only a preview. The patient's real dates are made when a treatment schedule\nis started.\n\n**If instead:** pressing **Create Protocol** lists problems under the phases. Each must be fixed first:\n\n- \"Add at least one phase.\"\n- \"AC: cycles must be at least 1.\" or \"AC: cycle length must be at least 1 day.\"\n- \"AC: add at least one drug.\"\n- \"AC · Doxorubicin: dose must be greater than 0.\" or \"… choose at least one day.\"\n- \"AC · Doxorubicin: day 15 is beyond the 14-day cycle.\"\n- \"AC · Doxorubicin: override for cycle 5 is outside this phase (1–4).\"\n\n### Edit a protocol\n\nThe pencil opens **Edit Protocol** (`/oncology/protocols/{id}/edit`), with the same builder. The\n**Protocol Code** is read-only. Editing a protocol does not change treatment schedules already started\nfrom it, because each schedule keeps a copy of the protocol as it was.\n\n## Treatment Schedules\n\n**Treatment Schedules** (`/oncology/treatment-plans`) lists each patient's dated chemotherapy schedule:\n**Patient**, **Protocol** (code and number of cycles), **Cancer type**, **Start → end**, **Next\ntreatment**, **Progress** (a bar with days given out of all treatment days) and **Status**. It opens on\n**Active** schedules; change the status filter to see **On hold**, **Completed**, **Cancelled** or all.\nSearch by patient, MRN, protocol or cancer type. **Day-care worklist** jumps to the\n[worklist](#day-care-worklist).\n\n### Start a schedule from a care plan\n\nThe usual way in is the calendar button on a care plan. It opens the plan's schedule if one is active or\non hold, and otherwise opens the start form with the care plan filled in.\n\n**Go to:** **Start schedule** (`/oncology/treatment-plans/new`).\n\n1. Under **Patient & Protocol**, choose the **Care plan**. Only active care plans are offered. The\n   patient shows underneath.\n2. The **Protocol** is filled from the care plan's chemotherapy modality; you can choose another protocol\n   for the same cancer type. Its phases show underneath, such as `AC 4×14d → Paclitaxel 4×14d · 8 cycles`.\n3. Under **Start Date & Dosing Basis**, set the **C1 D1 date** (today, or the care plan's planned start\n   if that is later), **Height (cm)** and **Weight (kg)**. **BSA (Mosteller)** is worked out as you type.\n   **CrCl (mL/min)** is required, and marked \"Needed for AUC dosing\", when any drug is dosed by AUC\n   (carboplatin, for example); otherwise it is optional.\n4. Check the **Schedule Preview**, which now shows the patient's real dates, with weekends in amber.\n5. Press **Generate schedule**.\n\n**Check:** the schedule opens, with one card per cycle.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"This care plan already has an active schedule — open it instead of starting another.\" Follow the link\n  rather than starting a second schedule.\n- **Generate schedule** stays greyed out until a care plan, protocol, a valid date, height and weight (and\n  CrCl where needed) are filled.\n- The server's refusal shows in red above the buttons, for example \"Protocol has no regimen\".\n\n### The schedule\n\n**Treatment Schedule** (`/oncology/treatment-plans/{id}`) is headed with the patient, MRN and protocol.\nThe summary card shows:\n\n- **Status**, **Start → end** and **Next treatment** (date, cycle and day).\n- **Progress**: treatment days given out of those still counted (skipped and cancelled days are left\n  out).\n- **Dosing basis**: BSA, weight, height and CrCl.\n\nBelow it is one card per cycle, edged in its phase colour: **Cycle 3**, the phase tag, \"cycle 1 of 4 ·\nstarts 12 Nov\", and an amber \"delayed +7d\" tag with the reason if it was delayed. A cycle whose days are\nall given, skipped or cancelled is faded.\n\nEach card lists the treatment days in that cycle:\n\n- the date (amber on a weekend; the row is tinted and says \"today\" on the day itself) and `C3 D1`;\n- the day's status;\n- each chemo drug with the patient's calculated dose; a skipped or cancelled day is struck through;\n- \"needs attention\" in amber when a dose carries a warning (see the day sheet);\n- **Day sheet**, the order button and a **⋯** menu.\n\nOnly an **Active** schedule can be changed. On hold, completed and cancelled schedules are read-only.\n\n### Day sheet\n\n**Day sheet** opens a side panel for that day, headed with the date, cycle, day, phase, BSA and status,\nand a link to the chemo order if there is one. It lists, in order:\n\n- **Before treatment**: the lab checks from the protocol's lab prerequisites, on the first treatment day\n  of each cycle.\n- **Administration sequence**: pre-medications and hydration, then each chemo drug in turn, each with\n  its time. Enter the **Chair start** time and every step shows its clock time as well as its offset\n  from the start. Each drug shows the calculated dose, route and infusion time, the protocol dose it\n  came from, the dose level, the diluent to prepare it in, \"capped at max dose\" where the cap applied,\n  and any note.\n- **Supportive care / take-home**, on the first day of each cycle.\n\nA warning in amber explains a dose that needs checking:\n\n- \"BSA unavailable\", \"Weight unavailable\" or \"CrCl needed for Calvert (AUC) dosing\": the measurement the\n  formula needs is missing, so no dose could be worked out. Use **Update measurements**.\n- \"No dose level -1 defined — full dose used\": the cycle is dose-reduced but the protocol gives no\n  reduced dose for this drug. A drug with no dose levels at all (trastuzumab, for example) is never\n  reduced and shows no warning.\n\nPre-medications and supportive care are free text on the protocol, so they appear on every treatment day\n(or every first day of a cycle) rather than against particular drugs.\n\n### Delay a cycle\n\n1. On the cycle card, press **Delay**.\n2. Enter **Delay by (days)** (1 to 90; default 7) and a **Reason**, such as \"ANC 900 — count recovery\".\n3. Press **Delay cycle 3 and later**.\n\nThat cycle and every later cycle move back by that many days. The card shows the amber \"delayed\" tag.\n\n**If instead:** **Delay** is greyed out (\"Cancel this cycle's orders before delaying\") once any day in\nthe cycle is ordered or given. The server also refuses when a later day is already ordered or given:\n\"C4 D1 is already ordered; cancel its order before delaying\". Cancel that order first.\n\n### Change the dose level\n\nChoose **DL 0**, **DL −1** or **DL −2** under **Dose level** on the cycle card. With **and later\ncycles** ticked (the default), every later cycle takes the same level. Doses are recalculated from the\nprotocol's dose levels, but only for days still **planned** or **held**; ordered and given days keep the\ndose they were ordered with. The control is greyed out on a finished cycle.\n\n### Hold, skip or restore a day\n\nThe **⋯** menu on a day without an order offers **Hold this day**, **Skip (omit) this day** and\n**Restore to planned**, depending on its current status. A held day can still be ordered later; a\nskipped day drops out of the progress count.\n\n**If instead:** \"This day is ordered; change it through its chemo order\". Once an order exists the day\nfollows the order: hold or cancel the order instead.\n\n### Create the order for a day\n\nPress **Create order** on a **planned** or **held** day. The chemo order is made with the cycle, day,\ndate, BSA, weight, height, CrCl and dose level, and the day becomes **ordered**. The button then reads\n**Order · pending** (or the order's current status) and opens the order.\n\nFrom then on the day follows the order: completing the order marks the day **given**, holding it marks it\n**held**, and cancelling it frees the day back to **planned**.\n\n**If instead:** \"An order already exists for this day\", \"Cannot order a skipped day\", or \"Treatment plan\nis on_hold\". Errors on this screen show in red at the top.\n\n### Update measurements and plan status\n\nOn an active schedule the summary card has:\n\n- **Update measurements**: change **Height (cm)**, **Weight (kg)** or **CrCl (mL/min)** and press\n  **Recalculate doses**. \"Only days that are not yet ordered or given are re-dosed.\"\n- **Put on hold** and, on a held schedule, **Resume plan**.\n- **Mark completed**.\n- **Cancel plan**, after confirming \"Cancel this treatment plan?\".\n\n## Chemo Orders\n\n**Chemo Orders** (`/oncology/orders`) lists every order: **Patient**, **Protocol**, **Cancer Type**,\n**Cycle / Day** (such as `C2 D1`), **Scheduled**, **BSA** and **Status**. Click a row to open it. Filter\nby status and search by patient, MRN, protocol or cancer type.\n\nMost orders are made from a treatment schedule with **Create order**. **New Order** makes one by hand.\n\n### New order by hand\n\n**Go to:** **New Order** (`/oncology/orders/new`).\n\n1. Pick the patient and the diagnosis.\n2. **Care Plan (Optional)**: an active care plan with a chemo protocol is linked for you, and its\n   **Planned protocol** shows. Choose **— No care plan —** to leave it unlinked.\n3. **Protocol**: filled from the care plan; otherwise choose one for the diagnosis's cancer type. The\n   summary shows the cycles, drugs, emetogenic risk, which phase and drugs the entered cycle uses, the lab\n   prerequisites and pre-meds.\n4. **Patient Biometrics**: enter **Weight (kg)** and **Height (cm)**; **BSA (m²)** is worked out for you,\n   and you can overwrite it. Add **Creatinine Clearance**, **Hepatic Function** (Child-Pugh A to C) and\n   **Renal Function** if relevant.\n5. **Calculated Doses** appears once there is a BSA: each drug of that cycle with its protocol dose,\n   formula, calculated dose in mg (capped at the max dose), route, day and infusion time. \"Verify\n   calculated doses before submission.\"\n6. **Cycle Details**: **Cycle Number**, **Day Number** and **Scheduled Date** (all required).\n7. **Dose Adjustments**: **Add** a drug, the percentage of dose and the reason for any reduction.\n8. **Pre-Chemo Safety Checklist**: tick what has been done (consent, allergies, labs reviewed and in\n   range, weight/BSA verified today, doses verified, pre-meds ordered) and fill in **Consent Date**,\n   **Allergies Summary** and **Labs Date**. Add notes.\n9. Press **Submit Order**.\n\n**Check:** the order is listed as **pending**.\n\n**If instead:** \"Cycle 9 is beyond this protocol's 8 cycles.\" The cycle number is past the end of the\nprotocol, so no doses are calculated.\n\n### Work an order\n\n**Chemo Order** (`/oncology/orders/{id}`) is headed with the protocol, cycle, day, date and status, and\nshows the patient and diagnosis banners. Below the action card are the **Order Summary**, the **Protocol\nRegimen** for that cycle, the **Pre-Chemo Safety Checklist** as ticks and crosses, any **Dose\nAdjustments**, and **Workflow History** (**Ordered**, **Approved**, **Verified**, **Administered**, each\nwith its time).\n\nThe action card changes with the status:\n\n1. **Pending**: **Pharmacist / Physician Approval**. Review the order, then press **Approve Order**.\n2. **Approved**: press **Start Drug Prep & Verification**, then:\n   - **Step 1 — Pharmacist Drug Preparation**: for each drug, **Prepared by**, **Prepared at**, **Lot\n     number**, **Expiry date**, **Diluent** and **Final volume (mL)**;\n   - **Step 2 — Nurse Bedside Verification (5 Rights)**: tick all nine checks (identity, drug, dose,\n     route, time, expiry, allergy, IV line, patient educated). Until then the screen says \"All checklist\n     items must be confirmed before verifying.\";\n   - **Step 3 — Second Nurse / Independent Check (Optional)**;\n   - press **Complete Verification**. **Back** leaves without saving.\n3. **Verified**: **Ready for Administration**. Press **Start Administration**.\n4. **In progress**: **Administration In Progress**, with the start time. For each drug, **Mark Started\n   Now** and **Mark Completed Now** stamp the time (or type it), with **Rate (mL/hr)** and **IV Access\n   Site**. Each drug is tagged **Pending**, **Infusing** (blue) or **Completed** (green). Under **Adverse\n   Reactions**, **Report Reaction** adds the drug, CTCAE grade (1 Mild to 5 Fatal), type and\n   description; the worst grade shows as a tag (yellow for 1–2, orange for 3, red for 4–5). Press **Save\n   Progress** (or **Save Reactions**) as you go.\n5. When the infusion is finished, press **Complete Cycle**, check the times and reactions in **Complete\n   Cycle — Final Record**, add **Completion Notes** and press **Mark Cycle Complete**.\n\n**Check:** the card reads **Cycle 2 Completed**, with the administration time and a reactions summary.\nThe treatment day on the schedule is now **given**. The read-only **Nurse Verification Record**, **Drug\nPreparation Record**, **Administration Timeline** and **Adverse Reactions (CTCAE)** appear below.\n\n### Hold or cancel an order\n\nWhile an order is pending, approved or verified, the action card has **Hold** and **Cancel**. Each asks\nfor a reason (\"Reason for hold\", \"Reason for cancellation\"); confirm with **Confirm Hold** or **Confirm\nCancel**. The reason is added to the order's notes. Holding marks the treatment day **held**; cancelling\nfrees the day so a new order can be made. A held order shows \"Order is on hold.\" with **Cancel Order**.\n\n## Day-care Worklist\n\n**Day-care Worklist** (`/oncology/day-care`) is everything scheduled on one date, across all patients,\nfrom the treatment schedules. It opens on today. Use the arrows, the date box or **Today** to move; the\nline beside them counts patients and chemo preparations. **Print** prints the page.\n\nThe **Administration** tab has a card per patient: name (linking to their schedule), MRN, protocol,\n`C2/8 D1`, BSA, dose level if reduced, the phase tag, the day's status, and the order button (**Order ·\napproved**, for example) or \"No order yet\". Under it is the patient's [day sheet](#day-sheet), without\nthe chair start time.\n\nThe **Pharmacy prep** tab is a table of every chemo drug to prepare: **Patient**, **Protocol** (with\ncycle and day), **Drug**, **Dose** (with any warning in amber), **Basis** (protocol dose, dose level,\n\"capped\"), **Diluent**, **Route / infusion** and **Order** status. **Totals by drug** underneath adds up\nhow many of each drug and the total dose.\n\n\"No chemotherapy scheduled on this date.\" means no treatment day falls on it.\n\n## Radiation Therapy\n\n**Radiation Oncology** (`/oncology/radiation`) lists radiation prescriptions: **Patient**, **Rx #**,\n**Intent**, **Modality / Technique**, **Dose** (total, with Gy per fraction), **Fractions**, **Start\nDate** and **Status**. Status is **DRAFT** grey, **APPROVED** blue, **ACTIVE** green, **COMPLETED** in\nthe primary colour, **CANCELLED** red. Search by patient, MRN or Rx number and filter by status.\n\n### New prescription\n\n**Go to:** **New Prescription** (`/oncology/radiation/new`).\n\n1. Pick the patient (and, if offered, the diagnosis).\n2. **Treatment Intent**: **Intent** (Curative, Adjuvant, Neoadjuvant, Palliative), **Laterality** and an\n   optional **Prescription #**.\n3. **Modality & Technique**: **Modality** (PHOTON, ELECTRON, PROTON, BRACHY) and **Technique** (3DCRT,\n   IMRT, VMAT, SBRT, SRS, ELECTRON, HDR, LDR).\n4. **Dose Prescription**: **Total Dose (Gy)** and **Dose / Fraction (Gy)**. **Planned Fractions** is\n   worked out from them; you can change it. Tick **Concurrent chemotherapy** if it applies.\n5. **Schedule**: **Planned Start Date**, **Planned End Date** and **Prescription Notes**.\n6. Press **Create Prescription**.\n\nPatient, intent, modality, both doses and the start date are required.\n\n### Work a prescription\n\n**Radiation Prescription** (`/oncology/radiation/{id}`) shows the **Prescription Details** and a\ncollapsible section for each stage:\n\n1. **Approve Prescription** (on a draft), then **Activate (Start Treatment)** (on an approved one).\n2. **CT Simulation**: **Add Simulation** records the date, **Patient Position**, **Immobilization\n   Device**, **Scan Region**, **Setup Reference / Isocentre**, **Contrast used**, **Tattoo marking done**\n   and notes. Press **Save Simulation**.\n3. **Treatment Plan**: **Add Plan** records the planning system, **Treatment Machine**, the simulation it\n   uses and notes (**Save Plan**). Each plan shows its machine, contouring and physics QA, and **Approve\n   Plan** until it is approved, then a green tick.\n4. **Fractions** (once there is a plan): **Generate Fractions** with the plan, **Number of Fractions**,\n   **Dose / Fraction (Gy)**, **Start Date** and **Treatment Machine**, then **Generate**. The heading\n   counts fractions delivered. On a scheduled fraction, **Deliver** asks for the delivered dose and\n   **Miss** asks for a reason.\n5. **On-Treatment Reviews**: **Add Review** with **Review Date** (required), **Week Number**, **Toxicity\n   Grade (CTCAE)**, **Pain Score (0–10)**, **Weight (kg)**, **Treatment break required** and notes (**Save\n   Review**).\n6. **Completion Summary**: on an active prescription, **Record Completion** with the completion date,\n   delivered dose and fractions, interruptions, acute toxicity, response assessment plan and follow-up\n   plan. **Save Completion Summary** completes the prescription.\n\n## Catalogs\n\n**Oncology Catalogs** (`/oncology/catalogs`) holds the reference lists the registry and protocol forms\npick from, on four tabs. Each list can be searched, and inactive entries are hidden unless you tick\n**Show inactive**.\n\n- **Cancer Types**: **Code** (UPPER_SNAKE_CASE, unique per hospital, such as `BREAST_CA`), **Name**,\n  **Category**, **Status**. **Add Cancer Type**.\n- **Primary Sites**: **ICD-O Code** (such as `C50.9`), **Site Name**, **Body System**, **Laterality**,\n  **Mapping**, **Status**. **Add Site**.\n- **Site Mappings**: which sites belong to which cancer type, and which is the **Default**. **Add\n  Mapping** picks the cancer type and site, with **Mark as default site for this cancer type**. **Set\n  default** changes the default; the bin removes a mapping. These mappings decide which sites the\n  registry form offers.\n- **Histologies**: **Morphology Code** (ICD-O-3, such as `8500/3`), **Name**, **Behavior**, **Status**.\n  **Add Histology**; choosing a **Behavior Code** fills the **Behavior Name**.\n\nThe pencil edits an entry (its code cannot be changed once created); **Deactivate** hides it from the\nforms without deleting it, and **Activate** brings it back.\n\n## Cancer Timeline\n\n**Cancer Timeline** (`/oncology/timeline`) first asks you to \"Select a patient to view their complete\ncancer journey\": search the registered patients by name or MRN and press **View Timeline**.\n\nThe patient's timeline (`/oncology/timeline/{patientId}`) runs down the page as dated events\nalong a date rail, each coloured by category: **Diagnosis**, **Staging**, **Tumor Board**, **Care Plan**,\n**Chemotherapy**, **Radiation**, **Surgery**, **Response**, **Follow-up** and **Note**. The legend at the\ntop shows the categories present. A run of related events, such as the sessions of a chemo course, is\ngrouped into one card. Major milestones have a ring round their dot; warnings (a held chemo, for\nexample) amber; adverse events red.\n\nEvents are added automatically as records are made: diagnoses, staging, tumour board cases and\ndecisions, care plans, chemo started, completed and held, and radiation. Where an event has a source, its\ncard opens it (the staging, the order, the prescription…).\n\n- **Filter Events** by type and by **From** and **To** dates; **Clear filters** resets them.\n- **Add Event** (or **Add a manual note**) records something with no workflow of its own: a **Date**,\n  **Category** (**Note**, **Surgery**, **Response Assessment**, **Follow-up**), **Title**, **Description**,\n  an optional diagnosis, and **Mark as major milestone**. Press **Add Event**.\n- Only manually added events can be deleted, with the bin on their card.\n- **Print Summary** prints the timeline.\n\nThe back arrow goes to the [Patient Cancer Profile](#patient-cancer-profile).\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Re-activate** on a held chemo order does not release it: it records another hold, and the order stays\n  **held**. To carry on, cancel the order and create a new one (from the treatment schedule, a cancelled\n  order frees the day for **Create order**).\n- **Step 3 — Second Nurse / Independent Check** takes free text, but the server stores it as a user ID.\n  Typing a name makes **Complete Verification** fail, with no message. Leave it blank until this is\n  fixed. The dual sign-off setting it mentions is not enforced.\n- Care plans cannot be approved or revised from the screens. The **Revised** status can be filtered but\n  not set, and the profile's \"Approved\" date only appears for plans approved some other way.\n- A deactivated chemo protocol cannot be reactivated from the screens.\n- Most of these screens give no message when a save fails; only the treatment schedule screens show the\n  server's reason.\n",
  "operating-theatre.md": "---\ntitle: 'Operating theatre'\ngroup: 'Clinical care'\norder: 50\nroutes: ['/ot']\ndescription: 'Running theatres day to day: the OT Board, OT Requests, OT Schedules with the day-of-surgery steps, OT Reports, and setting up OT Rooms.'\n---\n\nThe **Operating Theatre** section of the sidebar holds five screens: **OT Board**, **OT Requests**,\n**OT Schedules**, **OT Reports** and **OT Rooms**. Opening `/ot` on its own takes you straight to the\nboard.\n\nA case moves through them in this order:\n\n1. A clinician raises an **OT request** for a patient's operation. It starts as a draft.\n2. The request is submitted, reviewed if needed, and approved.\n3. A theatre scheduler books an approved request into a room and time slot: an **OT schedule**.\n4. On the day, the team steps the schedule through the theatre stages, from **Confirm** to\n   **Shift To Recovery**. The **OT Board** shows each room's current and next case as this happens.\n5. The surgeon writes the **OT report** (the operative note) and signs it.\n\nShifting the patient to recovery completes the request automatically.\n\nWhat you can see and press depends on your role's OT permissions. If a list loads with a red message\ninstead of rows, your role may not have access to that part of the module; ask your administrator.\n\n## OT Board\n\n**OT Board** (`/ot/board`) is the live picture of every theatre for one day. It is headed **Live\ntheatre operations**.\n\n**Go to:** **Operating Theatre** > **OT Board**.\n\n### Choosing the day and refreshing\n\n- **Board date** picks the day. It opens on today.\n- **Refresh** reloads the board. It does not update by itself, so press **Refresh** to see the latest\n  state. The bar under the tiles says when the data was fetched, for example \"Last updated 2 minutes\n  ago\".\n- **Open OT Schedules** and **Open OT Rooms** jump to those screens.\n\n### The tiles\n\nSix tiles count the rooms for the chosen day: **Active rooms**, **Occupied**, **Idle**, **Blocked**,\n**Next up** and **Cases in progress**.\n\n### Room cards\n\nEach configured OT room has a card, with a coloured strip along the top and a state badge. Under the\nroom name is its space number and specialty (or its notes).\n\n| State badge | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Case in progress** | Green | A case is live in the room now: the patient is in theatre, under anaesthesia, or in surgery. |\n| **Next case pending** | Amber | Nothing live now, but another case is booked later that day. |\n| **Room blocked** | Red | The room has an approved block covering the time. The reason shows in a red box under the name. |\n| **Idle** | Grey | The room is active with nothing live and nothing more booked that day. |\n| **Inactive** | Pale grey | The room is switched off in **OT Rooms**. |\n\nFor a past or future date the board judges \"now\" as the start of that day, so cases appear as next\ncases rather than live ones.\n\nEach card shows two panels:\n\n- **Current case**: the procedure, its planned start and end times, the patient's name, MRN, gender\n  and age, and the schedule's status badge. Empty, it says \"No case in progress.\"\n- **Next case**: the same details for the next booked case. Empty, it says \"No more cases queued for\n  today.\"\n\nThe strip along the bottom of the card summarises the room's day:\n\n- **Scheduled**, **Completed** and **Cancelled** case counts.\n- **Planned** and **Actual** theatre minutes.\n- **Delay**: minutes behind the planned start, in amber, or **On time**.\n\n**If instead:** the board says **No OT rooms configured**, no rooms have been set up yet. Configure\nthem in [OT Rooms](#ot-rooms) first.\n\n## OT Requests\n\n**OT Requests** (`/ot/requests`) lists every theatre request, newest first. Each row shows the\n**Procedure** (with its code or surgery type, and any remarks), the **Patient** (name, MRN, gender and\nage), **Priority**, **Preferred Date**, **Status** and **Requested At**.\n\n- Search by patient name, MRN or phone in **Search patients by name, MRN, or phone...**.\n- Filter by status with the drop-down (**All statuses** by default).\n- Click a row, or **View**, to open the request.\n\n### Priority and status badges\n\n| Priority | Colour |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Elective** | Teal |\n| **Urgent** | Amber |\n| **Emergency** | Solid red, so it stands out on the list |\n\n| Status | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Draft** | Slate | Saved but not yet sent. |\n| **Requested** | Blue | Submitted and waiting for review or approval. |\n| **Under Review** | Amber | Being reviewed. |\n| **Approved** | Green | Ready to be scheduled. |\n| **Rejected** | Red | Turned down. |\n| **Scheduled** | Indigo | Booked into a theatre slot. |\n| **Cancelled** | Rose | Withdrawn. |\n| **Completed** | Teal | The operation is done. |\n\n### Raising a request\n\n**Go to:** **OT Requests** > **New OT Request** (`/ot/requests/new`).\n\n1. In **Patient**, search for and pick the patient. If the patient has an oncology diagnosis, a grey\n   box shows it with the latest stage and clinical status.\n2. Pick the **Active Encounter** the operation belongs to. Only the patient's active encounters are\n   listed.\n3. In **Procedure Name**, start typing and pick from the surgical procedures in the catalogue. Picking\n   one fills **Procedure Code**, and, where the catalogue has them, **Surgery Type**, **Expected\n   Duration (minutes)** and **Anaesthesia Plan**. You can change those afterwards; the code cannot be\n   typed.\n4. Set **Priority** (**ELECTIVE** by default), and add **Diagnosis** if you wish.\n5. Optionally set **Preferred Date**, **Preferred OT Room** (active rooms only) and **Primary\n   Surgeon**.\n6. List any **Special Equipment** and **Implants Required**, separated by commas (for example \"C-arm,\n   microscope\").\n7. Tick **Anaesthetist required** or **Blood required** if they apply, and add **Remarks**.\n8. Press **Save Draft**.\n\n**Reset** clears the form.\n\n**Check:** a toast says \"OT request created: The request was saved in draft status.\" and you are back\non the list with the new row at **Draft**.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"Missing required fields: Patient, encounter, and procedure name are required.\" Pick all three.\n- \"Unable to create OT request\" with the server's reason, for example that the encounter was not found\n  for the patient.\n\n### Moving a request along\n\nThe list shows only the buttons that fit each row's status:\n\n| Status | Buttons |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Draft** | **Submit**, **Cancel** |\n| **Requested** | **Review**, **Approve**, **Cancel** |\n| **Under Review** | **Approve**, **Cancel** |\n| **Approved** | **Cancel** |\n| **Scheduled** | **Complete**, **Cancel** |\n\nEach press shows \"OT request updated\" on success, or \"Workflow action failed\" with the reason.\n\n- **Approve** works straight from **Requested**; you do not have to **Review** first.\n- **Complete** only works once the case's schedule has reached **Surgery Completed** or **Patient\n  Shifted To Recovery**. Otherwise it is refused with \"OT request can only be completed after surgery\n  is completed or shifted to recovery\". You rarely need it: shifting the patient to recovery completes\n  the request for you.\n- **Cancel** on a scheduled request is refused with \"Cancel the active OT schedule before cancelling\n  the OT request\". Cancel or postpone the schedule first.\n\n### The request page\n\nOpening a request (`/ot/requests/{id}`) shows the procedure with its status badge, the patient, and\nthe plan: **Priority**, **Surgery Type**, **Procedure Code**, **Expected Duration**, **Preferred\nDate**, **Preferred Room**, **Primary Surgeon**, **Anaesthesia**, **Blood Required**, **Requested\nAt**, and, when filled in, **Diagnosis**, **Remarks**, **Special Equipment**, **Implants** and\n**Rejection Reason**.\n\nBelow it:\n\n- **OT Schedules**: every slot this request has been booked into, with its status. Click one to open\n  it.\n- **OT Reports**: the operative reports for the case. Click one to open it.\n- **Status History**: each status change, newest first, with its time.\n\nThe request page is read-only. Use the buttons on the list to change its status.\n\n## OT Schedules\n\n**OT Schedules** (`/ot/schedules`) lists booked theatre slots in start-time order. Each row shows the\n**Start Time** (and when it ends), the **Room**, the **Patient**, the **Status**, the **Anaesthesia**\ntype, and **Current** or **Historical**. A request rebooked into a new slot keeps its old slot as\n**Historical**.\n\nSearch by patient and filter by status as on the requests list. Click a row, or **View**, to open it.\n\n### Booking a case\n\n**Go to:** **OT Schedules** > **New OT Schedule** (`/ot/schedules/new`).\n\n1. Pick the **OT Request**. Only **Approved** requests, and already **Scheduled** ones you want to\n   rebook, are listed.\n2. Pick the **OT Room**. Only active rooms are listed.\n3. Set **Scheduled Start** and **Scheduled End**.\n4. Pick the team: **Primary Surgeon**, **Anaesthetist**, **Scrub Nurse**, **Circulating Nurse** and\n   **Technician**. Each list shows all staff.\n5. Type the **Anaesthesia Type** if known.\n6. Press **Create Schedule**.\n\nAs soon as the room and both times are set, the screen checks the slot. If the room or any chosen\nteam member is already booked, unavailable, or the room is blocked, an amber box headed **Scheduling\nconflicts detected** lists each clash, and **Create Schedule** is greyed out until you change the\nroom, time or team.\n\n**Check:** a toast says \"OT schedule created: The OT request is now scheduled.\" The schedule starts at\n**Planned** and its request moves to **Scheduled**.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"Missing required fields: Request, room, start time, and end time are required.\"\n- \"Unable to create OT schedule\" with the reason, such as \"Scheduled end time must be after scheduled\n  start time\" or \"OT schedule conflicts detected\".\n\n**Rebooking:** booking a new slot for a request that is already scheduled replaces the old slot. The\nold one becomes **Postponed** and **Historical**, with the reason \"Superseded by a newer OT schedule\".\n\n### The day of surgery\n\nEach row offers the next step for its status. Press it as the case moves on:\n\n| Status | Colour | Next button |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Planned** | Sky | **Confirm** |\n| **Confirmed** | Blue | **Patient Ready** |\n| **Patient Ready** | Cyan | **Patient In OT** |\n| **Patient In OT** | Violet | **Anaesthesia Started** |\n| **Anaesthesia Started** | Amber | **Surgery Started** |\n| **Surgery Started** | Orange | **Surgery Completed** |\n| **Surgery Completed** | Green | **Shift To Recovery** |\n| **Patient Shifted To Recovery** | Teal | None: the case is finished and its request is completed. |\n| **Postponed** | Fuchsia | None |\n| **Cancelled** | Rose | None |\n\nThe colours run cool to warm to green through the day, so a list or board reads at a glance. While a\ncase is at **Patient In OT**, **Anaesthesia Started** or **Surgery Started**, its room shows as\n**Case in progress** on the board.\n\nEach step shows \"OT schedule updated\" on success, or \"Workflow action failed\" with the reason.\n\n### Postponing or cancelling a slot\n\n- **Postpone** is offered while the case is **Planned**, **Confirmed** or **Patient Ready**.\n- **Cancel** is offered at any status except **Cancelled**, **Postponed** and **Patient Shifted To\n  Recovery**.\n\nBoth act at once, with no confirmation, and send the request back to **Approved** so it can be booked\nagain.\n\n### The schedule page\n\nOpening a schedule (`/ot/schedules/{id}`) shows the room with the status badge, the patient, the\n**Scheduled Start** and **Scheduled End**, the **Actual Start** and **Actual End**, the team by name,\nthe **Anaesthesia Type**, and, when set, **Assistant Surgeons**, **Postponed Reason** and **Cancelled\nReason**.\n\nBelow it are the **Team Members** roster, the case's **OT Reports** (click to open), and the **Status\nHistory**, newest first. The page is read-only; use the buttons on the list to move the case on.\n\n## OT Reports\n\n**OT Reports** (`/ot/reports`) lists the operative reports, newest first. Each row shows the\n**Report** number with its remarks, the **Schedule** start time, the **Patient**, the **Status**,\n**Created At** (and when it was signed, or **Not signed**), and the number of **Versions**.\n\nSearch by patient and filter by status as on the other lists.\n\n| Status | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Draft** | Slate | Written but not signed. |\n| **Signed** | Green | Signed off. |\n| **Amended** | Amber | Changed after signing; needs signing again. |\n| **Cancelled** | Rose | Withdrawn. |\n\n### Writing a report\n\n**Go to:** **OT Reports** > **New OT Report** (`/ot/reports/new`).\n\n1. Pick the **OT Schedule**. Cases from **Confirmed** through **Patient Shifted To Recovery** are\n   listed, shown by patient, start time and MRN.\n2. Add **Remarks** if you wish.\n3. Fill in **Report Data (JSON)**. The box starts with a template of fields: procedure summary,\n   findings, operative steps, complications, blood loss in ml, implants used and disposition. Type\n   your text between the quotation marks, and keep the brackets and commas as they are.\n4. Press **Save Draft**.\n\n**Check:** a toast says \"OT report created: Draft operative report saved.\" and the report is listed\nat **Draft**.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"Schedule required: Select an OT schedule before creating the report.\"\n- \"Unable to save OT report\" with a message about the JSON: a quotation mark, bracket or comma is\n  missing or extra. Check the text against the template.\n- \"Unable to save OT report\" saying an active report already exists for the schedule. Each case has\n  one report; open and amend the existing one instead.\n\n### Signing\n\nPress **Sign** on a **Draft** or **Amended** report, on the list or on the report page. A toast says\n\"OT report signed: The operative report is now signed.\" If refused, it says \"Unable to sign OT\nreport\" with the reason.\n\n### Amending a signed report\n\n1. Press **Amend** on a **Signed** report. The **Amend OT Report** screen opens with the current\n   remarks and content filled in, and the schedule locked.\n2. Change what you need.\n3. Press **Save Amendment**.\n\n**Check:** \"OT report amended: A new report version has been created.\" The report moves to\n**Amended** and must be signed again. Earlier versions are kept.\n\n### Cancelling a report\n\n**Cancel** is on every report row that is not already **Cancelled**. It acts at once, with no\nconfirmation, and says \"OT report cancelled: The report was moved to cancelled status.\"\n\n### The report page\n\nOpening a report (`/ot/reports/{id}`) shows the report number with its status, the patient,\n**Created**, **Signed** and **Schedule** times, any **Remarks**, and the **Operative Report** itself,\nlaid out field by field. Lists show as bullets; empty fields say **Not documented** or **None**.\n**Sign** and **Amend** are here too.\n\nWhen a report has more than one version, **Version History** lists them, newest first, with the\ncurrent one marked **(current)**.\n\n## OT Rooms\n\n**OT Rooms** (`/ot/rooms`) lists the rooms set up for theatre scheduling, active and inactive, by\nname. Each row shows the **Room** and its space number, the **Facility** and floor, the\n**Specialty**, the **Space Type**, the **Status** (**Active** or **Inactive**) and **Notes**.\n\nRooms come from the facility's spaces, which are set up in\n[Administration](administration.md). This screen marks a space as a theatre and adds OT details.\n\n### Configuring a room\n\n**Go to:** **OT Rooms** > **Configure OT Room** (`/ot/rooms/new`).\n\n1. Pick the **Facility**. It starts on your current facility.\n2. Pick the **Space**. Only active spaces are listed.\n3. Add a **Specialty** (for example \"Cardiac, ortho, neuro\") and **Notes** (for example \"Laminar flow,\n   robotics, hybrid theatre setup\") if you wish.\n4. Leave **OT room is active and schedulable** ticked for a room that takes bookings. Untick it to\n   keep the room on file but out of scheduling.\n5. Press **Save Room Config**.\n\n**Check:** \"OT room saved: The room configuration was updated successfully.\" The room appears on the\nlist and the board.\n\n**If instead:** \"Space required: Select a Foundation space to configure it as an OT room.\", or\n\"Unable to save OT room\" with the reason.\n\nTo change a room's specialty, notes or active setting, configure the same space again: saving\nreplaces its OT details.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Reject** a request: there is no reject button, so a request can be approved or cancelled but not\n  rejected from the screens. A **Rejection Reason** only shows on requests rejected some other way.\n- Request, schedule and room pages have no edit. Details entered on a request or schedule cannot be\n  changed afterwards; rebook the case or raise a new request.\n- On **New OT Schedule**, the **Notes** box is not saved, and assistant surgeons and extra team\n  members cannot be added, so **Assistant Surgeons** and **Team Members** stay empty for schedules\n  made here.\n- **Postpone** and **Cancel** on schedules do not ask for a reason, so **Postponed Reason** and\n  **Cancelled Reason** stay blank unless the slot was superseded by a rebooking.\n- The OT report is entered as raw JSON text; there is no form for it yet.\n- The board does not refresh by itself.\n",
  "patient-access.md": "---\ntitle: 'Patient access'\ngroup: 'Clinical care'\norder: 20\nroutes: ['/patients', '/patients-ai', '/encounters', '/scheduling']\ndescription: 'Finding and registering patients, the patient record with its documents, policies, Care Context, results and Patient AI+, encounters, appointments, the Staff Calendar and Staff Scheduling.'\n---\n\nEverything here sits under **Patient Access** in the sidebar: **Patients**, **Encounters**,\n**Appointments**, **Staff Calendar** and **Staff Scheduling**. The usual day runs from left to right:\nfind or register the patient, book an appointment, turn the appointment into an encounter when the\npatient arrives, then chart it (see [Charting and triage](clinical-charting.md)).\n\nStaff must have availability set up on **Staff Scheduling** before they can be picked when booking\nor viewed on the **Staff Calendar**. If a clinician is missing from those lists, start there.\n\n## Patients\n\n**Patients** (`/patients`) is the patient list for your organisation. It opens showing the first 20\npatients and a count, \"Found _n_ patients\".\n\nThe table shows:\n\n- **MRN**, the patient's medical record number;\n- **Patient Name**;\n- **Age**, worked out from the date of birth;\n- **Gender**;\n- **Phone Number**;\n- **Status**, as a badge: **active** green, **inactive** amber, anything else (such as deceased) grey;\n- **Actions**: **View**, **AI+**, and a round grid button that opens Care Context (shown only for\n  patients with enough history; see [Care Context](#care-context)).\n\nClick anywhere on a row, or press Enter on it, to open the patient record.\n\n### Finding a patient\n\nType in **Search patients by name, MRN, or phone number...**. The list updates a moment after you stop\ntyping. Only the first 20 matches are shown (\"Showing 20 of _n_ patients\"), and there is no next page,\nso if the person you want is not there, type more of the name or use the MRN.\n\n**If instead:** the list says **No patients found**, try a different term, or press **Add Patient** to\nregister them. **Error loading patients** means the list could not be fetched; the message under it is\nthe server's reason.\n\n## Registering a patient\n\n**Go to:** **Patients**, then **New Patient**. This opens **Register New Patient** (`/patients/new`).\n\n1. **Personal Information**: **Title** (optional), **First Name**, **Middle Name**, **Last Name**,\n   **Date of Birth** and **Gender** (Male, Female or Other; it starts on Male, so check it).\n2. **Contact Information**: **Phone Number**, **Alternate Contact**, **Email**, **Address**, **City**,\n   **State**, **Country** and **Postal Code**. **Country**, **City** and **Nationality** are filled in\n   from your organisation's registration defaults; change them if they are wrong for this patient.\n3. **Identity Information**: **Nationality**, **National ID** and **Passport Number**.\n4. **Emergency Contact**: **Name**, **Contact Number** and **Relation**.\n5. Press **Save Patient**.\n\nFields marked * are required: first name, last name, date of birth, gender and phone number.\n\n**Check:** the toast says \"Patient registered successfully\" and you are taken back to the patient\nlist. The new patient's MRN is given by the system.\n\n**If instead:** the form will not save, read the red line under each field:\n\n- \"First name is required\", \"Last name is required\", \"Date of birth is required\";\n- \"Valid phone number is required\": the phone number needs at least 10 characters;\n- \"Invalid email\" when **Email** is filled in but is not an email address.\n\nIf the server refuses, the toast shows its reason, or \"Failed to register patient\".\n\n**Cancel**, or the back arrow, leaves without saving.\n\n## The patient record\n\nOpen a patient from the list to see their record (`/patients/{id}`). The header shows the name, a\nstatus badge (**ACTIVE** green, **INACTIVE** amber, **DECEASED** grey), the **MRN**, age and gender.\n\nThe buttons across the top:\n\n- **Insurance Policies** opens the patient's policies ([Insurance policies](#insurance-policies)).\n- **Patient AI+** opens the AI summary ([Patient AI+](#patient-ai)).\n- The round grid button opens [Care Context](#care-context), when the patient has enough encounters.\n- **Edit Patient** opens the same form as registration, headed **Edit Patient**\n  (`/patients/{id}/edit`). Press **Save Changes**; the toast says \"Patient updated successfully\" and you\n  return to the record.\n\nThe record is laid out in cards:\n\n- **Upcoming Appointment**: the next appointment that is not cancelled, with its **Primary Staff**,\n  **Date & Time**, status and type. Otherwise \"No upcoming appointments scheduled.\"\n- **Previous Encounter**: the most recent encounter, with its staff, time, status and encounter\n  number. Otherwise \"No previous encounters recorded.\"\n- **Personal Information** and **Contact Information**.\n- **Identity Documents**, **Emergency Contact**, **Medical Information** (allergies, chronic\n  conditions, current medications) and **Insurance Information**: each appears only when there is\n  something to show.\n- **Patient Documents** (below).\n- **Audit Information**: when the record was created and last updated, and by whom.\n\n**If instead:** you see **Patient Not Found**, the patient does not exist or you do not have\npermission to view them. Use **Back to Patient List**.\n\n### Patient documents\n\nThe **Patient Documents** card lists scanned and uploaded files for this patient, each with its type,\ndocument number, size, date and a verification badge: **Verified** green, **Rejected** red,\n**Pending** grey.\n\n1. Press **Upload Document**.\n2. Drag a file onto the box, or click it to browse. PDF, image and Word files are accepted; the box\n   says the limit is 10MB.\n3. Choose the **Document Type**: Emirates ID, Passport, Visa, Insurance Card, Lab Report,\n   Prescription, Referral Letter, Consent Form, Medical Report, Radiology Report, Discharge Summary or\n   Other.\n4. Optionally type the **Document Number (optional)**, such as the passport number.\n5. Press **Upload Document**.\n\n**If instead:** the dialog says \"Upload failed. Please try again.\", the file was not stored.\n\nThe eye button opens a document in a new tab. The bin button removes it after you confirm \"Are you\nsure you want to delete this document?\".\n\n## Insurance policies\n\n**Patient Policies** (`/patients/{id}/policies`) lists the patient's insurance policies under\n**Existing policies**. Each shows the payer, policy number, group, relationship, effective and expiry\ndates, whether it is the primary policy, and a status badge: **active** in the theme colour,\n**expired** red, **inactive** and **cancelled** grey.\n\nTo add one:\n\n1. Press **Add policy**.\n2. Choose the **Payer**. Only active payers are listed; your billing team sets them up (see\n   [Billing setup](billing-setup.md)).\n3. Type the **Policy number**, and if you have them the **Group number**, **Relationship**,\n   **Effective date** and **Expiration date**.\n4. **Benefits (JSON)** takes benefit details as JSON, for example `{\"copay\":\"20%\"}`. Leave it empty if\n   you do not have them.\n5. Turn on **Primary policy** if this is the main cover, and set the **Status**.\n6. Press **Add policy**.\n\n**Check:** the toast says \"Policy added\" and the policy appears in the list.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"Select a payer first.\" or \"Policy number is required.\": fill that field in.\n- \"Invalid JSON\" / \"Fix the benefits structure.\": the benefits text is not valid JSON. Correct it or\n  clear it.\n- \"Unable to save policy\" with the server's reason.\n\n**Deactivate** on a policy takes effect straight away, with no confirmation; the toast says \"Policy\ndeactivated\".\n\n## Care Context\n\n**Care Context** (`/patients/{id}/care-context`) is a three-pane workspace for patients with a long\nhistory. It appears only once the patient has at least a set number of encounters, five unless your\nadministrator has changed the **care_context.min_encounters** setting under Configurations (see\n[Administration](administration.md)). Open it with the round grid button on the patient list or the\npatient record. If you open it for a patient below the threshold, you are sent to the patient record.\n\nOpening it collapses the sidebar to give you room. Across the top: **View chart** (the patient record),\n**Patient AI+**, **Results** and **Policies**.\n\n**The left pane** is the patient summary:\n\n- **Risk Flags**, in red chips, or \"No risk flags on file.\"\n- **New** starts an encounter with this patient already filled in ([Creating an encounter](#creating-an-encounter)).\n- **Details** opens the patient's personal, contact, emergency, insurance and identity details.\n- **Vitals**, with when they were last captured, and biomarkers.\n- **Medical Information**: allergies, active conditions and active medications.\n- **Care team**: click a clinician to show only their encounters in the middle pane; click again, or\n  **Clear**, to show all.\n- **Previous Encounter**, **Next Appointment** and **Audit Information**.\n\nThe collapse button folds this pane into a narrow strip showing the MRN, name, age and gender; the\nexpand button brings it back.\n\n**The middle pane** has three views:\n\n- **Encounters · _n_**: every encounter, newest first, as cards.\n- **Timeline**: the same encounters on a timeline.\n- **Cancer**: the oncology timeline, shown only for patients who have one (see [Oncology](oncology.md)).\n\nAbove the encounters is the **Care Narrative · AI Summary**, a written summary of the patient's\nhistory. **Refresh** regenerates it. If the AI service is unavailable you see a local preview built\nfrom the encounter data instead, and the panel says so. Use the arrow buttons, or the left and right\narrow keys, to step through encounters.\n\nAt the bottom, **External records** fetches the patient's records from a Health Information Exchange.\nThe badge shows the consent state: **Consent granted**, **Consent expiring**, **Consent expired**,\n**Consent revoked** or **No consent**. Press **Request & fetch records** (or **Fetch records** when\nconsent is already in place). When it finishes it says how many records were ingested; they are saved\nas patient documents. If the fetch fails, the reason is shown, with **Retry** when a retry is allowed.\n\n**The right pane** shows the selected encounter: its class, priority and chief complaint, then\nsections you can jump to: **Results**, **Documents**, **Claims**, **Pre-Auth**, **Invoices** and\n**Policy**, each with a count. **Clinical charting** opens the chart for that encounter.\n\n## Results\n\n**Results** (`/patients/{id}/results`) lists the patient's lab, imaging and procedure results on a\ntimeline, with the selected result in full on the right. Filter with **All**, **Lab**, **Imaging** or\n**Procedure**. Results come 20 to a page, with **Previous** and **Next**. The back arrow returns to\nCare Context. For how results are entered and reported, see\n[Imaging and procedure results](results-reporting.md) and [Laboratory](lab.md).\n\n## Patient AI+\n\n**Patient AI+** (`/patients-ai/{id}`) is marked **Beta · AI generated**. It shows the patient summary\non the left, the **AI Care Narrative** in the middle and **AI Recommendations** on the right. The\nrecommendations are considerations for the clinician, not orders. **Care Context** at the top opens\nthat workspace.\n\nWhen the patient has an encounter open today (arrived, triaged, in progress or on leave), a banner\noffers **Copy AI Summary to Chart**. It opens that encounter's chart with the summary ready as a\ndraft. Nothing is saved until you review and insert it on the charting page.\n\n**If instead:** the cards say the narrative or recommendations are \"unavailable for this patient right\nnow\", the AI service could not produce them. Try again later.\n\n## Encounters\n\n**Clinical Encounters** (`/encounters`) lists visits. It opens on **Today**.\n\nFilter with:\n\n- **Search encounters by patient or MRN...**;\n- the status list: **All Statuses**, **Planned**, **Arrived**, **Triaged**, **In Progress**,\n  **Finished**, **Cancelled**;\n- the date list: **Today**, **Yesterday** or **Date Range**, which opens a two-month calendar to pick\n  the range.\n\nThe table shows **Patient** (name, MRN, gender and age), **Primary Staff**, **Priority**, **Start\nTime**, **Status** and **Class**. Press **View** to open an encounter. When there is more than one\npage, **Previous** and **Next** appear with \"Showing _n_ of _m_ encounters\".\n\n| Status | Colour |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Planned** | Sky blue |\n| **Arrived** | Cyan |\n| **Triaged** | Amber |\n| **In Progress** | Violet |\n| **On leave** | Orange |\n| **Finished** | Green |\n| **Cancelled** | Rose |\n| **Entered in error** | Red |\n\n**Priority** is coloured too: **stat** solid red, **asap** orange, **urgent** amber, **routine** blue,\n**elective** teal. **Class** uses the standard codes, each in its own colour: **AMB** ambulatory,\n**EMER** emergency, **IMP** inpatient, **VR** virtual, **HH** home health, and so on.\n\n**If instead:** **No encounters found** for today suggests trying yesterday or a date range.\n\n## Creating an encounter\n\n**Go to:** **Encounters**, then **New Encounter** (`/encounters/new`). You also get here from\n**Create Encounter** on an appointment, and from **New** in Care Context.\n\n1. Pick the patient in **Search by name, MRN, or mobile**.\n2. Under **Primary Staff**, type in **Search by staff name or employee ID** and pick the clinician.\n   **Change** lets you pick someone else.\n3. Choose the **Encounter Class** (**Ambulatory**, **Emergency**, **Virtual**, **Inpatient**, **Home\n   Health**) and **Priority** (**Routine**, **Urgent**, **ASAP**, **STAT**, **Elective**).\n4. Set the **Start Date** and **Start Time**.\n5. Press **Create Encounter**.\n\nA new encounter starts as **Planned**.\n\n**From an appointment**, a note says the patient and staff have been pre-filled from the appointment,\nand the patient, staff, date and time are locked to the appointment's.\n\n**Check:** the toast says \"Encounter Created\" and the encounter page opens.\n\n**If instead:** the form shows \"Please select a patient\", \"Please select a staff member\", \"Start date\nis required\" or \"Invalid time format (HH:MM)\". A refusal from the server shows its reason, or \"Failed\nto create encounter\". \"No staff found.\" under the staff search means nobody active matches.\n\n## The encounter page\n\n**Encounter Details** (`/encounters/{id}`) shows the patient, MRN, gender and age, and the\n**Encounter #**.\n\n**Encounter Information** shows the status, class, priority, start and end times, source and primary\nstaff.\n\n**Triage** opens triage for this encounter; see [Charting and triage](clinical-charting.md).\n\n### Changing the status\n\nPick a new status in the list at the top right: **Planned**, **Arrived**, **Triaged**, **In Progress**,\n**Finished** or **Cancelled**. It saves as soon as you pick it, with no confirmation. The toast says\n\"Status Updated\". If the change is refused, the toast shows the server's reason, or \"Failed to update\nstatus\".\n\n### Adding coverage\n\n**Encounter Coverages** links the visit to a payer or policy, so eligibility, prior authorisation and\nclaims use the right cover.\n\n1. Press **Add coverage**.\n2. Choose a **Policy (optional)** from the patient's active policies; this fills in the payer and plan.\n   Or choose the **Payer** directly.\n3. Set the **Financial class** (**Insurance**, **Corporate**, **TPA**, **Government**, **Cash / Self\n   Pay**) and **Coverage level** (**Primary**, **Secondary**, **Tertiary**, **Self-pay**).\n4. Fill in what you have of **Plan name**, **Network**, **Member name**, **Member ID**, **Copay\n   (currency)** and **Coinsurance (%)**. The two snapshot fields take JSON and can be left empty.\n5. Press **Save coverage**.\n\n**Check:** the toast says \"Coverage added\" and the coverage appears with an **Active** badge.\n\n**If instead:** \"Invalid JSON\" means a snapshot field is not valid JSON. \"Unable to save coverage\"\nshows the server's reason.\n\nFor what happens to coverage afterwards, see [Revenue cycle and claims](revenue-cycle.md).\n\n## Appointments\n\n**Appointments** (`/scheduling/appointments`) lists your facility's appointments for one day, starting\nwith today. Move between days with **Previous**, **Next** and **Today**.\n\nFilter by status: **All Statuses**, **Scheduled**, **Confirmed**, **Checked In**, **In Progress**,\n**Completed**, **Cancelled**, **No Show**.\n\nThe table shows **Time** (start and end), **Patient** (name, MRN, gender and age), **Type**, **Status**,\n**Staff** (\"Not assigned\" when nobody is), **Visit Type** and **Actions**.\n\n| Status | Colour |\n| --- | --- |\n| **scheduled** | Sky blue |\n| **confirmed** | Blue |\n| **checked in** | Cyan |\n| **in progress** | Violet |\n| **completed** | Green |\n| **cancelled** | Rose |\n| **no show** | Orange |\n| **rescheduled** | Amber |\n\nThe **…** menu on each row has:\n\n- **View Details**, the appointment page;\n- **Create Encounter**, not offered for a cancelled appointment;\n- **Reschedule**, not offered for a cancelled or completed appointment ([Rescheduling](#rescheduling)).\n\n**If instead:** **No appointments found** with no filters set offers **Book Appointment**.\n\n## Booking an appointment\n\n**Go to:** **Appointments**, then **Book Appointment**. This opens **Book New Appointment**\n(`/scheduling/appointments/new`).\n\n1. Pick the patient in **Search by name, MRN, or mobile**.\n2. Choose the **Appointment Type**: **Health Checkup**, **Consultation** (the default), **Follow-up**,\n   **Vaccination**, **Lab Test**, **Imaging**, **Procedure** or **Emergency**.\n3. Choose the **Preferred Staff (Optional)**. Only staff with availability on\n   [Staff Scheduling](#staff-scheduling) are listed.\n4. Set the **Appointment Duration**: 15, 30 (the default) or 45 minutes, 1, 1.5 or 2 hours.\n5. Pick the date in **Select Date**. Past dates cannot be picked. For a **Consultation**, choosing a\n   staff member sets the date to today for you.\n6. With a staff member and date chosen, **Available Time Slots** shows their free start times in\n   15-minute steps, with a count. Click one: it is highlighted with a tick, across the whole duration,\n   and **Start Time** and **End Time** are filled in. Switch the list to **All Slots** to see the\n   whole day from 8 am to 6 pm, with times that are not free greyed out.\n7. Without a staff member there are no slots; type the **Start Time** and **End Time** yourself.\n8. Choose the **Visit Type**: **In-Person**, **Telehealth** or **Home Visit**.\n9. Add any **Notes**, then press **Book Appointment**.\n\n**Check:** the toast says \"Appointment Booked\" and the new appointment's page opens.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"Please select a patient\", \"Appointment type is required\", \"Appointment date is required\" or\n  \"Invalid time format (HH:MM)\" under the field concerned.\n- **No available slots**: the staff member has no availability that day. Pick another day or another\n  clinician.\n- A refusal from the server shows its reason, or \"Failed to book appointment\".\n\n## The appointment page\n\n**Appointment Details** (`/scheduling/appointments/{id}`) shows:\n\n- **Appointment Information**: status, type, start and end time, visit type and notes;\n- **Patient & Staff**: the patient with MRN, **Assigned Staff**, and the facility and space when set;\n- **Allocated Resources**, when staff, equipment or rooms are attached, each with its role, status\n  and time;\n- **Cancellation Details** for a cancelled appointment: the reason and when it was cancelled;\n- **Metadata**: when it was created and last updated.\n\nThe buttons at the top:\n\n- **Create Encounter** starts an encounter from this appointment. Not shown once cancelled.\n- **Reschedule** and **Cancel Appointment**. Not shown once cancelled or completed.\n\n### Rescheduling\n\n1. Press **Reschedule**. The **Reschedule Appointment** dialog opens on the current date and times.\n2. Pick the **New Date** and **Duration**.\n3. If the appointment has a staff member, **Available Slots** lists their free times; click one to\n   fill in the times. Otherwise type the **Start Time** and **End Time**.\n4. Add a **Reason (optional)** and press **Confirm**.\n\n**Check:** the toast says \"Appointment Rescheduled\".\n\n**If instead:** \"Please pick a new date and time before rescheduling.\" or \"End time must be after\nstart time.\" A server refusal shows under \"Unable to reschedule\".\n\n### Cancelling\n\nPress **Cancel Appointment** and confirm \"Are you sure you want to cancel this appointment?\". The\nreason is recorded as \"Cancelled by user\". The toast says \"Appointment Cancelled\" and you return to\nthe list.\n\n## Staff Calendar\n\n**Staff Calendar** (`/scheduling/calendar`) shows one staff member's appointments and encounters as a\ndiary.\n\n1. Pick someone in **Select a staff member...**. Only staff with availability on Staff Scheduling are\n   listed.\n2. Choose **Day**, **Week** (the default) or **Month**, and move with the arrows and **Today**. The\n   range shown sits beside them.\n\nThe heading gives the staff member's name, type and the number of events. Each event shows the\npatient, MRN, time and type, with a status badge and whether it is an **appointment** or an\n**encounter**:\n\n- appointments have a blue left edge, encounters a green one;\n- an icon shows the source: calendar for scheduled, people for walk-in, alert for emergency, camera\n  for telemedicine;\n- the status dot is blue for scheduled, green for confirmed, yellow for in progress, grey for\n  completed or finished, red for cancelled and orange for no show.\n\nToday is highlighted. In **Month**, each day shows up to three events and \"+_n_ more\"; click a day to\nopen it in **Day** view. In **Week** and **Month**, hover over an event for its details. Click an\nevent to open the appointment or encounter. A legend under the diary repeats the colours and icons.\n\n**Day** view runs from 7 am to 8 pm, so events outside those hours are not shown there; use **Week**.\n\n## Staff Scheduling\n\n**Staff Scheduling** (`/scheduling/staff`) sets each clinician's recurring weekly availability. It\ndrives the staff lists and free slots in booking, rescheduling and the Staff Calendar.\n\n1. Pick the **Staff Member**; the first is chosen for you. Their qualification, languages and\n   specialties show underneath.\n2. **Effective date** (today by default) shows the slots in force on that date. Turn on **Include\n   expired slots** to see old ones too.\n\n**Weekly availability** lists the slots: **Day**, **Time**, **Effective** (from and to), **Availability**\n(**Available** green, **Unavailable** red), **Type** and **Notes**.\n\n### Adding one slot\n\n1. Press **New slot**.\n2. Choose the **Day of Week**, **Start Time** and **End Time** (9:00 to 17:00 to begin with).\n3. Set **Effective From** (today) and, if it ends, **Effective To**.\n4. **Schedule Type** is free text and starts as \"regular\".\n5. **Facility** is your current facility and cannot be changed here.\n6. Leave **Staff available during this slot** on for working time; turn it off to record time they\n   are not available.\n7. Add **Notes** if useful and press **Save schedule**.\n\n**Check:** the toast says \"Schedule created\".\n\n### Adding the same slot on several days\n\nPress **Weekly slots**. Pick the **Weekdays** (Monday to Friday are chosen to begin with), then fill\nin the same fields and press **Create weekly schedule**. With no day picked it says \"Select at least\none weekday.\" and will not save. The toast says \"Weekly schedule created\".\n\n### Changing or removing a slot\n\nThe pencil opens **Edit schedule**; press **Save schedule**. The bin removes the slot after you confirm\n\"Are you sure you want to delete this availability slot?\".\n\n**If instead:** a refusal shows as \"Unable to create schedule\", \"Unable to update schedule\", \"Unable\nto create weekly schedule\" or \"Unable to delete schedule\", with the server's reason.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Cancel** in the **…** menu on the Appointments list only shows \"Cancel functionality will be\n  implemented.\" Cancel from the appointment page instead.\n- The Appointments search box matches the appointment type and the patient's internal ID only, not\n  the patient's name or MRN.\n- These screens have no control to confirm an appointment, check a patient in or mark a no-show.\n- The patient list shows only the first 20 matches, with no next page.\n- **Call** and **Message** in Care Context do nothing yet.\n- Document verification (**Pending**, **Verified**, **Rejected**) is shown but cannot be changed from\n  the patient record.\n",
  "patient-engagement.md": "---\ntitle: 'Patient engagement'\ngroup: 'Engagement and wellness'\norder: 150\nroutes: ['/prm', '/pe-setup']\ndescription: 'Patient engagement events, tasks and messages, and the PE Setup screens for rules, templates, preferences, provider callbacks and the webhook tester.'\n---\n\nPatient engagement (PRM) reacts to things that happen to a patient, such as a confirmed appointment or a\nready lab result. Each of these is an **event**. **Rules** decide what an event should cause: send a message\nfrom a **template**, or create a **task** for staff. This page covers the day-to-day screens and the setup\nbehind them.\n\nThe **Patient Engagement** section of the sidebar is shown when your organisation has patient engagement\nswitched on. It lists three screens: **Events**, **Tasks** and **Messages**. Opening `/prm` on its own takes\nyou straight to **Events**.\n\nThe setup screens (**Rules**, **Templates**, **Preferences**, **Provider callbacks** and **Webhook tester**)\nare not in that section. You reach them from **PE Setup**, under **Administration** in the sidebar, which is\nalways shown. Their **Back** buttons return you to **PE Setup**.\n\n## Finding a patient\n\nSeveral screens below filter or record by patient using the same search box.\n\n1. Type a name, MRN or mobile number in **Search by name, MRN, or mobile**.\n2. Pick the patient from the list. Each row shows the name, phone (or **No phone**), sex, age and MRN.\n3. The chosen patient appears in a card. Press **Change** to clear it and search again.\n\nWhile it looks, the box says \"Searching patients...\". If nothing matches it says \"No patients found.\"\n\n## Events\n\n**PRM Events** (`/prm/events`) lists the events the engagement service has received, across all patients,\n25 to a page.\n\n**Filters**:\n\n- the patient search;\n- **Event Type**, such as `appointment_confirmed`, `lab_result_ready` or `payment_overdue`;\n- **Entity Type**, the kind of record the event is about, such as `appointment`, `invoice` or `lab_result`.\n\nEach filter starts at **All**. Changing any filter returns you to page 1.\n\nEach event row shows four columns:\n\n| Column | What it shows |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Occurred** | The date and time the event happened. |\n| **Patient** | The patient name (or **Unknown**) and **MRN** (or **N/A**). |\n| **Event** | The event type and its subtype (a dash when there is none). |\n| **Entity** | The entity type and the **Severity** number: 0 info, 1 warning, 2 critical. |\n\nUnder the list, \"Showing 1-25 of 140\" tells you where you are. Use **Previous** and **Next** to page; each is\ngreyed out at the start or end. If there is nothing to show you see \"No events found.\"\n\n### Recording an event by hand\n\n**New PRM Event** (`/prm/events/new`) sends an event in yourself, as another system would. It is mainly for\ntesting rules. Press **New Event** on the events list.\n\n1. Find the patient.\n2. Choose **Source System** (it starts at `zeal-clinical`; the others are `zeal-rcm`, `zeal-foundation` and\n   `external`).\n3. Choose **Event Type**. **Event Subtype** then offers the subtypes for that type, for example\n   `new_patient`, `existing_patient` or `rescheduled` for `appointment_confirmed`. Changing the type clears\n   the subtype. Each has a **Clear** button.\n4. Choose **Severity**: **Info**, **Warning** or **Critical**. It starts at **Info**.\n5. Check **Occurred At**. It starts at now.\n6. Choose **Entity Type** and type the record's **Entity ID**.\n7. Type the event details in **Payload JSON**.\n8. Optionally add a **Correlation ID**. Type a **Dedupe Key**: an event with a key already used is skipped.\n9. Press **Ingest Event**. It says **Submitting...** while it sends.\n\n**Check:** a toast says **Event ingested**, with how many jobs were created and rules evaluated, for example\n\"2 jobs created · 3 rules evaluated\". A **Latest Ingestion** card shows the **Event ID**, **Duplicate**\n(Yes or No), **Rules Evaluated** and **Jobs Created**. The form stays filled, so you can change a field and\nsend another. If the dedupe key was already used, the toast says **Duplicate event skipped** instead.\n\n**If instead:** the form refuses and marks the field:\n\n- \"Enter a valid patient UUID\": no patient chosen.\n- \"Event type is required\", \"Entity type is required\", \"Occurred at is required\", \"Dedupe key is required\".\n- \"Enter a valid entity UUID\": the **Entity ID** is not a UUID.\n- \"Payload must be valid JSON\": the payload does not parse.\n- If the server refuses, a toast says **Ingestion failed** with its reason, or \"Failed to ingest event.\".\n\n## Tasks\n\n**Tasks** (`/prm/tasks`) lists engagement tasks for staff, such as follow-up calls or document requests.\nRules with the **Create Task** action add them here; you can also create them by hand.\n\nFilter by:\n\n- the patient search;\n- **Assigned User ID**: type a user's ID;\n- **Status**: **All**, **Pending**, **In Progress**, **Completed**, **Cancelled** or **Skipped**.\n\n**Task Results** shows each task's type, title (or **Untitled task**), the patient's ID, and its status in\nplain text. Press **View** to open it. If nothing matches you see \"No tasks found.\"\n\n### Creating a task\n\n**Create Task** (`/prm/tasks/new`). Press **Create Task** on the tasks list.\n\n1. Find the patient.\n2. Choose **Task Type**: `follow_up`, `document_request`, `consent_needed` or `payment_reminder`.\n3. Type a **Title** and, optionally, a **Description**.\n4. Optionally choose **Priority**: **Low**, **Medium**, **High** or **Urgent**.\n5. Optionally fill **Assigned User ID**, **Assigned Role**, **Due At**, **Related Entity Type** and\n   **Related Entity ID**.\n6. Press **Create Task**.\n\n**Check:** a toast says **Task created** (\"Task is ready in PRM.\") and you return to the tasks list.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"Patient ID must be a UUID\": no patient chosen.\n- \"Task type is required\" or \"Title is required\".\n- If the server refuses, a toast says **Creation failed** with its reason, or \"Failed to create task.\".\n\n### Working a task\n\n**Task Details** (`/prm/tasks/{taskId}`) lets you move a task on. Change **Status**, **Assigned User ID**,\n**Outcome** or **Completed At**, then press **Update Task**. A toast says **Task updated** (\"Changes saved\nsuccessfully.\"). If the server refuses, it says **Update failed** with the reason.\n\nTo remove a task press **Delete**. The browser asks \"Delete this task? This action cannot be undone.\" On\nconfirming, a toast says **Task deleted** and you return to the list.\n\nThe **Raw Payload** card underneath shows everything the server holds for the task, as JSON. There is no\n**Back** button on this screen; use the sidebar or your browser.\n\n## Messages\n\n**Messages** (`/prm/messages`) lists the messages engagement has sent or tried to send to patients.\n\nFilter by:\n\n- the patient search;\n- **Channel**: **All**, **Sms**, **Whatsapp**, **Email**, **In App** or **Push**;\n- **Status**: type a status exactly as the server stores it.\n\n**Message Results** shows each message's channel, subject (or **Message**) and **Status**. Press **View** to\nopen it. If nothing matches you see \"No messages found.\"\n\n**Message Details** (`/prm/messages/{messageId}`) shows the whole message record as JSON. It is read-only. If\nthe message cannot be found it says \"Message not found.\"\n\n## PE Setup\n\n**PE Setup** (`/pe-setup`) is the front door to engagement configuration. It has five tiles; press one to open\nthat screen:\n\n| Tile | Opens | Use it to |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Rules** | `/prm/rules` | Configure event-driven rules. |\n| **Templates** | `/prm/templates` | Manage message templates for each channel. |\n| **Preferences** | `/prm/preferences` | Update a patient's communication preferences and consent. |\n| **Provider callbacks** | `/prm/providers/callbacks` | Review delivery updates sent back by messaging providers. |\n| **Webhook tester** | `/prm/providers/webhooks` | Send a test provider webhook. |\n\n## Rules\n\n**Rules** (`/prm/rules`) lists engagement rules. Filter by **Category** (type it) and **Active** (**All**,\n**Active**, **Inactive**). **Rule Results** shows each rule's category (or **Uncategorized**), name, the\n**Trigger** event type, and **Active** or **Inactive**. Press **View** to open a rule. If nothing matches you\nsee \"No rules found.\"\n\n### Creating a rule\n\n**Create Rule** (`/prm/rules/new`). Press **Create Rule** on the rules list.\n\n1. Type a **Code** and **Name**.\n2. Choose **Category**: `appointment_reminders`, `billing_followup`, `care_coordination`, `wellness`,\n   `chronic_care`, `post_op` or `marketing`.\n3. Choose **Trigger Event Type** and, optionally, **Trigger Event Subtype**. The rule runs when a matching\n   event arrives.\n4. Choose **Schedule Mode**: **Immediate**, or **Delayed** with a **Delay (seconds)**.\n5. Choose **Action Type**:\n   - **Send Message**: a **Template** list appears, showing active templates. Picking one fills **Action\n     Payload (JSON)** with its channel and code, and shows the template's **Channel** underneath.\n   - **Create Task**: type the task details into **Action Payload (JSON)** yourself.\n6. Optionally set **Priority**, **Cooldown (seconds)**, **Idempotency Window (seconds)**, **Max Executions Per\n   Day**, **Effective From** and **Effective To**.\n   - **Cooldown** is the least time before the rule can run again for the same patient. A repeat event\n     inside it is skipped, so the patient is not spammed.\n   - **Idempotency Window** stops near-identical events (retries, duplicates) triggering the rule twice\n     within that many seconds.\n7. Set **Active** (it starts at **Active**) and, optionally, a **Description**.\n8. Type the rule's conditions in **Condition Expression (JSON)**.\n9. Press **Create Rule**.\n\n**Check:** a toast says **Rule created** and you return to the rules list.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"Code is required\", \"Name is required\", \"Category is required\", \"Trigger event type is required\".\n- \"Condition JSON is required\" or \"Condition must be valid JSON\".\n- \"Action payload JSON is required\" or \"Action payload must be valid JSON\".\n- If the server refuses, a toast says **Creation failed** with its reason, or \"Failed to create rule.\".\n\n### Changing or deleting a rule\n\n**Rule Details** (`/prm/rules/{ruleId}`) shows the same form filled in. Change it and press **Update Rule**; a\ntoast says **Rule updated**. To switch a rule off without deleting it, set **Active** to **Inactive**.\n\n**Delete** asks \"Delete this rule? This action cannot be undone.\" and, on confirming, says **Rule deleted**\nand returns you to the list. A **Raw Payload** card shows the stored rule as JSON.\n\n## Templates\n\n**Templates** (`/prm/templates`) lists message templates. Filter by **Channel** (**SMS**, **WhatsApp**,\n**Email**, **In App**, **Push**), **Language**, **Category** and **Active**. **Template Results** shows each\ntemplate's channel, name, **Category** and **Active** or **Inactive**. Press **View** to open one. If nothing\nmatches you see \"No templates found.\"\n\n### Creating a template\n\n**Create Template** (`/prm/templates/new`). Press **Create Template** on the templates list.\n\n1. Type a **Code** and **Name**. Rules refer to the template by its code.\n2. Choose **Category**: `transactional`, `promotional`, `care` or `billing`.\n3. Choose **Channel** (it starts at **Sms**).\n4. Check **Language** (it starts at `en`) and, for email, type a **Subject**.\n5. Set **Approval Status**: **Draft** (the default), **Pending**, **Approved** or **Rejected**.\n6. Optionally type a **Version** number, set **Active** and add a **Description**.\n7. Type the message text in **Body**.\n8. Describe the template's placeholders in **Variables Schema (JSON)**.\n9. Press **Create Template**.\n\n**Check:** a toast says **Template created** (\"Template is ready to use.\") and you return to the list.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"Code is required\", \"Name is required\", \"Category is required\", \"Body is required\".\n- \"Variables schema JSON is required\" or \"Variables schema must be valid JSON\".\n- If the server refuses, a toast says **Creation failed** with its reason, or \"Failed to create template.\".\n\n### Changing or deleting a template\n\n**Template Details** (`/prm/templates/{templateId}`) shows the form filled in. Press **Update Template** to\nsave (**Template updated**). **Delete** asks \"Delete this template? This action cannot be undone.\" and then\nsays **Template deleted**. A **Raw Payload** card shows the stored template as JSON.\n\n## Preferences\n\n**Patient Preferences** (`/prm/preferences`) holds one patient's communication preferences and consent, edited\nas JSON.\n\n1. Find the patient.\n2. Press **Load Preferences**. The **Preferences JSON** box fills with what is stored.\n3. Edit the JSON.\n4. Press **Save Preferences**.\n\n**Check:** a toast says **Preferences updated** (\"Patient preferences saved.\").\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- No patient chosen: **Patient ID required**.\n- The JSON does not parse: **Invalid JSON** (\"Preferences must be valid JSON.\").\n- The server refuses: **Update failed** with its reason, or \"Failed to update preferences.\".\n\n## Provider callbacks\n\n**Provider Callbacks** (`/prm/providers/callbacks`) lists the delivery updates messaging providers have sent\nback, such as delivered or failed receipts. Filter by **Channel** and **Processed** (**All**, **Processed**,\n**Unprocessed**). Each callback is shown as raw JSON. If nothing matches you see \"No callbacks found.\"\n\n## Webhook tester\n\n**Provider Webhook Tester** (`/prm/providers/webhooks`) posts a pretend provider webhook, to check how\nengagement handles it.\n\n1. Choose **Channel** (it starts at **Sms**).\n2. Type the provider's payload in **Payload JSON**.\n3. Press **Send Webhook**.\n\n**Check:** a toast says **Webhook sent** (\"Provider webhook was posted successfully.\").\n\n**If instead:** **Invalid JSON** (\"Webhook payload must be valid JSON.\"), or **Webhook failed** with the\nserver's reason.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- Most engagement screens are technical. Messages, message details, provider callbacks and the **Raw\n  Payload** cards show raw JSON, and preferences, rule conditions, action payloads and template variables\n  are edited as raw JSON with no guided form.\n- Task and rule lists show statuses as plain text, not coloured badges. Tasks show the patient's ID rather\n  than their name.\n- **Assigned User ID** (on tasks) and **Status** (on messages) are free-text boxes; there is no user or status\n  picker.\n- **Task Details** has no **Back** button. **Rule Details** and **Template Details** go **Back** to **PE\n  Setup**, not to their list.\n- The **Patient ID required** toast on **Preferences** tells you to \"Enter a patient UUID\", but the screen\n  uses the patient search; choose a patient there.\n- After creating a rule the toast says \"The rule is now active in PRM.\" even if you saved it as\n  **Inactive**.\n\nSee also [Wellness](wellness.md), [Membership](membership.md) and [Administration](administration.md).\n",
  "pharmacy.md": "---\ntitle: 'Pharmacy'\ngroup: 'Diagnostics and pharmacy'\norder: 90\nroutes: ['/pharmacy', '/pharmacy/queue', '/pharmacy/dispensings', '/pharmacy/stock', '/pharmacy/alerts']\ndescription: 'Dispensing prescriptions from the Dispense queue, direct (OTC and paper) dispensing, receiving and adjusting stock batches, and the low-stock and expiry alerts.'\n---\n\nShown when your organisation has Pharmacy switched on.\n\nThe **Pharmacy** section of the sidebar has three entries:\n\n- **Dispense** (`/pharmacy/queue`): the prescriptions waiting for the pharmacy, and the history of\n  everything dispensed.\n- **Stock Management** (`/pharmacy/stock`): every stock batch on the shelves.\n- **Alerts** (`/pharmacy/alerts`): batches running low or about to expire.\n\nOpening `/pharmacy` on its own takes you straight to **Dispense**. So does the old dispensing list\naddress, `/pharmacy/dispensings`, which now lives in the **History** tab.\n\nThe usual day runs like this: a clinician prescribes, the prescription appears in the queue, you\nprocess it, verify it, pick a stock batch for each drug and dispense. Stock comes down as you\ndispense, and the charge is posted to the patient's bill.\n\n## The dispense queue\n\n**Dispense** (`/pharmacy/queue`) opens on the **Queue** tab. It lists active prescriptions, one card\nper prescription with all its drug lines. A prescription leaves the queue once it is dispensed, so\nthe queue is only the work still to do.\n\nAbove the cards:\n\n- **Search by patient name or MRN...**: narrows the cards as you type.\n- **Direct Dispense**: dispense without an electronic prescription (see\n  [Direct dispense](#direct-dispense)).\n- **All**, **Outpatient** and **Inpatient** tabs, each with a count in brackets.\n\nEach card shows:\n\n- the prescription number, with a **v2**, **v3** badge when the prescription has been amended, the\n  encounter type (outpatient or inpatient) and the encounter number;\n- the patient's name, **MRN**, age and gender;\n- the drug lines: name, dosage, frequency, duration and **Qty**. Press the **_n_ drugs** line to fold\n  them away or open them again;\n- **Prescribed:** with the date and time, the prescriber, and for an inpatient the ward and bed;\n- any note the prescriber left, after **Note:**;\n- a status badge, and a button on the right.\n\n| Badge | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| **New** | Nobody in the pharmacy has started on it. The button says **Process**. |\n| **queued** | A dispensing record exists but the prescription is not verified yet. The button says **View**. |\n| **verified** | Verified and ready to dispense. The button says **View**. |\n\n### Processing a prescription\n\n1. Find the patient's card. Search by name or MRN if the queue is long.\n2. Press **Process**. The system opens a dispensing record for the prescription: outpatient\n   prescriptions go to the **Outpatient** counter channel, inpatient ones to the **Inpatient Ward**\n   channel.\n3. The dispensing record opens (`/pharmacy/dispensings/{id}`). Carry on from\n   [The dispensing record](#the-dispensing-record).\n\n**Check:** the card now says **queued** and its button says **View**. Pressing **View** takes you\nback to the same record, so two pharmacists cannot open two records for one prescription.\n\n**If instead:** the server refuses to open the record when one already exists (\"A dispensing record\nalready exists for this prescription\", with its number), or when the prescription is no longer\nactive (\"Prescription is not active\", with its status).\n\nThe queue says **No prescriptions in queue** when there is nothing to do.\n\n## Dispensing history\n\nThe **History** tab on **Dispense** lists every dispensing record. Filter it with the\n**All statuses** list. The columns:\n\n- **Dispensing #**;\n- **Patient**, with the **MRN** under the name;\n- **Source**: digital prescription, otc, paper op (outpatient paper prescription) or paper ward;\n- **Channel**: **Outpatient**, **Inpatient Ward**, **Bedside** or **Emergency**;\n- **Status**;\n- **Date**: when the record was opened.\n\nPress the eye button at the end of a row to open the record.\n\n## Direct dispense\n\n**Direct Dispense** (`/pharmacy/dispensings/new`) is for a sale over the counter or a paper\nprescription, when there is no electronic prescription to work from.\n\n**Go to:** **Dispense**, then **Direct Dispense** next to the search.\n\n1. Under **Patient**, search for and pick the patient. You must pick one.\n2. The screen looks for the patient's active encounter:\n   - If there is one, a blue panel says **Active Outpatient Encounter**, **Active Inpatient\n     Encounter** or **Active Emergency Encounter**, with its number, status and start time, and\n     \"Dispensing type pre-selected based on encounter\".\n   - If not, it says **No active encounter found for this patient**, and the dispensing is not tied\n     to any encounter.\n3. Under **Dispensing Type**, choose one. Only the types that fit the encounter are offered:\n\n   | Type | Offered for | Default for |\n   | --- | --- | --- |\n   | **Over the Counter (OTC)**: direct sale without a prescription | outpatient, emergency, no encounter | emergency, no encounter |\n   | **Paper OP Prescription**: from an outpatient doctor | outpatient, no encounter | outpatient |\n   | **Paper Ward Prescription**: from a ward or inpatient | inpatient, no encounter | inpatient |\n\n   The chosen type is edged in the primary colour and says **Selected**. **Dispensing channel:**\n   under the list shows where it will be recorded: **Inpatient Ward** for a ward prescription,\n   **Outpatient Counter** for the rest.\n4. For a paper prescription you can also fill in:\n   - **Paper Prescription Reference** (optional): the number written on the paper.\n   - **Upload Prescription Scan** (optional): press **Click to upload a photo or PDF of the\n     prescription**. It uploads at once to the patient's documents and says **Uploaded successfully**\n     with a **Saved** badge. Press the cross to remove it.\n5. Press **Create & Process**. The dispensing opens straight on the dispense screen, with no\n   medicines on it yet. Add them as in [Dispensing medicines](#dispensing-medicines).\n\n**If instead:** the upload refuses a file that is not a PDF or image (\"Only PDF, JPEG, PNG, or WebP\nfiles are allowed\"), a file over 10 MB (\"File must be smaller than 10 MB\"), or fails (\"Upload failed.\nPlease try again.\"). **Create & Process** stays greyed out while a scan is still uploading.\n\nOTC and paper dispensings skip the separate verify step: the system verifies them for you when you\ndispense.\n\n## The dispensing record\n\nThe dispensing record (`/pharmacy/dispensings/{id}`) is where you verify, dispense, and follow a\ndispensing through to the ward.\n\nThe banner at the top shows **Dispensing**, the dispensing number, its status, and the encounter\nnumber. Under it: **Patient**, **Age**, **Gender**, **MRN**, **Channel**, and for an inpatient the\n**Ward** and bed. When the encounter was triaged, the banner also shows **Triage Vitals** and, in red,\n**Allergies**. Check the allergies before you dispense.\n\nThe status badge is coloured:\n\n| Status | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **queued** | Grey | Opened, not verified. |\n| **verified** | Amber | Verified, ready to dispense. |\n| **dispensed** | Green | Medicines handed over and stock deducted. |\n| **partially_dispensed** | Blue | Part of the prescription dispensed. |\n| **cancelled** | Red | Cancelled before dispensing. |\n| **returned** | Grey | Returned after dispensing. |\n\nBelow the banner:\n\n- **Prescription**: for an electronic prescription, its number, version, status and each prescribed\n  drug with dosage, frequency, duration, instructions and **Qty**, then **Prescribed by:** and any\n  note.\n- **Dispensed Medications**: once dispensed, each drug given with its strength, form, **Batch:**,\n  **Expires:**, instructions, a **Substituted** badge where a substitute was given, the quantity and\n  the line amount in AED.\n- **Timeline**: **Queued**, **Verified**, **Dispensed**, **Dispatched to Ward** and **Ward Received**,\n  each with its time once it has happened, and **Charge Posted** in green once the charge reached the\n  bill.\n\n### Verifying a prescription\n\nAn electronic prescription must be verified before it can be dispensed.\n\n1. Read the prescription against the patient's allergies and vitals in the banner.\n2. Press **Verify Prescription**.\n\n**Check:** the status turns amber **verified**, **Verified** appears in the timeline, and the button\nbecomes **Dispense Medication**.\n\n**If instead:** the server refuses if the prescription has been stopped or replaced since it was\nqueued (\"Prescription is no longer active\", with its status).\n\n### The buttons\n\nWhich buttons you see depends on the status:\n\n- **Verify Prescription**: a queued electronic prescription.\n- **Dispense Medication**: a verified record. **Add Medicines & Dispense**: a queued OTC or paper\n  dispensing. Both open the dispense screen.\n- **Confirm Ward Receipt**: a dispensed record that has been dispatched to the ward but not yet\n  received. Press it when the ward confirms they have the medicines; **Ward Received** is stamped on\n  the timeline.\n- **Cancel** (red): a queued or verified record. It cancels at once, with the reason \"Cancelled by\n  pharmacist\", and there is no confirmation. A cancelled prescription goes back to **New** in the\n  queue.\n\n## Dispensing medicines\n\nThe dispense screen (`/pharmacy/dispensings/{id}/dispense`) opens from **Dispense Medication** or\n**Add Medicines & Dispense**, and straight after **Create & Process** on a direct dispense. The\npatient banner is the same as on the record.\n\n**Medication Dispensing Plan** has one row per drug, in two halves: **Prescribed Medication** on the\nleft and **Stock Batch Selection** on the right. Its header counts **_n_ of _m_ batches selected**.\n\n- For an electronic prescription, every prescribed drug is already listed, marked **Prescribed**,\n  with its dosage, frequency and quantity. The prescriber's instructions are copied into\n  **Instructions**.\n- For OTC and paper dispensings the plan starts empty: \"Use the search below to add medications\".\n\n### Picking stock and dispensing\n\n1. On each prescribed row, under **Select Stock Batch**, the search is pre-filled with the drug code.\n   Type at least two characters to search by batch or drug name. Only active batches are offered,\n   each with its **Batch:**, **Exp:** and **On hand:**. Pick one.\n2. Check the quantity. **Qty (max _n_)** starts at the prescribed quantity, or the stock on hand if\n   that is less. Change it if you need to.\n3. Edit **Instructions** if needed, for example \"Take 1 tablet twice daily after food\".\n4. If you give a generic instead of the drug prescribed, tick **Generic substitution** and type the\n   **Reason for substitution…**.\n5. To use another batch, press **change** next to the batch. To drop a drug, press the bin.\n6. To add a drug that is not on the prescription, use **Add medication by name or code…** under the\n   plan. It adds a row with the batch already chosen. On a prescription this is labelled **Add extra\n   or substitution drug**.\n7. Under **Patient Counselling**, tick **Counselling provided to patient** if you counselled them, and\n   add **Counselling Notes** on use, side effects and storage.\n8. Press **Dispense _n_ Items**. Only rows with a batch chosen are dispensed; rows without one are\n   left out.\n\n**Check:** you land back on the record, the status is green **dispensed**, the drugs appear under\n**Dispensed Medications**, the prescription leaves the queue, and stock on each batch has come down.\nWhen the batch is linked to a billing item, the charge is posted to the patient's bill and **Charge\nPosted** appears on the timeline.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- The button stays greyed out until at least one row has a batch: \"Select a stock batch for each drug\n  to proceed\", or with an empty plan \"Add at least one medication to dispense\".\n- An electronic prescription that is not verified yet does not open the plan. The screen says the\n  dispensing is **queued** and to \"verify the prescription before dispensing\", with **Go to Detail\n  Page**.\n- The server refuses a quantity larger than the batch has free (on hand less anything reserved):\n  \"Insufficient stock for\" the drug and batch, with the amount available.\n\n## Stock management\n\n**Stock Management** (`/pharmacy/stock`) lists every stock batch, 20 to a page. Each batch of each\ndrug is its own row.\n\n- **Search by name, code or batch…**: searches a moment after you stop typing.\n- **All statuses**: filter by **active**, **quarantined**, **expired**, **depleted** or **recalled**.\n- **Receive Stock**: book in a new batch.\n\nThe columns:\n\n- **Drug**: name, generic name or code, form and strength. A **catalog** badge means the batch is\n  linked to the medication catalogue; press it to open the catalogue entry.\n- **Batch**.\n- **On Hand**: in red with a warning sign when at or below the batch's reorder level.\n- **Expiry**: month and year, in amber with a clock when it expires within 30 days.\n- **Location**.\n- **Status**: solid for **active**, red for **quarantined**, **expired** and **recalled**, grey for\n  **depleted**.\n- **Billing**: a **linked** badge when the batch is linked to a billing item; press it to open the\n  item in billing setup ([Billing setup](billing-setup.md)). A dash means dispensing this batch posts\n  no charge.\n\nThe foot of the table says **Showing _a_–_b_ of _n_ batches**, with **Previous** and **Next**. Press\nthe eye button on a row to open the batch.\n\n### Receiving a stock batch\n\n**Receive Stock Batch** (`/pharmacy/stock/new`).\n\n1. Under **Search Medication Catalog**, type at least two characters and pick the medication. The\n   drug code, name, generic name, dosage form and strength fill in and lock.\n2. The screen looks up the billing item for that medication:\n   - green **Billing item resolved:** with its code, description and AED price, or\n   - amber \"No billing item mapped for this medication. Stock will be created without a billing\n     link.\" Dispensing that batch will then post no charge. Map the medication in billing setup first\n     if it should be charged.\n3. Fill in **Unit** (tablets, vials, bottles…).\n4. Under **Batch Details**, fill in **Batch Number**, **Expiry Date** and **Quantity Received**, and\n   if you have them **Manufacturer**, **Reorder Level** (the level that raises a low-stock alert),\n   **Storage Location** and **Unit Cost Price (AED)**.\n5. Press **Receive Stock**. You go back to the stock list with the new batch on it.\n\nTo enter a drug that is not in the catalogue, leave the search empty and type the drug fields\nyourself, or press **Clear selection** to unlock them after picking one.\n\n**If instead:** the form refuses missing fields: \"Drug code is required\", \"Drug name is required\",\n\"Dosage form is required\", \"Unit is required\", \"Batch number is required\", \"Expiry date is required\",\nand \"Quantity must be positive\".\n\n### A stock batch\n\nThe batch page (`/pharmacy/stock/{id}`) is headed with the drug name and its status: solid for\n**active**, red for anything else.\n\n- **Drug Details**: **Drug Code**, **Dosage Form**, **Strength**, **Generic**, and in amber\n  **Controlled substance** with its class where it applies.\n- **Batch & Inventory**: **Batch #**, **Manufacturer**, **Expiry** (amber with **(Soon)** within 30\n  days), **Received**, **On Hand** (red with a warning sign at or below the reorder level),\n  **Reserved**, **Reorder Level**, **Location** and **Cost/Unit**.\n- **Movement History**: every receipt, dispense, adjustment and other movement, with **Type**,\n  **Qty** (green when stock came in, red when it went out), **Balance After**, **Reason** and\n  **When**.\n\n**Adjust Quantity** corrects the count after a stock-take, damage or write-off:\n\n1. Press **Adjust Quantity**.\n2. Type the **New Quantity On Hand**: the true count, not the difference.\n3. Type a **Reason** (required), for example \"cycle count\".\n4. Press **Save Adjustment**. The adjustment is added to **Movement History**.\n\n**Quarantine Batch** (red, on an active batch only) takes the batch out of use at once, with no\nconfirmation. A quarantined batch is no longer offered when you pick stock on the dispense screen.\n\n## Alerts\n\n**Pharmacy Alerts** (`/pharmacy/alerts`) carries a red count of all alerts next to its title, and two\ntabs:\n\n- **Low Stock** (red count): batches at or below their reorder level, with batch, the amount **On\n  hand** in red and the level it reorders at. \"No low stock alerts\" when there are none.\n- **Expiring Soon** (amber count): batches expiring in the next 30 days, with batch, amount on hand\n  and **Expires** with the date and days left: amber, turning red at seven days or less. \"No batches\n  expiring in the next 30 days\" when there are none.\n\nPress the eye button on any alert to open the batch, where you can adjust or quarantine it. A batch\nonly raises a low-stock alert if a reorder level was set when it was received.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Dispatch to Ward**, offered on a dispensed inpatient record, opens a page that does not exist\n  yet. Ward dispatch cannot be recorded from the screen, so **Confirm Ward Receipt** will not appear\n  either.\n- When the server refuses a step (verify, dispense, receive stock, adjust, quarantine), the screen\n  does not show the reason yet: the button simply comes back. If nothing happens, check the\n  conditions listed under each task.\n- **Qty (max _n_)** on the dispense screen does not stop you typing more than is on hand; the server\n  refuses it on dispensing.\n- There is no screen for partial dispensing, returns, recording a recall, or editing a batch's\n  details after it is received.\n- On the queue, the search only narrows the **All** tab. On **Outpatient** and **Inpatient** the\n  search is ignored, and each tab's count reflects the tab you are on rather than the whole queue.\n",
  "results-reporting.md": "---\ntitle: 'Imaging and procedure results'\ngroup: 'Diagnostics and pharmacy'\norder: 80\nroutes: ['/results/imaging', '/results/procedure']\ndescription: 'Writing, submitting and finalising imaging and procedure reports: the Imaging Results and Procedure Results lists, the report screens, and where results show on a patient.'\n---\n\nShown when your organisation has Results & Reporting switched on.\n\nImaging and procedure reports live under **Results & Reporting** in the sidebar, which has two entries:\n**Imaging Results** and **Procedure Results**. Laboratory results sit beside them under **Lab**, and are\ncovered in [Laboratory](lab.md).\n\nEvery report belongs to a clinical order. An imaging report is written against an imaging order, a\nprocedure report against a procedure order. You cannot start a report from nothing: you pick the order\nfirst, and the report takes the patient and encounter from it.\n\n## Report statuses\n\nImaging and procedure reports share one set of statuses. The same coloured badge appears in the lists,\non the report screen and on the patient's results timeline.\n\n| Status | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Draft** | Grey | Started, still being written. Only you have seen it. |\n| **Preliminary** | Amber | Submitted as a first read. Still editable. |\n| **Final** | Green | Signed off. Read-only. |\n| **Amended** | Blue | A final report that has since been amended. Read-only. |\n| **Corrected** | Orange | A final report that has since been corrected. Read-only. |\n| **Cancelled** | Red | Withdrawn. |\n\nThe report screen also shows a small progress line, **Draft**, **Preliminary**, **Final**: steps already\npassed are filled green, the current step has a green ring and bold label, and steps still to come are\ngrey. A cancelled report shows **Cancelled** in red instead. An amended or corrected report counts as\nhaving reached **Final**.\n\nA report on its second or later version shows **v2**, **v3** and so on beside its badge, with\n**(amended)** or **(corrected)** where that applies.\n\n## Imaging Results\n\n**Imaging Results** (`/results/imaging`) lists every imaging report in your organisation, 20 to a page. The line above the table says how many there are, for example \"Found 12 imaging results\".\n\nEach row shows:\n\n- **Order Name**: the imaging order the report is for.\n- **Patient**.\n- **Modality** and **Body Part**, or a dash when they are not recorded.\n- **Critical**: a red **Critical** badge when the report is flagged as a critical finding, otherwise a\n  dash.\n- **Status**: the report's status badge (see [Report statuses](#report-statuses)).\n- **Reported**: when the report was last submitted as Preliminary or Final, or a dash for a report\n  still in Draft.\n\nPress a row, or its **View** button, to open the report.\n\n### Filtering the list\n\nUse the status filter beside the search box. Choose **All Statuses** (the default), **Draft**,\n**Preliminary**, **Final**, **Amended** or **Cancelled**. The list goes back to page 1 each time you\nchange it. **Previous** and **Next** under the table move between pages when there is more than one;\nthe line beside them says \"Page 2 of 5\" and so on.\n\nThe search box (**Search imaging results...**) is greyed out: it does not search yet.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"No imaging results found\": nothing matches the filter, or no imaging report has been started yet.\n  Reports appear here once they are created from clinical orders.\n- \"Error loading imaging results\": the list could not be fetched. The server's message is shown under\n  it, or \"Please try again or contact support.\"\n\n### Starting an imaging report\n\n1. Press **New Imaging Report** at the top right.\n2. The **Create New Imaging Report** window lists imaging orders that do not have a report yet, newest\n   order first (up to 30). Each shows its **Order Name**, **Patient**, order **Status** and the date it\n   was **Ordered**. Type in **Search orders by name...** to narrow the list by order name.\n3. Press **Create** on the order you are reporting.\n\n**Check:** the window closes and the new report opens in **Draft**, ready to write.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"No reportable orders found\" with \"Try a different search term.\": nothing matches your search.\n- \"No reportable orders found\" with \"All imaging orders already have reports, or no orders of this type\n  exist.\": there is nothing left to report. Cancelled orders are never listed, and nor is any order\n  that has ever had a report, including one whose report was cancelled.\n- If the report cannot be created, the window stays open with no message. Close it and try again.\n\nThe order status badge in the window is green for **completed**, blue for **in progress** and grey for\nanything else. You can report on an order in any status except cancelled.\n\n## Writing an imaging report\n\n**Imaging Report** (`/results/imaging/{orderId}`) opens from a row in the list, from **New Imaging\nReport**, or from **Continue editing** on a patient's results.\n\nThe band at the top shows:\n\n- a back arrow to **Imaging Results**, **Imaging Report** and the report's status;\n- the encounter number on the right, as **Encounter #...**;\n- the patient's **Patient** name, **Age**, **Gender** and **MRN**;\n- **Triage Vitals** from the encounter's triage, when recorded, for example \"Temp 37.2°C · HR 82 bpm · BP\n  120/80\";\n- **Allergies** recorded at triage, in red.\n\nIf the order has no report yet, the screen says \"No imaging report exists for this order yet.\" Press\n**Create Imaging Report** to start one in Draft.\n\n### Writing and saving\n\nWhile the report is **Draft** or **Preliminary** you can write it under **Report Content**. The editor\nstarts empty with a prompt to organise the report under headings such as Technique, Findings and\nImpression.\n\nThe toolbar above the text gives you **Heading 1**, **Heading 2** and **Heading 3**, **Bold** and\n**Italic**, **Bullet List**, **Numbered List**, **Quote**, a **Horizontal Rule**, and **Undo** and\n**Redo** on the right. Hover over an icon to see its name.\n\nThe buttons under the editor:\n\n- **Save Draft** saves what you have written and leaves the status as it is.\n- **Submit as Preliminary** saves and moves a Draft report to **Preliminary**. It only shows while the\n  report is a Draft.\n- **Finalize Report** saves and moves the report to **Final**. You can finalise straight from Draft.\n\nSubmitting as Preliminary or Final records you as the reporter and stamps the time shown in the\n**Reported** column.\n\n**Check:** the status badge and the progress line change, and the list shows the new status and\nreported time.\n\n**If instead:** nothing on screen confirms a failed save. If the badge does not change after\n**Submit as Preliminary** or **Finalize Report**, the save was refused; reload the page before writing\nmore, since text not saved is lost on reload.\n\n### Reading a finalised imaging report\n\nOnce the report is **Final** (or **Amended**) the screen turns read-only and shows:\n\n- a red **CRITICAL FINDING** banner when the report is flagged, with when it was **Notified** and\n  **Acknowledged**, when those are recorded;\n- **Modality**, **Body Part** and **Accession #**, when recorded;\n- the **Report** text as written;\n- for older reports written before the editor existed, separate **Technique**, **Comparison**,\n  **Findings**, **Impression** and **Recommendations** sections;\n- **Comments**, when there are any;\n- when it was **Reported** and **Reviewed**.\n\n## Procedure Results\n\n**Procedure Results** (`/results/procedure`) lists every procedure report in your organisation, 20 to a\npage, with \"Found _n_ procedure results\" above the table.\n\nEach row shows:\n\n- **Order Name** and **Patient**.\n- **Duration**: how long the procedure took, as \"45m\" or \"1h 30m\", or a dash.\n- **Complications**: the first 50 characters in amber, or **None**.\n- **Status** and **Reported**, as on [Imaging Results](#imaging-results).\n\nPress a row, or **View**, to open the report.\n\nThe status filter, paging and the greyed-out search box (**Search procedure results...**) work exactly\nas on [Imaging Results](#filtering-the-list). The empty and error messages read \"No procedure results\nfound\" and \"Error loading procedure results\".\n\n### Starting a procedure report\n\nPress **New Procedure Report** and follow the same steps as [Starting an imaging\nreport](#starting-an-imaging-report). The window, **Create New Procedure Report**, lists procedure\norders that have no report yet. When none are left it says \"All procedure orders already have reports,\nor no orders of this type exist.\"\n\n## Writing a procedure report\n\n**Procedure Report** (`/results/procedure/{orderId}`) has the same patient band as the imaging report,\nwith its back arrow returning to **Procedure Results**. With no report yet it says \"No procedure report\nexists for this order yet.\" and offers **Create Procedure Report**.\n\nWhile the report is **Draft** or **Preliminary**, fill in **Procedure Details** above the editor:\n\n| Field | What to enter |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Anesthesia Type** | **None**, **Local**, **Regional**, **General** or **Conscious Sedation**. |\n| **Estimated Blood Loss** | Free text, for example \"50ml\". |\n| **Start Time** and **End Time** | Date and time the procedure started and ended. |\n| **Duration (min)** | Minutes, as a whole number. It is not worked out from the times: type it in. |\n\nNone of these is required. Then write the narrative under **Report Content**, with sections such as\nIndication, Procedure Description, Findings, Complications and Post-Procedure Instructions. The toolbar\nand the **Save Draft**, **Submit as Preliminary** and **Finalize Report** buttons work as for an\n[imaging report](#writing-and-saving), and the details are saved with the text each time.\n\n### Reading a finalised procedure report\n\nA **Final** or **Amended** procedure report is read-only and shows:\n\n- **Procedure Details**: **Anesthesia**, **Est. Blood Loss**, **Start**, **End** and **Duration**, for\n  whichever were filled in;\n- the **Report** text;\n- for older reports, separate **Indication**, **Procedure Description**, **Findings**,\n  **Complications** and **Post-Procedure Instructions** sections;\n- **Specimens**, each with where it was **Sent to** and its label, when recorded;\n- **Comments**, and when it was **Reported**.\n\n## On the patient\n\nA patient's own results page (`/patients/{patientId}/results`), opened with **Results** on the patient's\ncare context, brings their lab, imaging and procedure reports together. Choose **All**, **Lab**,\n**Imaging** or **Procedure** to narrow it.\n\nThe timeline on the left groups reports by date. Each entry shows the order name, status badge, version\nand a one-line summary: for imaging, **Critical finding** or the start of the impression; for a\nprocedure, its complications. An imaging entry with a critical finding is tinted red.\n\nPick an entry to read it on the right. A Final or Amended report shows in full, as above. A Draft or\nPreliminary report says \"This report is still in progress and hasn't been finalized yet.\" with a\n**Continue editing** button that opens it on the imaging or procedure report screen.\n\nSee [Patient access](patient-access.md) for finding a patient.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Search** on both lists is disabled.\n- **Amending, correcting, verifying or cancelling** a report cannot be done from these screens. Once a\n  report is **Final** it stays read-only here; the **Amended**, **Corrected** and **Cancelled**\n  statuses only appear on reports changed by other means.\n- **Critical findings** cannot be flagged, notified or acknowledged from the imaging report screen. The\n  **Critical** badge and banner only show for reports flagged elsewhere.\n- **Modality**, **Body Part** and **Accession #** are not filled in when you start a report from these\n  screens, so the list shows dashes for them on new reports.\n- **Complications** and **Specimens** on a procedure report have no field on the form. New reports show\n  **None** in the Complications column; write complications in the report text instead.\n- **Comments** are shown when present but cannot be added here.\n",
  "revenue-cycle.md": "---\ntitle: 'Revenue cycle and claims'\ngroup: 'Billing and revenue'\norder: 110\nroutes: ['/revenue-cycle', '/revenue-cycle/claims', '/revenue-cycle/batches', '/revenue-cycle/eligibility', '/revenue-cycle/preauth', '/revenue-cycle/remittance']\ndescription: 'Insurance work: eligibility checks, pre-authorisation requests, claims, claim batches and remittance reconciliation.'\n---\n\nShown when your organisation has Revenue Cycle switched on.\n\nThe **Revenue Cycle** section of the sidebar is the insurance side of billing:\n\n- **Claims** (`/revenue-cycle/claims`): bills sent to an insurer.\n- **Batches** (`/revenue-cycle/batches`): claims grouped for sending together.\n- **Eligibility** (`/revenue-cycle/eligibility`): checking a patient's cover before treatment.\n- **PreAuth** (`/revenue-cycle/preauth`): asking an insurer to approve a service in advance.\n- **Remittance** (`/revenue-cycle/remittance`): the insurer's payment advice, matched back to claims.\n\nOpening `/revenue-cycle` on its own shows a **Revenue Cycle** page with a tile for each of these.\n\nRefunds, credit notes and debit notes have addresses under `/revenue-cycle` too, but they sit in\nthe **Billing** section of the sidebar and are described in [Billing](billing.md).\n\nThe usual order for an insured patient is: check **Eligibility**, raise a **PreAuth** if the\nservice needs one, treat, then raise and **Validate** a **Claim**, gather claims into a **Batch** and\nsubmit, and finally record the **Remittance** and **Reconcile** it.\n\nInsurers are set up under [Payers](billing-setup.md#payers). Several screens here ask for a\n**Payer ID**: this is the payer's internal ID, which you copy from the payer record, not its name.\n\n## Eligibility\n\n**Eligibility requests** (`/revenue-cycle/eligibility`) lists every eligibility check with its\n**Request ID**, **Patient**, **Payer** (the payer's internal ID) and **Status**. Search by request\nID, patient name or MRN.\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Accepted** | The payer answered. Open it to see the benefits returned. |\n| **Rejected** | The payer, or the connector, did not accept the request. |\n| **Error** | The check could not be completed. |\n\n### Checking a patient's cover\n\n**Go to:** **Eligibility**, then **New request** (`/revenue-cycle/eligibility/new`), headed **New\neligibility request**.\n\n1. **Patient**: search by name, MRN or mobile and pick.\n2. **Payer ID** (required).\n3. **Policy ID** and **Encounter ID** if you have them. When a policy is given, its policy number is\n   sent to the payer as the member number.\n4. **Request type**: **Eligibility** or **Benefits**.\n5. **Service types (comma separated)**, for example \"30, 47\", and the **Service date** (today by\n   default).\n6. Press **Submit request**.\n\n**Check:** \"Eligibility request submitted\" and you return to the list, where the new check shows\nits status.\n\nThe check is sent through the eligibility connector named in the payer's **Configuration**\n(`eligibilityConnector`). A payer with no connector set is answered by a built-in test connector,\nso its answers are not real cover decisions.\n\n**If instead:** nothing happens when the patient or **Payer ID** is missing. The server refuses an\nID that is not one of your payers (\"Payer with ID ... not found\"), and the screen does not show\nthat message.\n\n### Reading the answer\n\nOpen a request (`/revenue-cycle/eligibility/{id}`). **Request details** shows the patient, payer,\npolicy, encounter, request type and service date. **Benefits summary** and **Errors** show what the\npayer returned, as raw data, or \"No benefits summary available.\" and \"No errors recorded.\"\n\n## PreAuth\n\n**Pre-auth requests** (`/revenue-cycle/preauth`) lists requests with **PreAuth #**, **Patient**,\n**Payer** and **Status**. Search by pre-auth number, patient name or MRN.\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Draft** | Created, not yet sent. |\n| **Pending** | Waiting to be sent. |\n| **Submitted** | Sent to the payer. |\n| **Approved** / **Partially approved** | The payer agreed, in full or in part. |\n| **Denied** | The payer refused. |\n| **Cancelled** / **Expired** | No longer usable. |\n\n### Raising a pre-authorisation\n\n**Go to:** **PreAuth**, then **New pre-auth** (`/revenue-cycle/preauth/new`), headed **New pre-auth\nrequest**.\n\n1. **Patient**: search and pick.\n2. **Payer ID** (required), then **Policy ID** and **Encounter ID** if known.\n3. **Urgency**: **Routine**, **Urgent** or **Emergency**.\n4. **Clinical notes**: the justification.\n5. **Requested services**: for each service, a **Procedure code** (required), **Description**,\n   **Quantity**, **Estimated cost** and **Diagnosis codes (comma separated)**, such as \"E11.9, I10\".\n   **Add service** adds another; **Remove** deletes one.\n6. Press **Create request**.\n\n**Check:** \"Pre-auth request created\". The request is saved as **Draft**. Services without a\nprocedure code are dropped.\n\n**If instead:** nothing happens when the patient or **Payer ID** is missing or no service has a\nprocedure code.\n\n### Sending it\n\nOpen the request (`/revenue-cycle/preauth/{id}`) and press **Submit**. \"Pre-auth submitted\".\n\n- For a payer connected to the national claims exchange (NHCX, set by `nhcxParticipantCode` in the\n  payer's configuration), the request is sent through the exchange. If the payer answers at once,\n  the decision is applied to the request.\n- For any other payer, the request is only marked **Submitted** here; nothing is sent.\n\nOnly a **Draft** or **Pending** request can be submitted. The page also shows **Request details**\n(including the **Authorization #** once one is granted) and **Requested services** as raw data.\n\n## Claims\n\n**Claims** (`/revenue-cycle/claims`) lists claims for the chosen day.\n\n- **Search** by claim number, patient name or MRN.\n- **Status**: any of the claim statuses below.\n- **Date**: **Today** (the default), **Yesterday** or **Date Range**.\n\nThe columns are **Claim #**, **Patient**, **Payer** (name, with a coloured type badge such as\ninsurance, TPA, government, corporate or self), **Service date**, **Total** and **Status**. Use\n**View** to open a claim.\n\nStatus badges are coloured by where the claim is in its life: cool colours while being prepared,\nblue and purple while with the payer, green when settled, red when refused.\n\n| Status | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Draft** | Slate | Being prepared. |\n| **Pending** | Amber | Generated from charges, waiting for work. |\n| **Ready** | Sky | Ready to check. |\n| **Scrubbing** | Cyan | Being checked. |\n| **Validated** | Teal | Passed checks. |\n| **Failed validation** | Orange | Failed checks; fix and try again. |\n| **Submitted** | Blue | Sent to the payer. |\n| **Acknowledged** | Indigo | The payer has received it. |\n| **Rejected** | Red | The payer rejected it on receipt. |\n| **Pending adjudication** | Purple | The payer is assessing it. |\n| **Adjudicated** | Violet | The payer has decided. |\n| **Paid** | Emerald | Paid in full. |\n| **Partially paid** | Green | Paid in part. |\n| **Denied** | Red | The payer refused payment. |\n| **Appealed** | Fuchsia | The refusal is being appealed. |\n| **Cancelled** | Rose | Withdrawn. |\n\n### Raising a claim\n\n**Go to:** **Claims**, then **New claim** (`/revenue-cycle/claims/new`), headed **Create claim**.\n\n1. **Patient**: search and pick.\n2. **Encounter**: the patient's encounters are listed; narrow them by **Date** or **Doctor name**\n   and click one. Picking an encounter sets the **Service date** to the encounter's date. **Change**\n   picks another.\n3. **Payer ID**: the insurer's internal ID.\n4. **Service date** (required) and **Currency** (AED by default).\n5. Press **Create claim**.\n\n**Check:** \"Claim created\" and you return to the list. The claim is saved as **Draft** with a total\nof zero: this form creates the claim header only, with no lines.\n\n**If instead:** nothing happens when no patient is chosen.\n\n### Validating and submitting a claim\n\nOpen the claim (`/revenue-cycle/claims/{id}`). The header shows **Claim #**, the total and the\nstatus. **Claim overview** shows the patient, **Encounter ID**, **Payer ID** and **Service date**;\n**Claim lines** and **Diagnoses** show the claim's contents as raw data.\n\n1. Press **Validate**. The claim is checked by the claim format set for its payer. \"Claim\n   validated\" if it passes, or \"Validation failed\" if it does not.\n2. Press **Submit**.\n   - For an NHCX-connected payer, the claim is sent through the exchange and marked **Submitted**.\n   - For any other payer, the claim is validated again; if it passes, a claim file is generated\n     and the claim is marked **Submitted** (\"Claim submitted\").\n   - If it fails, the claim is marked **Failed validation** and the screen says \"Submission\n     failed\" with \"Claim failed validation\".\n\n## Batches\n\n**Batches** (`/revenue-cycle/batches`) lists claim batches: **Batch #**, **Status**, **Claims**\n(how many), **Total**, **Format**. Search by batch number.\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Open** | Claims can be added or removed. |\n| **Closed** | Locked; ready to generate a file and submit. |\n| **Submitting** / **Submitted** | Being sent, or sent. |\n| **Acknowledged** | Received by the payer. |\n| **Rejected** | Refused by the payer. |\n| **Partially processed** | Some claims processed. |\n\n### Making and sending a batch\n\n1. **New batch** (`/revenue-cycle/batches/new`), headed **Create batch**: choose a **Batch type**\n   (professional, institutional, dental, pharmacy), a **Claim format** (required) and, optionally, a\n   **Payer ID**. Press **Create batch**. \"Batch created\". Nothing happens without a claim format.\n2. Open the batch (`/revenue-cycle/batches/{id}`). Under **Manage claims in batch**, type claim IDs\n   into **Claim IDs (comma separated)** and press **Add claims** (\"Claims added to batch\") or\n   **Remove claims** (\"Claims removed from batch\"). Claims can be changed only while the batch is\n   **Open**.\n3. Press **Close** (\"Batch closed\"). An empty batch cannot be closed, and only an **Open** batch can\n   be.\n4. Press **Generate file**. \"Batch file generated\" with the file name, or \"Generation failed\" with\n   the reason. The batch must be closed first.\n5. Press **Submit** (\"Batch submitted\"). The batch must be closed first. Submitting marks the batch\n   **Submitted**; it is not yet sent to a clearinghouse.\n\n**Batch details** shows the format, payer, total and type; **Claims** lists the batch's claims as\nraw data.\n\n## Remittance\n\n**Remittances** (`/revenue-cycle/remittance`) lists payment advices from payers: **Remittance ID**,\n**Payer**, **Format**, **Paid** and **Status** (**Received**, **Processing**, **Processed**,\n**Reconciled**, **Error**). Search by remittance ID.\n\n### Recording a remittance\n\n**Go to:** **New remittance** (`/revenue-cycle/remittance/new`).\n\n1. **Payer ID** (required) and **Format**: **ERA_835**, **EOB_PDF**, **MANUAL** or **JSON**.\n2. **Check number**, **Check date** and **Payment amount** (required).\n3. **File content**: paste the remittance file or payload, if you have one.\n4. **Lines (JSON array)**: one entry per claim paid, with its claim number, billed amount and paid\n   amount, as the placeholder shows.\n5. Press **Create remittance**. \"Remittance created\".\n\n**If instead:** \"Invalid lines JSON\" if the lines are not valid JSON. Nothing happens without a\npayer or payment amount.\n\n### Reconciling\n\nOpen the remittance (`/revenue-cycle/remittance/{id}`) and press **Reconcile**. Each line is matched\nto a claim by claim number. A matched claim becomes **Paid** if the line paid anything, or\n**Denied** if it paid nothing. The toast says \"Remittance reconciled\" with how many lines matched\nand how many did not. A line whose claim number is not found is left unmatched. A remittance can\nbe reconciled once.\n\n**Remittance details** shows the payer, format, payment amount and check number; **Lines** shows\nthe lines as raw data.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Payer, claim and encounter fields** on these forms take internal IDs typed in by hand; there is\n  no payer picker. Lists show the payer's ID rather than its name, except on **Claims**.\n- **Claims**: **Create claim** makes an empty draft with no lines; there is no screen to add claim\n  lines. \"Validation failed\" says to review errors below, but the errors are not shown. Claim\n  lines, diagnoses, batch claims, pre-auth services, benefits and remittance lines are shown as raw\n  data, not tables. The claims list does not open a claim when you click the row; use **View**.\n- **Batches**: **Submit** only changes the batch's status.\n- **PreAuth**: for payers not on NHCX, **Submit** only changes the status. There is no screen to\n  record the payer's decision.\n- **Errors**: when the server refuses an action on these screens, the reason is not shown.\n",
  "wellness.md": "---\ntitle: 'Wellness'\ngroup: 'Engagement and wellness'\norder: 130\nroutes: ['/wellness']\ndescription: 'The Wellness section: the dashboard, assessments and their templates, biomarker results, program enrolment, longevity treatments, lifestyle plans and device sync.'\n---\n\nShown when your organisation has Wellness switched on.\n\nThe **Wellness** section of the sidebar holds eight screens: **Wellness Dashboard**, **Assessments**,\n**Assessment Templates**, **Biomarkers**, **Programs**, **Longevity**, **Lifestyle** and **Device Sync**.\nToday the working parts are the assessment list, the template builder, and the \"record\" dialogs on the\nBiomarkers, Programs, Longevity and Lifestyle screens. Most of those screens do not yet list what you have\nrecorded; see [Not live yet](#not-live-yet) before you rely on one.\n\nEvery dialog that needs a patient uses the same patient picker. Type a name, MRN or mobile number into\n**Search by name, MRN, or mobile**. Matches appear as you type; if none do, it says \"No patients found.\".\nPick one and a card shows the patient's name, sex, age, MRN and phone. Press **Change** to pick someone else.\n\n## Wellness Dashboard\n\n**Wellness Dashboard** (`/wellness`) is a launch page headed \"Comprehensive wellness and longevity\nmanagement\". It shows one card per area; press a card to open it:\n\n| Card | Opens | What it says |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Assessments** | `/wellness/assessments` | Create and manage wellness assessments with scoring |\n| **Biomarkers** | `/wellness/biomarkers` | Track biomarkers, trends, and alerts |\n| **Programs** | `/wellness/programs` | Enroll patients in multi-session wellness programs |\n| **Longevity** | `/wellness/longevity` | Longevity protocols and biological age tracking |\n| **Lifestyle** | `/wellness/lifestyle` | Nutrition plans and exercise prescriptions |\n| **Device Sync** | `/wellness/devices` | Connect wearables and sync health data |\n\nThere is no card for **Assessment Templates**; reach it from the sidebar. The dashboard shows no counts or\nfigures.\n\n## Assessments\n\n**Assessments** (`/wellness/assessments`) lists every wellness assessment started for your organisation, in\na table headed **Wellness Assessments**.\n\n- **Patient**: the first eight characters of the patient's record id, not their name.\n- **Template**: the template's name.\n- **Status**: a coloured badge (below).\n- **Score**: the overall score to one decimal place, or **—** if none has been calculated.\n- **Date**: the day the assessment was created.\n\n| Status | Colour | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Draft** | Grey | Started, nothing recorded yet. |\n| **In Progress** | Blue | Being filled in. |\n| **Completed** | Green | Finished and scored. |\n| **Cancelled** | Red | Abandoned. |\n\nWith nothing recorded the table reads \"No assessments yet. Click \"New Assessment\" to create one.\"\n\n### Start an assessment\n\n**Go to:** **Assessments** (`/wellness/assessments`), then **New Assessment**.\n\n1. In **Create New Assessment**, find and pick the patient.\n2. Choose an **Assessment Template \\***. Only active templates are offered, shown as name and code, for\n   example \"Annual Wellness Check (WELLNESS_ANNUAL)\".\n3. Press **Create Assessment**.\n\n**Check:** the dialog closes and the new row appears in the table.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- **Create Assessment** stays greyed out until both a patient and a template are chosen.\n- \"No assessment templates available.\" appears instead of the template list when your organisation has no\n  active template. Use the **Create a template** link, or see [Assessment Templates](#assessment-templates).\n- \"Failed to create assessment. Please try again.\" appears in red if the server refuses. The dialog stays\n  open so you can try again or **Cancel**.\n\nCreating an assessment only starts it. There is no screen yet to answer its questions; see\n[Not live yet](#not-live-yet).\n\n## Assessment Templates\n\n**Assessment Templates** (`/wellness/templates`) shows each template as a card with:\n\n- its name and a badge: **Active** (solid) or **Draft** (grey) when the template is not active;\n- the description, or \"No description provided.\";\n- **Code:** and the category;\n- how many sections it has, for example \"3 Sections\";\n- an **Edit Template** button.\n\nOnly **Active** templates can be chosen when starting an assessment. With no templates the page shows\n\"No templates found\" and a **Create Template** button.\n\n### Build a template\n\n**Go to:** **Assessment Templates**, then **New Template**. This opens **Create New Template**\n(`/wellness/templates/new`).\n\n1. Under **Template Details**, fill in:\n   - **Template Name \\***, for example \"Annual Wellness Check\".\n   - **Unique Code \\***, for example \"WELLNESS_ANNUAL\". Letters, numbers, hyphens and underscores only.\n   - **Category \\***: **General** (the default), **Cardiology**, **Mental Health**, **Nutrition** or\n     **Physical Therapy**.\n   - **Description**, optional.\n2. Each section is a card headed **Section 1**, **Section 2** and so on. The first is named \"General\n   Information\" and holds one empty question. Click the name to rename it. The bin icon on the card removes\n   the whole section.\n3. For each question:\n   - type the wording into **Question text...**;\n   - choose its type: **Text Answer**, **Numeric**, **Yes/No**, **Single Select**, **Multi Select** or\n     **Scale (1-N)**;\n   - for **Single Select** and **Multi Select**, list the choices separated by commas, e.g. \"Yes, No, Maybe\";\n   - for **Scale (1-N)**, give the **Min** and **Max**;\n   - leave **Req** on if an answer is mandatory (new questions start on);\n   - the bin icon beside the question removes it.\n4. **Add Question** adds another question to that section; **Add Section** adds a new section at the\n   bottom, named \"New Section 2\" and so on, with no questions.\n5. Press **Save Template**.\n\n**Check:** a \"Template Created\" message says \"The assessment template has been successfully created.\", and\nyou return to **Assessment Templates**.\n\n**If instead:** the form will not save while a field is wrong, and shows the reason under it:\n\n- \"Template name is required\"\n- \"Template code is required\"\n- \"Code must be alphanumeric\" (the code has a space or a character other than a letter, number, `-` or `_`)\n- \"Category is required\"\n- \"Section name is required\"\n- \"Question text is required\"\n\nIf the server refuses, an \"Error\" message says \"Failed to create template. Please check your input.\" A\ncommon cause is a code another template already uses.\n\n**Cancel** or **Back to Templates** leaves without saving.\n\n## Biomarkers\n\n**Biomarkers** (`/wellness/biomarkers`) records a single biomarker test result for a patient. The\n**Biomarker Tracking** panel below is a placeholder; results you save are not listed here.\n\n### Record a biomarker result\n\n**Go to:** **Biomarkers**, then **Add Biomarker Result**.\n\n1. Pick the **Patient**.\n2. Choose the **Biomarker**. The list shows each active biomarker with its unit in brackets. Choosing\n   one fills in **Unit**, which you cannot edit.\n3. Enter the **Value**. Decimals are allowed.\n4. **Date Recorded** starts at today. Press it to pick another day; future dates cannot be chosen.\n5. Add **Notes (Optional)** if useful.\n6. Press **Save Result**.\n\n**Check:** \"Biomarker result recorded\" with \"The result has been successfully added.\", and the dialog\ncloses. The result is saved as a manual entry.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"Patient is required\", \"Biomarker is required\", \"Value must be a number\" or \"Date is required\" appear\n  under the field that needs it.\n- \"Failed to record result\" with \"An error occurred while saving the result.\" means the server refused. The\n  dialog stays open.\n\n## Programs\n\n**Programs** (`/wellness/programs`) enrols a patient in a multi-session wellness program. The **Wellness\nPrograms** panel below is a placeholder; enrolments are not listed here.\n\n### Enrol a patient\n\n**Go to:** **Programs**, then **New Program Enrollment**.\n\n1. Pick the **Patient**.\n2. Choose the **Program**. Each active program shows its length in weeks in brackets. If there\n   are none, the list says \"No active programs found\".\n3. **Start Date** starts at today; press it to change.\n4. Add **Notes (Optional)**.\n5. Press **Enroll Patient**.\n\n**Check:** \"Patient enrolled successfully\" with \"The patient has been enrolled in the wellness program.\" The\nserver creates the program's schedule of sessions and milestones.\n\n**If instead:** \"Patient is required\", \"Program is required\" or \"Start date is required\" under the field;\nor \"Enrollment failed\" with \"An error occurred while enrolling the patient.\" if the server refuses.\n\nThere is no screen to create a program itself; programs must already exist for your organisation.\n\n## Longevity\n\n**Longevity** (`/wellness/longevity`) schedules a longevity treatment for a patient against one of your\norganisation's protocols. The **Longevity Treatments** panel below is a placeholder.\n\n### Schedule a treatment\n\n**Go to:** **Longevity**, then **New Treatment Protocol**.\n\n1. Pick the **Patient**.\n2. Choose the **Protocol**. Each active protocol shows its administration route in brackets, in capitals. If\n   there are none, the list says \"No active protocols found\".\n3. **Scheduled At** starts at now. Press it to pick another day. You can pick a day, not a time: the time\n   shown is the time you opened the dialog.\n4. Add **Pre-treatment Notes (Optional)**.\n5. Press **Schedule Treatment**.\n\n**Check:** \"Treatment scheduled successfully\" with \"The longevity treatment protocol has been scheduled.\"\n\n**If instead:** \"Patient is required\", \"Protocol is required\" or \"Scheduled date is required\" under the\nfield; or \"Scheduling failed\" with \"An error occurred while scheduling the treatment.\" if the server\nrefuses.\n\n## Lifestyle\n\n**Lifestyle** (`/wellness/lifestyle`) creates nutrition plans and exercise prescriptions. Its two panels,\n**Nutrition Plans** and **Exercise Prescriptions**, are placeholders.\n\nPress **New Plan** to open **New Lifestyle Plan**. It has two tabs, **Nutrition Plan** and **Exercise\nPrescription**, each saved separately. **Pick the patient on the Nutrition Plan tab, even for an exercise\nprescription**: the Exercise tab has no patient picker and shows \"No patient selected. Please select in\nNutrition tab.\" until you do.\n\n### Create a nutrition plan\n\n1. On **Nutrition Plan**, pick the **Patient**.\n2. Enter a **Plan Name**, e.g. \"Keto Diet\".\n3. Choose the **Plan Type**: **Weight Loss**, **Maintenance** (the default), **Muscle Gain** or\n   **Therapeutic**.\n4. Optionally enter **Target Calories**.\n5. **Start Date** starts at today.\n6. Add **Notes** if useful, then press **Create Nutrition Plan**.\n\n**Check:** \"Nutrition plan created successfully\", and the dialog closes.\n\n**If instead:** \"Patient is required\" or \"Plan name is required\" appear under the field; \"Failed to create\nnutrition plan\" means the server refused.\n\n### Create an exercise prescription\n\n1. Pick the patient on the **Nutrition Plan** tab first, then switch to **Exercise Prescription**. Check the\n   name after **Selected:**.\n2. Enter a **Prescription Name**, e.g. \"Cardio Routine\".\n3. Choose the **Goal**: **Weight Loss**, **Cardiovascular** (the default), **Strength** or **Flexibility**.\n4. Set **Sessions per Week** (default 3) and **Minutes per Session** (default 30); each must be at least 1.\n5. **Start Date** starts at today. Add **Notes** if useful.\n6. Press **Create Prescription**.\n\n**Check:** \"Exercise prescription created successfully\", and the dialog closes.\n\n**If instead:**\n\n- \"Prescription name is required\" appears under the field.\n- If no patient was picked, **Create Prescription** does nothing and shows no message. Go back to the\n  Nutrition Plan tab and pick one.\n- \"Failed to create exercise prescription\" means the server refused.\n\nThe prescription is saved without a list of individual exercises; there is nowhere to add them yet.\n\n## Device Sync\n\n**Device Sync** (`/wellness/devices`) is meant to connect wearables (Apple Health, Fitbit, Oura Ring, and\nDexcom or Libre glucose monitors) and show their data. It is a placeholder today; see below.\n\n## Not live yet\n\n- **Device Sync** (`/wellness/devices`): the **Connect Device** button does nothing, and **Connected\n  Devices** shows only a description of what is planned.\n- **Biomarkers**, **Programs**, **Longevity** and **Lifestyle**: the panels under the buttons are\n  placeholder text (\"... will be displayed here.\"). What you save through the dialogs is stored, but these\n  screens do not list it, show trends or alerts, or let you open, edit or cancel a record.\n- **Assessments**: rows look clickable but do not open. There is no screen to answer an assessment's\n  questions, submit it, or see its score breakdown, so new assessments stay as you created them.\n  **Patient** shows a shortened record id rather than the patient's name.\n- **Assessment Templates**: **Edit Template** opens a page that does not exist yet, so templates cannot be\n  changed after saving, and there is no way to switch a template between **Active** and **Draft** from\n  here. The template builder has no field for a maximum score or question weights.\n- **Longevity**: every treatment is booked against one fixed default facility, not the facility you are\n  working in, and **Scheduled At** cannot take a time.\n- **Lifestyle**: nutrition plans take calories only; protein, carbohydrate and fat targets are not on the\n  form.\n- **Programs** and **Longevity** need programs and protocols already set up for your organisation; there is\n  no screen here to create them.\n- The **Create a template** link in **Create New Assessment** always opens the English version of the app.\n\nFor membership plans, see [Membership](membership.md). For outreach to patients, see\n[Patient engagement](patient-engagement.md). Terms are explained in the [Glossary](glossary.md).\n",
};
